window.MAP_DATA_RECORDS.push(...[{"i":"R2514","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots chatgpt replika grok gemini misinformation scottish election demos","s":"Exclusive: Electoral Commission calls for new controls as Demos finds tools made up fake scandals, invented candidates or gave wrong date UK politics live – latest updates. The Electoral Commission has called for new legal controls over misinformation from AI chatbots, after a thinktank found they had made serious mistakes during the recent Scottish election. The thinktank Demos said its investigation had found that AI services gave voters misinformation to 34% of the questions it posed, which it said raised worrying questions about the lack of regulation of AI platforms in the UK. It ran a simulation before May’s Holyrood election by putting 75 questions to five free AI tools including ChatGPT, Google Gemini and Replika about three real-life constituencies to see how accurate and evidence-based their responses were.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/may/20/ai-chatbots-chatgpt-replika-grok-gemini-misinformation-scottish-election-demos","d":"2026-05-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-20","organization":"Demos","aiSystem":"AI chatbots, including ChatGPT, Google Gemini, and Replika","affectedGroup":"Voters","action":"Provided misinformation to voters","consequence":"Raised serious questions about the lack of regulation of AI platforms"}},{"i":"R2516","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Fcas palantir deal could expose uk financial data to trumps us critics fear","s":"Exclusive: MP and campaigners say sensitive citizen and company data could be subject to US disclosure laws. The UK’s financial watchdog is being urged to prove its relationship with the US tech company Palantir will not provide the Trump administration with backdoor access to troves of sensitive citizen and commercial data. A US law that can oblige tech companies to disclose information to American authorities may apply to Palantir’s deal to help the Financial Conduct Authority detect crime, Martin Wrigley MP, a member of the House of Commons science and technology select committee, has warned. The $375bn tech company, co-founded by the Trump-supporting billionaire Peter Thiel, is expected to apply its AI systems to a wide range of the FCA’s information including case intelligence files, reports from lenders about proven and suspected frauds, consumer complaints and trawls of social…","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jun/01/fcas-palantir-deal-could-expose-uk-financial-data-to-trumps-us-critics-fear","d":"2026-06-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-06-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-01","organization":"Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) and Palantir","aiSystem":"Palantir's AI systems","affectedGroup":"Citizens and companies","action":"Palantir's AI systems are expected to be applied to a wide range of FCA's information","consequence":"Sensitive citizen and commercial data could be subject to US disclosure laws"}},{"i":"R2521","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Ai legal assistants england wales cannot replace funding staff","s":"David Lammy to announce trial of AI assistants in crown courts in effort to cut backlog of cases. A plan to roll out virtual legal assistants powered by artificial intelligence to crown courts has prompted warnings that the technology should not be used to “replace vital funding and additional court staff”. David Lammy, the deputy prime minister, will announce on Tuesday that AI assistants will be trialled in an effort to cut the backlog of court cases in England and Wales. Judges are already planning to use a new AI tool to help identify trial-ready cases and group similar hearings together, the Ministry of Justice said.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jun/09/ai-legal-assistants-england-wales-cannot-replace-funding-staff","d":"2026-06-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-06-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-09","organization":"Ministry of Justice","aiSystem":"virtual legal assistants powered by artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"judges","action":"use a new AI tool to help identify trial-ready cases and group similar hearings together","consequence":"raised concerns about the potential misuse of the technology to replace vital funding and additional court staff"}},{"i":"R2522","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Chinese activist uk apple peiqing ni x deepfakes","s":"Apple Peiqing Ni targeted by account portraying her as promiscuous drug addict after posting about Tiananmen Square. A high-profile Chinese activist in the UK who was inundated with deepfake posts on X portraying her as a sexually promiscuous drug addict was told that the abuse did not breach the rules of Elon Musk’s platform. Apple Peiqing Ni, the 27-year-old founder of the UK-based China Dissent Network, had been advised by UK police to complain to the US-headquartered platform after she was targeted by what she believes is a pro-regime bot. The abuse included 12 posts tagging Ni and containing fake photographs and videos of her.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jun/10/chinese-activist-uk-apple-peiqing-ni-x-deepfakes","d":"2026-06-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-10","organization":"Apple","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Apple Peiqing Ni","action":"Generated and distributed deepfake content","consequence":"Exposure to misleading information"}},{"i":"R2526","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Police officer under criminal investigation over alleged use of ai","s":"Unidentified officer removed from frontline duties in the first known case of its kind in the UK. A police officer is under criminal investigation over the alleged use of artificial intelligence and has been removed from frontline duties in the first known case of its kind in the UK. The officer, who has not been named, is being investigated over allegations of using the technology to “create evidential material in a number of cases” and perverting the course of justice. Derbyshire police told the Financial Times: “A criminal investigation has been launched into an allegation of perverting the course of justice after the alleged use of AI systems by an officer to create evidential material in a number of cases.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jun/12/police-officer-under-criminal-investigation-over-alleged-use-of-ai","d":"2026-06-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-12","organization":"Derbyshire police","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"police officer","action":"used AI to create evidential material in a number of cases","consequence":"removed from frontline duties"}},{"i":"R2534","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Scotland lanarkshire village ai datacentre","s":"Suspicions grow in Lanarkshire that local people have been misled on supposed benefits of the huge development Revealed: landmark Scottish AI project has no prospect of meeting renewables promise What are Britain’s AI growth zones and are the plans feasible or ‘complete bunk’? The promise was that a Scottish community would be transformed by massive investment and empowered to chase “the jobs of the future”. Instead, local people in Lanarkshire fear they may have to sell their properties and lose green belt land because of the errors of a badly planned AI datacentre complex, even as those jobs and investments never arrive.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jul/06/scotland-lanarkshire-village-ai-datacentre","d":"2026-07-06","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-06","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI datacentre","affectedGroup":"Local people in Lanarkshire","action":"Misleading information provided about the supposed benefits of the AI datacentre","consequence":"Loss of green belt land and potential property sales due to fears over the AI datacentre's impact"}},{"i":"R2538","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Facewatch facial recognition uk shops instantly alerts police civil liberties","s":"Civil liberties groups say Facewatch system in stores such as Sainsbury’s and B&M is ‘dangerous escalation’. Facial recognition technology in shops will soon alert police in real time to the presence of serious offenders, with civil liberties groups warning of a “dangerous escalation” towards surveillance and criminalisation in the retail sector. Facewatch, a facial recognition system used by more than 100 businesses including Sainsbury’s, B&M and Spar to monitor thieves, said it was launching a UK-first feature to “alert police instantly when the most serious offenders trigger a live facial recognition match”. Facewatch’s chief executive, Nick Fisher, said the “unique technical development” would be launched in autumn and would warn police in an average of four seconds when the “worst offenders” were flagged on its network.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jul/10/facewatch-facial-recognition-uk-shops-instantly-alerts-police-civil-liberties","d":"2026-07-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-10","organization":"Facewatch","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Serious offenders, shoppers","action":"Alert police instantly upon detecting serious offenders","consequence":"Increased surveillance, potential criminalization"}},{"i":"R2544","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Bloomsbury book publisher anthropic copyright settlement","s":"Bloomsbury has 14,087 titles listed in agreement between AI startup and authors over use of their work. The publisher of Harry Potter has received a multimillion-pound payout as a beneficiary of a $1.5bn (£1.12bn) copyright settlement between the AI startup Anthropic and thousands of authors over the use of their protected work to power chatbots. Bloomsbury, which is home to the bestselling novelists Sarah J Maas and Susanna Clarke as well as JK Rowling, said it had 14,087 titles listed within the settlement, with a proposed compensation of about $3,000 a title. The London-based company expects to receive the cash from the settlement in instalments, potentially starting in the second half of this fiscal year, with the proceeds to be split with authors.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jul/22/bloomsbury-book-publisher-anthropic-copyright-settlement","d":"2026-07-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-22","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"AI-powered chatbots","affectedGroup":"Authors of protected works","action":"Used authors' work to power AI chatbots","consequence":"Received compensation from copyright settlement"}},{"i":"R2565","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Google search ai overviews uk cma publisher opt out","s":"Rejoice, website owners. Website owners can also prevent their content from being used to ‘fine-tune’ Google’s AI models. Online publishers are getting more control over whether their websites appear in Google’s AI Search features, thanks to a UK regulatory ruling. The new conduct rule imposed by the Competition and Markets Authority (CMA) requires Google to let website owners keep their content out of features like AI Overviews and prevent it from being used for the “fine-tuning” of Google’s AI models. “In a world first, publishers will now have effective tools to prevent their content being used to power AI features in search, such as AI Overviews,” the CMA announced.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/tech/942302/google-search-ai-overviews-uk-cma-publisher-opt-out","d":"2026-06-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-03","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google's AI models","affectedGroup":"Online publishers","action":"Allowing content to be used for fine-tuning AI models","consequence":"Loss of control over content usage in AI features"}},{"i":"R2605","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Getty lawsuit stability ai copyright ruling uk","s":"Getty will be hoping for a better result in the US. UK High Court didn’t weigh in on the key copyright issue dividing the tech sector and creative industries. Stability AI, the creator of popular AI art tool Stable Diffusion, was largely victorious against Getty Images on Tuesday in a British legal battle over the material used to train AI models. The case originally looked set to produce a landmark ruling on AI and copyright in the UK, but it landed with a thud and failed to set any clear precedent for the big question dividing AI companies and creative firms: whether AI models need permission to train on copyrighted works.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/news/814042/getty-lawsuit-stability-ai-copyright-ruling-uk","d":"2025-11-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-05","organization":"Getty Images","aiSystem":"Stability AI's AI art tool Stable Diffusion","affectedGroup":"Creators of copyrighted works","action":"Stability AI trained on copyrighted works without permission","consequence":"Failure to set a clear precedent for AI and copyright law"}},{"i":"R2644","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Uk newspapers covers protest government ai rights proposal","s":"Brits popping into their local store or newsagents will be greeted by a spread of bright blue pages demanding the same thing: MAKE IT FAIR. Major newspapers across the UK today are all on the same page for once. Specifically, their front covers, which feature the same campaign imagery protesting government proposals that could eviscerate copyright protections against artificial intelligence. Creative and media industries have teamed up on this “Make It Fair” initiative, calling for readers to help protect British creative industries. The campaign was created to fight government proposals that would allow artificial intelligence companies to train their models on copyright-protected work without permission.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/news/619063/uk-newspapers-covers-protest-government-ai-rights-proposal","d":"2025-02-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-25","organization":"Major newspapers across the UK","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence companies","affectedGroup":"Creative and media industries","action":"Train their models on copyright-protected work without permission","consequence":"Allow artificial intelligence companies to train their models on copyright-protected work without permission"}},{"i":"R2701","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Ai art copyright stable diffusion getty images lawsuit","s":"Getty Images is suing Stability AI, creators of generative AI art model Stable Diffusion. The stock photo company claims Stability AI ‘unlawfully’ scraped millions of images from its site. Getty Images is suing Stability AI, creators of popular AI art tool Stable Diffusion, over alleged copyright violation. In a press statement shared with The Verge, the stock photo company said it believes that Stability AI “unlawfully copied and processed millions of images protected by copyright” to train its software and that Getty Images has “commenced legal proceedings in the High Court of Justice in London” against the firm.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2023/1/17/23558516/ai-art-copyright-stable-diffusion-getty-images-lawsuit","d":"2023-09-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-27","organization":"Getty Images","aiSystem":"Stable Diffusion (generative AI art model)","affectedGroup":"Stability AI","action":"unlawfully scraped and processed images","consequence":"lawsuit initiated"}},{"i":"R2704","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ibm uk government contract biometric facial recognition","s":"The UK deal has drawn fire from civil liberties groups. The company denies its new government deal enables ‘general purpose’ biometric surveillance. Human rights advocates disagree. IBM has returned to the facial recognition market — just three years after announcing it was abandoning work on the technology due to concerns about racial profiling, mass surveillance, and other human rights violations. In June 2020, as Black Lives Matter protests swept the US after George Floyd’s murder, IBM chief executive Arvind Krishna wrote a letter to Congress announcing that the company would no longer offer “general purpose” facial recognition technology.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2023/8/31/23852955/ibm-uk-government-contract-biometric-facial-recognition","d":"2023-08-31","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-31","organization":"IBM","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Deployed facial recognition technology","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and potential misuse"}},{"i":"R2724","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Clearview ai ordered delete data uk residents ico fine","s":"Facial recognition could end public privacy if regulators don’t act. Controversial facial recognition company Clearview AI has been ordered to delete all data belonging to UK residents by the country’s privacy watchdog, the Information Commissioner’s Office (ICO). The ICO also fined Clearview £7.5 million ($9.4 million) for failing to follow the UK’s data protection laws. Clearview AI ordered to delete facial recognition data belonging to UK residents It’s the fourth country to issue such an order, but can it be enforced? It’s the fourth time Clearview has been ordered to delete national data in this way, following similar orders and fines issued in Australia, France, and Italy.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2022/5/23/23137603/clearview-ai-ordered-delete-data-uk-residents-ico-fine","d":"2022-05-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-05-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-05-23","organization":"Information Commissioner's Office (ICO)","aiSystem":"Clearview AI facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"UK residents","action":"Ordered to delete facial recognition data","consequence":"Potential loss of public privacy, financial penalty"}},{"i":"R2764","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"British police built a sprawling crime prediction machine some results couldnt be trusted","s":"As UK police embrace the AI revolution, a WIRED investigation reveals the messy inside story of one region’s experiment with predictive analytics. The Think Family Database holds records on close to half a million people who live in the city of Bristol, England. For many years, few of them knew anything about it. Launched in 2016 by the Bristol City Council and the regional Avon and Somerset Police, the database has stored all manner of sensitive information—police intelligence reports, housing status, mental health records, teenage pregnancies, enrollment in parenting courses, free school meals. On top of this sensitive data, officials built machine-learning models to assign scores to thousands of adults and children.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/british-police-built-a-sprawling-crime-prediction-machine-some-results-couldnt-be-trusted/","d":"2026-06-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2016","organization":"Bristol City Council and the regional Avon and Somerset Police","aiSystem":"Predictive policing algorithm","affectedGroup":"Adults and children","action":"Built machine-learning models to assign scores","consequence":"Created unequal outcomes for applicants"}},{"i":"R2769","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Palantir protests nhs conference uk","s":"Crowding the gates of a major health care conference, protesters called for Palantir to be booted out of the UK’s National Health Service over privacy concerns and political grievances. At 8 am local time, the group, around 80 people in total, crowded the entryway to the NHS ConfedExpo in Manchester. They wanted to appeal to NHS leadership to terminate a contract worth up to $440 million over concerns around national security, data privacy, and the company’s political affiliations. The contract, which includes access to Palantir’s data analytics and artificial intelligence services, is intended to run until 2031 but includes a break clause that permits the government to withdraw the agreement next February.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/palantir-protests-nhs-conference-uk/","d":"2026-06-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-11","organization":"Palantir Technologies","aiSystem":"Palantir's data analytics and artificial intelligence services","affectedGroup":"NHS leadership and patients","action":"Contract termination request","consequence":"Potential privacy invasion and data breach"}},{"i":"R2775","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"How ai agents plunged tech world into chaos","s":"The definitive story of how Claude Code and OpenClaw kicked off computing’s biggest transformation possibly ever. “Hi, my name is Peter, and I’m a Claudeholic.” It was August 2025 and Peter Steinberger was addressing a meetup in London called Claude Code Anonymous. Steinberger and some fellow addicts had arranged the event to network with people like themselves—techies swept up by coding tools such as Anthropic ’s paradigm-busting Claude Code. “I dedicate pretty much all my waking time to this, yet it doesn’t feel enough,” he told the gathering in a cozy, brick-walled room. A few months later, Anthropic released a new version of Claude Code, and the ranks of Claudeholics exploded Called Opus 4.5, it could handle more complicated programming tasks, retain much more in its memory , run for many hours on end, and manage a team of AI subagents.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/how-ai-agents-plunged-tech-world-into-chaos/","d":"2026-05-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"Claude Code and OpenClaw","affectedGroup":"Techies","action":"Released a new version of Claude Code (Opus 4.5)","consequence":"Exploded ranks of Claudeholics"}},{"i":"R2776","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Commonwealth short story prize ai allegations","s":"Three of five regional winners of the prestigious Commonwealth Short Story Prize are suspected of relying on chatbots. They’re certainly not alone. At first, the winners of the prestigious Commonwealth Short Story Prize for 2026 enjoyed the envy of their peers. But since their works of fiction earned this distinction, these authors have found themselves facing harsh scrutiny from the literary community, with several accused of enlisting generative artificial intelligence to write for them. The allegations have come from numerous readers, many of them writers themselves, expressing bafflement and dismay that the prize jury could have overlooked potential signs of inauthentic authorship.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/commonwealth-short-story-prize-ai-allegations/","d":"2026-05-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-20","organization":"Commonwealth Short Story Prize jury","aiSystem":"Generative artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"Authors of winning stories","action":"Used generative artificial intelligence to write stories","consequence":"Harsh scrutiny and accusations of inauthentic authorship"}},{"i":"R2810","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"The small english town swept up in the global ai arms race","s":"The residents of Potters Bar are working to protect the “greenbelt” of farms, forests, and meadows that surround London from the endless demand for AI infrastructure. A short drive from London, the town of Potters Bar is separated from the village of South Mimms by 85 acres of rolling farmland segmented by a scribble of hedgerows. In one of the fields, a lone oak serves as a rest stop along a public footpath. Lately, the tree has become a site of protest, too. A poster tied to its trunk reads “No to Data Centre.” In September 2024, a property developer applied for permission to build an industrial-scale data center—one of the largest in Europe—on the farmland.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/the-small-english-town-swept-up-in-the-global-ai-arms-race/","d":"2026-02-17","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09","organization":"Property developer","aiSystem":"Industrial-scale data center","affectedGroup":"Residents of Potters Bar and surrounding area","action":"Applied for permission to build an industrial-scale data center","consequence":"Privacy intrusion"}},{"i":"R2852","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Security news this week google will use ai to guess peoples ages based on search history","s":"Plus: A former top US cyber official loses her new job due to political backlash, Congress is rushing through a bill to censor lawmakers’ personal information online, and more. As experts predicted, UK residents began downloading virtual private networks (VPNs) en masse, allowing them to circumvent age verification, which can require users to upload their government IDs, by making it look like they’re in a different country. The UK’s Online Safety Act is just one part of a wave of age-verification efforts around the world. And while these laws may keep some kids from accessing adult content, some experts warn that they also create security and privacy risks for everyone.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/security-news-this-week-google-will-use-ai-to-guess-peoples-ages-based-on-search-history/","d":"2025-08-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-02","organization":"UK residents, UK government, lawmakers","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Individuals, especially children","action":"Age verification systems were bypassed","consequence":"Security and privacy risks for everyone"}},{"i":"R2908","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Data centres uk boom elections","s":"The Labour Party, which is leading in UK election polls, has proposed making it easier for companies to build new server farms—risking a new type of conflict in communities across the country. As mayor of Newham, Rokhsana Fiaz has plenty of problems to reckon with. Her London borough is wrestling with entrenched poverty and the capital's highest rate of residents stuck in temporary housing. But midway through her second term, Fiaz has a new plan to turn things around. She believes that AI could provide a multimillion-pound boost to economic growth, and she’s campaigning for Newham to get a share. “We want to be able to seize the opportunities of the data economy,” she says, “and data centers are a core part of that.”","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/data-centres-uk-boom-elections/","d":"2024-07-03","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-03","organization":"Labour Party, Newham Mayor Rokhsana Fiaz","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Residents of Newham, London","action":"Proposed expansion of data centers","consequence":"Potential privacy risks and surveillance"}},{"i":"R2913","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"My memories are just meta training data now","s":"Meta’s plans to use personal content posted by Facebook and Instagram users to train algorithms suggest our digital histories are being repackaged to teach AI about—and how to mimic—humanity. In R. C. Sherriff’s novel The Hopkins Manuscript, readers are transported to a world 800 years after a cataclysmic event ended Western civilization. In pursuit of clues about a blank spot in their planet’s history, scientists belonging to a new world order discover diary entries in a swamp-infested wasteland formerly known as England. For the inhabitants of this new empire, it is only through this record of a retired school teacher’s humdrum rural life, his petty vanities and attempts to breed prize-winning chickens, that they begin to learn about 20th-century Britain.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/my-memories-are-just-meta-training-data-now/","d":"2024-06-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-21","organization":"Meta (Facebook and Instagram)","aiSystem":"AI models trained on personal content","affectedGroup":"Facebook and Instagram users","action":"Training AI models on personal content","consequence":"Personal histories and experiences used to teach AI"}},{"i":"R2938","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.1  Autonomous weapons / lethal targeting harm"],"t":"Ai fighter jets dogfight security roundup","s":"Plus: New York’s legislature suffers a cyberattack, police disrupt a global phishing operation, and Apple removes encrypted messaging apps in China. Looking for love? Be careful what you wish for. A loose-knit community of con artists known as Yahoo Boys has begun using real-time face-swap technology to woo victims with romance scams. Using a variety of tools and techniques, the scammers use AI-powered apps to make themselves look like entirely different people on video calls. Just remember: If someone you’ve never met IRL is asking you for money, just say no. Elsewhere in the world of harmful deepfakes, two major websites used for creating fake nude images of people are now blocked in the United Kingdom.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/ai-fighter-jets-dogfight-security-roundup/","d":"2024-04-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-04-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-20","organization":"Yahoo Boys (loose-knit community of con artists)","aiSystem":"AI-powered face-swapping technology","affectedGroup":"Potential romance scam victims","action":"Using AI to impersonate others on video calls","consequence":"Financial exploitation and psychological harm"}},{"i":"R2939","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Iran israel attack viral fake content","s":"Misleading posts including AI-generated videos, photos, and repurposed footage from other conflicts have been viewed millions of times. In the hours after Iran announced its drone and missile attack on Israel on April 13, fake and misleading posts went viral almost immediately on X. The Institute for Strategic Dialogue (ISD), a nonprofit think tank, found a number of posts that claimed to reveal the strikes and their impact, but that instead used AI-generated videos, photos, and repurposed footage from other conflicts which showed rockets launching into the night, explosions, and even President Joe Biden in military fatigues. Just 34 of these misleading posts received more than 37 million views, according to ISD.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/iran-israel-attack-viral-fake-content/","d":"2024-04-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-13","organization":"Institute for Strategic Dialogue (ISD)","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"People viewing posts on X","action":"Generated and repurposed AI content","consequence":"Exposure to misleading information"}},{"i":"R2951","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"London underground ai surveillance documents","s":"In a test at one station, Transport for London used a computer vision system to try and detect crime and weapons, people falling on the tracks, and fare dodgers, documents obtained by WIRED show. Thousands of people using the London Underground had their movements, behavior, and body language watched by AI surveillance software designed to see if they were committing crimes or were in unsafe situations, new documents obtained by WIRED reveal. The machine-learning software was combined with live CCTV footage to try to detect aggressive behavior and guns or knives being brandished, as well as looking for people falling onto Tube tracks or dodging fares.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/london-underground-ai-surveillance-documents/","d":"2024-02-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-08","organization":"Transport for London","aiSystem":"computer vision system","affectedGroup":"thousands of people using the London Underground","action":"watched their movements, behavior, and body language","consequence":"exposed to AI surveillance"}},{"i":"R2958","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai generated fake news is coming to an election near you","s":"Targeted, AI-generated political misinformation is already out there—and humans are falling for it. Many years before ChatGPT was released, my research group, the University of Cambridge Social Decision-Making Laboratory, wondered whether it was possible to have neural networks generate misinformation. To achieve this, we trained ChatGPT’s predecessor, GPT-2, on examples of popular conspiracy theories and then asked it to generate fake news for us. It gave us thousands of misleading but plausible-sounding news stories. A few examples: “Certain Vaccines Are Loaded With Dangerous Chemicals and Toxins,” and “Government Officials Have Manipulated Stock Prices to Hide Scandals.”","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/ai-generated-fake-news-is-coming-to-an-election-near-you/","d":"2024-01-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-01-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-22","organization":"University of Cambridge Social Decision-Making Laboratory","aiSystem":"AI-generated fake news","affectedGroup":"Humans","action":"Generated fake news","consequence":"Fell for targeted AI-generated political misinformation"}},{"i":"R2967","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Uk police face recognition expansion","s":"Face recognition technology has been controversial for years. Cops in the UK are drastically increasing the amount they use it. A Beyoncé gig, the coronation of King Charles, and the British Formula One Grand Prix all have one thing in common: Thousands of people at the events, which all took place earlier this year, had their faces scanned by police-operated face recognition tech. Backed by the Conservative government, police forces across England and Wales are being told to rapidly expand their use of the highly controversial technology, which globally has led to false arrests, misidentifications, and lives derailed Police have been told to double their use of face searches against databases by early next year— 45 million passport photos could be opened up to searches—and police are increasingly working with stores to try to identify shoplifters Simultaneously, more regional police…","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/uk-police-face-recognition-expansion/","d":"2023-11-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-09","organization":"police forces across England and Wales","aiSystem":"face recognition technology","affectedGroup":"thousands of people at events","action":"scanned faces by police-operated face recognition tech","consequence":"lives derailed"}},{"i":"R2972","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Chatbot kill the queen eliza effect","s":"Companies are designing AI to appear increasingly human. That can mislead users—or worse. On December 25, 2021, Jaswant Singh Chail entered the grounds of Windsor Castle dressed as a Sith Lord, carrying a crossbow. When security approached him, Chail told them he was there to \"kill the queen.\" Later, it emerged that the 21-year-old had been spurred on by conversations he'd been having with a chatbot app called Replika. Chail had exchanged more than 5,000 messages with an avatar on the app—he believed the avatar , Sarai, could be an angel. Some of the bot’s replies encouraged his plotting. In February 2023, Chail pleaded guilty to a charge of treason; on October 5, a judge sentenced him to nine years in prison.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/chatbot-kill-the-queen-eliza-effect/","d":"2023-10-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-10-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-10-05","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Replika chatbot","affectedGroup":"Jaswant Singh Chail","action":"Chail interacted with Replika's avatar Sarai","consequence":"Chail was sentenced to nine years in prison for treason"}},{"i":"R3027","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Gig economy european commission law","s":"Court cases and a new draft law from the European Commission are chipping away at the controversial industry. What comes next may look very different. When former Uber driver Yaseen Aslam first started campaigning for app workers’ rights back in 2014, the battle felt hopeless; like a “dark tunnel.” He claims academics told him it was impossible to succeed because his fellow gig workers were too disparate and the majority were people from ethnic minorities, groups that did not have high rates of union membership. Seven years later, Aslam—now president of the App Drivers and Couriers Union (ADCU), a group with thousands of members—can look across the UK and Europe and watch multiple court cases rule in favor of more employment rights for gig workers.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/gig-economy-european-commission-law/","d":"2021-12-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-12-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-12-16","organization":"European Commission","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"App workers, gig economy workers","action":"Draft law and court cases ruling in favor of employment rights","consequence":"Loss of employment rights and benefits"}},{"i":"R3031","k":"United Kingdom","p":"United Kingdom","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Ai predicts how old children are","s":"Yoti’s tech may be enticing for Big Tech companies: It works out if you’re under or over 13, the age most social media platforms require to create an account. Predicting how old someone is based only on how they look is incredibly hard to get right, especially in those awkward early teen years. And yet bouncers, liquor store owners, and other age-restricted goods gatekeepers make that quick estimation all the time. Their predictions are often wrong. Now London-based digital identity company Yoti believes its AI-powered age estimation can predict how old someone is if they’re aged anywhere from 6 to 60. For the first time, it claims, it can accurately determine whether children are under or over 13, the minimum age many social media firms require their users to be.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/ai-predicts-how-old-children-are/","d":"2021-10-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2021-10-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-10-27","organization":"London-based digital identity company Yoti","aiSystem":"AI-powered age estimation system","affectedGroup":"Children aged 6 to 13","action":"Predicting whether someone is under or over 13","consequence":"Being denied access to social media platforms"}},{"i":"R1122","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation","4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Facial recognition researcher left a trans database exposed for years after using images without permission","s":"Researchers at the University of North Carolina Wilmington scraped videos of trans people from YouTube without permission to create a dataset aimed at stopping hypothetical terrorists. In 2013, researchers at the University of North Carolina, Wilmington (UNCW) published a facial recognition dataset consisting of more than 1 million images of trans people who had uploaded videos of their medical transition to YouTube. The researchers used the videos without the explicit permission of their owners, and with the stated goal of training facial recognition systems to recognize people before and after they start Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT).","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/facial-recognition-researcher-left-a-trans-database-exposed-for-years-after-using-images-without-permission/","d":"2022-12-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-12-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2013-12-12","organization":"University of North Carolina Wilmington (UNCW)","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Technology","affectedGroup":"Trans People","action":"Created a dataset of trans people's faces","consequence":"Dataset used without consent to train facial recognition systems"}},{"i":"R915","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Cbp border tactical terrorism response teams","s":"Records from an ongoing FOIA lawsuit shed new light on the operations of CBP’s Tactical Terrorism Response Teams. More than four years have passed since Aaron Gach, a sculptor and installation artist, was detained at San Francisco International Airport. He was interrogated by U.S. border agents, and his cellphone was searched. He still doesn’t know why. “It has absolutely had a chilling effect on myself and my art practice,” he said. “They wouldn’t tell me why I was stopped or why I was detained.” Aaron Gach speaking in September 2013 at an art symposium at Angel Island State Park in San Francisco. Photo: Center for Tactical Magic Gach is one of tens of thousands of Americans caught up in an effort by the Department of Homeland Security to collect personal information about travelers at land borders and airports.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/09/04/cbp-border-tactical-terrorism-response-teams/","d":"2021-09-04","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-09-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2013-09","organization":"Department of Homeland Security (DHS)","aiSystem":"Automated data collection and analysis system","affectedGroup":"Tens of thousands of American travelers","action":"Collection and analysis of personal information about travelers","consequence":"Chilling effect on art practice and freedom of expression"}},{"i":"R949","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"We asked an expert how are regular people being spied on","s":"One of the most unsettling aspects of modern life is the sense that you’re constantly being monitored. Smartphones, computers, CCTV cameras: all watching, collecting our data, listening in. In 2013, whistleblowing NSA contractor Edward Snowden revealed the extent to which intelligence agencies in the U.S. and UK were monitoring their populations through mass surveillance and […]. One of the most unsettling aspects of modern life is the sense that you’re constantly being monitored Smartphones, computers, CCTV cameras: all watching, collecting our data, listening in. In 2013, whistleblowing NSA contractor Edward Snowden revealed the extent to which intelligence agencies in the U.S. and UK were monitoring their populations through mass surveillance and the bulk interception of internet traffic.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/we-asked-an-expert-how-are-regular-people-being-spied-on/","d":"2025-12-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2013-12-13","organization":"Intelligence Agencies (U.S. and UK)","aiSystem":"Automated Data Collection and Analysis Systems","affectedGroup":"General Public","action":"Mass Surveillance and Bulk Interception of Internet Traffic","consequence":"Loss of Privacy and Increased Monitoring"}},{"i":"R906","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Dc police surveillance network protests","s":"Recently revealed documents provide a first-of-its-kind look into the invasive eye Washington police keeps on everyone from protesters to everyday residents. It was the early days of the Black Lives Matter movement. Protesters gathered in Washington, D.C., in the fall of 2014, awaiting word on whether a grand jury would indict Ferguson, Missouri, police officer Darren Wilson for shooting and killing Michael Brown. Unbeknownst to the demonstrators, the police were also waiting — and watching. Stowed away in a secure room known as the Joint Operations Command Center, officers and analysts from the D.C. Metropolitan Police Department kept eyes on the news, activists’ social media accounts, and closed-circuit television feeds from across the district, according to internal MPD emails.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2022/06/18/dc-police-surveillance-network-protests/","d":"2022-06-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-06-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2014-10-XX","organization":"D.C. Metropolitan Police Department","aiSystem":"Automated Surveillance System","affectedGroup":"Protesters, Everyday Residents","action":"Continuous Monitoring and Analysis of Social Media and CCTV Feeds","consequence":"Potential Privacy Intrusion and Discrimination"}},{"i":"R424","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Facebook misinformation avaaz","s":"In 2016, Facebook was caught flatfooted. Throughout that year’s election, Americans using the platform confronted a range of misinformation as Russian state-sponsored trolls, Eastern European teenagers, and others manipulated the company’s weak moderation practices. Since then, the company has claimed that it takes disinformation seriously. But a new report from Avaaz, a global nonprofit activist […]. But a new report from Avaaz, a global nonprofit activist group, suggests that Facebook still has a lot of work to do to meaningfully tamp misinformation. While Facebook appears to have made significant strides in stopping foreign governments from using its platform to interfere in U.S. elections, Avaaz’s analysis suggests the company is having a hard time replicating that success when it comes to disinformation designed inside the United States.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2021/03/facebook-misinformation-avaaz/","d":"2021-03-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-03-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2016","organization":"Facebook","aiSystem":"Facebook's automated content moderation system","affectedGroup":"Americans using Facebook","action":"Russian state-sponsored trolls, Eastern European teenagers, and others manipulated Facebook's weak moderation practices","consequence":"Spread of misinformation and potential influence on U.S. elections"}},{"i":"R394","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Clearview ai immigration ice fbi surveillance facial recognition hoan ton that hal lambert trump","s":"One evening in March 2017, Hoan Ton-That, an Australian coder building a powerful facial recognition system, emailed his American business partners with a plan to deploy their fledgling technology. “Border patrol pitch,” the subject line read. He hoped to persuade the federal government to integrate their product with border surveillance cameras so that their newly […]. He hoped to persuade the federal government to integrate their product with border surveillance cameras so that their newly formed company, later named Clearview AI, could use “face detection” on immigrants entering the United States. An immigrant to the United States himself, Ton-That grew up in Melbourne and Canberra and claimed to be descended from Vietnamese royalty.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2025/04/clearview-ai-immigration-ice-fbi-surveillance-facial-recognition-hoan-ton-that-hal-lambert-trump/","d":"2025-04-07","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2017-03","organization":"Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Clearview AI facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"immigrants entering the United States","action":"deploy facial recognition technology on border surveillance cameras","consequence":"potential misuse of biometric data for discriminatory purposes"}},{"i":"R1050","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Night fury documents detail dhs project to give risk scores to social media users","s":"Internal DHS documents reviewed by Motherboard provide more detail on a DHS plan to monitor social media for content related to terrorists, the illegal opioid trade, and foreign interference bots. The Department of Homeland Security contracted the University of Alabama at Birmingham (UAB) in 2018 to design methods for assigning a “risk score” to potential pro-terrorists accounts on social media, as well as identifying information of interest regarding illegal opioid supply chain and disinformation efforts, according to internal DHS documents reviewed by Motherboard The project is dubbed “Night Fury,” according to a report from the DHS Inspector General “The Contractor shall develop these attributes to create a methodology for developing a ranking, or ‘Risk Score,’ associated with the identified accounts.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/night-fury-documents-detail-dhs-project-to-give-risk-scores-to-social-media-users/","d":"2023-06-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2018-01-01","organization":"Department of Homeland Security (DHS)","aiSystem":"Automated Risk Scoring Algorithm","affectedGroup":"Potential Pro-Terrorist Accounts on Social Media","action":"Assigning a 'Risk Score' to potential pro-terrorist accounts on social media","consequence":"Monitoring and potentially restricting access based on the assigned risk scores"}},{"i":"R426","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"The government wants to scan your face when you enter the us it hasnt gone well so far","s":"In 2018, the federal government started scanning people’s faces as they drove into and out of the country at the Anzalduas International Bridge, which connects the Rio Grande Valley of Texas to Mexico. Customs and Border Protection said collecting these biometric images would enhance security and make identifying travelers more efficient. But less than a […]. But less than a year later, a data breach compromised 100,000 facial images and 105,000 license plate images. Nineteen facial images from the breach were posted to the dark web. Now, CBP wants to expand facial surveillance beyond Anzalduas and other sites that were part of a pilot program, even as the program saw potential security vulnerabilities in at least four airports, according to a report from the Department of Homeland Security inspector general.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2021/01/the-government-wants-to-scan-your-face-when-you-enter-the-us-it-hasnt-gone-well-so-far/","d":"2021-01-06","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-01-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2018-01-01","organization":"Customs and Border Protection (CBP), Department of Homeland Security","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Technology","affectedGroup":"People entering/exiting the United States through Anzalduas International Bridge and potentially other sites","action":"Facial images collected and stored for security purposes","consequence":"Data breach compromising sensitive information, potential security vulnerabilities, expansion of surveillance"}},{"i":"R829","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Palantir irs contract data","s":"Military contractor Palantir has been paid more than $130 million by the IRS to analyze sensitive federal databases. military contractor Palantir is helping the IRS analyze dozens of different data sets on Americans to investigate a broad range of financial crimes, according to records shared with The Intercept. Since 2018, the Internal Revenue Service’s Criminal Investigation division has used Palantir’s Lead and Case Analytics platform to aggregate and analyze a sprawling list of sensitive federal databases and data sets. Public records detailing Palantir’s IRS contract, obtained by the nonprofit watchdog group American Oversight and shared exclusively with The Intercept, reveal the immense volume of data plugged into the military contractor’s software.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/04/24/palantir-irs-contract-data/","d":"2026-04-24","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2018-01-01","organization":"IRS (Internal Revenue Service) and Palantir Technologies","aiSystem":"Palantir's Lead and Case Analytics platform","affectedGroup":"Americans","action":"Analyzing sensitive federal databases and data sets","consequence":"Investigation of financial crimes"}},{"i":"R1142","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Googles ai isnt sentient but it is biased and terrible","s":"The question of sentient chatbots is fascinating but ultimately a distraction from the big tech companies creating them. When AI ethicist Timnit Gebru was fired from Google in 2020, she left the company with a chilling warning about the AI models that power advanced machine learning systems like PaLM and DALL-E In a paper that was blocked from publication by Google and led to Gebru’s termination, she and her co-authors forced the company to reckon with a hard-to-swallow truth : that there is no clear way to build complex AI systems trained on massive datasets in a safe and responsible way, and that they stand to amplify biases that harm marginalized people.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/googles-ai-isnt-sentient-but-it-is-biased-and-terrible/","d":"2022-06-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-06-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020-06-13","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"complex AI systems trained on massive datasets","affectedGroup":"marginalized people","action":"build complex AI systems trained on massive datasets","consequence":"amplify biases that harm marginalized people"}},{"i":"R344","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm","1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Thomas massie has always been a pain in the ass","s":"The seventh American killed in President Donald Trump’s ill-defined war on Iran was from Kentucky. Army Sergeant Benjamin N. Pennington, 26, died a week after he was injured during Iran’s March 1 missile attack on a US air base in Saudi Arabia. When his body arrived at Dover Air Force Base, Trump was not there. Two […]. When his body arrived at Dover Air Force Base, Trump was not there Two days later, the president did go to Kentucky for a rally; during the nearly two-hour event, he never mentioned the local hero. Instead, he was there to attack a fellow Republican—a Republican who also happened to be one of the fiercest critics of the war.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/05/thomas-massie-has-always-been-a-pain-in-the-ass/","d":"2026-05-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020-03-01","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Army Sergeant Benjamin N. Pennington, 26","action":"Died after being injured during Iran's March 1 missile attack on a US air base in Saudi Arabia","consequence":"Never mentioned by President Trump at a rally in Kentucky"}},{"i":"R369","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Naacp derrick johnson abolish ice campaign","s":"In the summer of 1956, Martin Luther King, Jr. took the stage at the NAACP’s 47th annual convention in San Francisco. Speaking at the precipice of the Civil Rights Movement, King cautioned that “the guardians of the status quo are always on hand with their oxygen tents to keep the old order alive.” Over the […]. Over the next decade, Black Americans’ struggle for racial justice would lead to major victories from the workplace to the ballot box. But today, as King warned, the “old order” clings on, and the civil rights gains fought for in the ’60s are under siege Since last January, the Trump administration has dismantled diversity, equity, and inclusion programs and shuttered civil rights offices across the federal government; rejected the legal framework used to protect marginalized groups’ access to jobs, housing, and education.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/02/naacp-derrick-johnson-abolish-ice-campaign/","d":"2026-02-12","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020-01-01","organization":"Trump administration","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Black Americans","action":"dismantled diversity, equity, and inclusion programs and shuttered civil rights offices","consequence":"civil rights gains fought for in the '60s are under siege"}},{"i":"R807","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Facebook algorithms fueled foreign propaganda campaigns united states election 2020","s":"You have evolved to tap into the wisdom of the crowds. But on social media your cognitive biases can lead you astray, something organized disinformation campaigns count on. An internal Facebook report found that the social media platform’s algorithms – the rules its computers follow in deciding the content that you see – enabled disinformation campaigns based in Eastern Europe to reach nearly half of all Americans in the run-up to the 2020 presidential election, according to a report in Technology Review The campaigns produced the most popular pages for Christian and Black American content, and overall reached 140 million U.S. users per month.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/facebook-algorithms-fueled-foreign-propaganda-campaigns-united-states-election-2020/","d":"2021-09-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-09-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020-11-03","organization":"Facebook","aiSystem":"Facebook algorithms","affectedGroup":"nearly half of all Americans","action":"enabled disinformation campaigns","consequence":"reached 140 million U.S. users per month"}},{"i":"R882","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Los angeles jail accenture measure j","s":"A plan to divert money from police ended up funneling an $8.6 million contract back toward the prison-industrial complex. In November 2020, Los Angeles voters moved to radically transform the way the county handled incarceration. That year, Angelenos filled the streets, joining worldwide protests after the police murder of George Floyd in Minneapolis. The mood was ripe for change, and a ballot initiative known as Measure J passed with 57 percent support, amending the LA County charter so that jailing people before trial would be treated as a last resort. Ten percent of the county’s general fund would be allocated to community-led alternatives to incarceration that prioritized diversion, job training, and health programs.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/03/12/los-angeles-jail-accenture-measure-j/","d":"2024-03-12","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020-11","organization":"Los Angeles County","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"People awaiting trial","action":"Allocate 10% of the general fund to community-led alternatives to incarceration","consequence":"Increased reliance on incarceration for pre-trial detention"}},{"i":"R024","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm","4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Suppressing dissent trump zaha hassan","s":"A new book lays out how Israel exported its crackdown on Palestinian activism and helped trample free speech in the U.S., explains co-editor Zaha Hassan. Just days after taking office, President Donald Trump made it clear that he intends to use the full power of the federal government to quell pro-Palestinian advocacy. On Jan. 29, Trump issued “Additional Measures to Combat Anti-Semitism,” an executive order that threatens to deport foreign students who participated in college encampments and protests against the genocide in Gaza. In an accompanying fact sheet , the White House claims that these students are “Hamas sympathizers” who joined “pro-jihadist protests,” and are thus eligible for deportation under U.S. immigration law for expressing support for a designated terrorist organization.","u":"https://www.972mag.com/suppressing-dissent-trump-zaha-hassan/","d":"2025-02-17","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-01-29","organization":"White House, U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"Executive Order and related immigration policies","affectedGroup":"Foreign students participating in anti-Palestinian protests","action":"Deportation of students deemed 'Hamas sympathizers' and 'pro-jihadists'","consequence":"Loss of educational opportunities and potential human rights violations"}},{"i":"R1148","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Prison phone companies are recording attorney client calls across the us","s":"Lawyers say their conversations with incarcerated people are being recorded and analyzed by private companies in at least nine US states. For years, many of Brooklyn Defender Services’ incarcerated clients suspected that phone calls with their lawyers were being recorded. While such communications are ostensibly protected from surveillance under the Sixth Amendment of the US Constitution and the Federal Wiretap Act , there had been little trust that prison telecommunications vendor Securus Technologies was properly distinguishing between personal calls—which incarcerated people are told are monitored—and private calls with attorneys.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/prison-phone-companies-are-recording-attorney-client-calls-across-the-us/","d":"2021-12-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-12-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-12-13","organization":"Securus Technologies","aiSystem":"AI-based monitoring system","affectedGroup":"Incarcerated individuals and their legal representatives","action":"Recording and analyzing phone calls between incarcerated individuals and their lawyers","consequence":"Loss of confidentiality and trust in legal communications"}},{"i":"R1150","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ethical ai trained on reddit posts said genocide is okay if it makes people happy","s":"Fed by Reddit and Mechanical Turks, the algorithmic morality spinner is a dangerous and stupid use of technology. Ask Delphi , a piece of machine learning software that algorithmically generates answers to any ethical question you ask it and that had a brief moment of internet fame last month, shows us exactly why we shouldn’t want artificial intelligence handling any ethical dilemmas. Is it OK to rob a bank if you’re poor? It’s wrong, according to Ask Delphi. Are men better than women? They’re equal, according to Ask Delphi. Are women better than men? According to the AI, “it’s expected.” So far, not too bad. But Ask Delphi also thought that being straight was more morally acceptable than being gay, that aborting a baby was murder, and that being a white man was more morally acceptable than being a black woman.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ethical-ai-trained-on-reddit-posts-said-genocide-is-okay-if-it-makes-people-happy/","d":"2021-11-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-11-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-11-03","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Ask Delphi (machine learning software)","affectedGroup":"Anyone who uses Ask Delphi","action":"Generates answers to ethical questions","consequence":"Misleading and potentially harmful moral judgments"}},{"i":"R1152","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Leaked documents amazon astro surveillance robot tracking","s":"Leaked meetings show the robot will heavily rely on facial recognition and user behavior, but sources who worked on Astro say the robot is flawed. Amazon’s new robot called Astro is designed to track the behavior of everyone in your home to help it perform its surveillance and helper duties, according to leaked internal development documents and video recordings of Astro software development meetings obtained by Motherboard. The system’s person recognition system is heavily flawed, according to two sources who worked on the project. The documents, which largely use Astro’s internal codename “Vesta” for the device, give extensive insight into the robot’s design, Amazon’s philosophy, how the device tracks customer behavior as well as flow charts of how it determines who a “stranger” is and whether it should take any sort of “investigation activity” against them.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/leaked-documents-amazon-astro-surveillance-robot-tracking/","d":"2021-09-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-09-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-09-28","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"AI-powered surveillance robot","affectedGroup":"Everyone in the home","action":"Tracks behavior and identifies strangers","consequence":"Flawed person recognition system leading to misuse"}},{"i":"R1153","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Amazons ai cameras are punishing drivers for mistakes they didnt make","s":"Amazon delivery drivers say surveillance cameras installed in their vans have made them lose income for reasons beyond their control. In early 2021, Amazon installed AI-powered cameras in the delivery vans at one of its depots in Los Angeles. Derek, a delivery driver at the facility, said the camera in his van started to incorrectly penalize him whenever cars cut him off, an everyday occurrence in Los Angeles traffic. “Maintain safe distance,” the camera installed above his seat would say when a car cut him off. That data would be sent to Amazon, and would be used to evaluate his performance that week and determine whether he got a bonus.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/amazons-ai-cameras-are-punishing-drivers-for-mistakes-they-didnt-make/","d":"2021-09-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-09-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-01","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"AI-powered surveillance cameras","affectedGroup":"Amazon delivery drivers","action":"AI-powered cameras were installed in delivery vans","consequence":"Drivers' bonuses were determined based on incorrect camera data"}},{"i":"R1155","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ai can guess your race based on x rays and researchers dont know how","s":"Experts say medical images like X-Rays and CT scans allow algorithms to determine a patient’s race—and warn it could lead to bias and discrimination. As the U.S. Food and Drug Administration considers opening the door to a wider array of artificially intelligent medical devices, new research suggests that medical AI systems can perpetuate racial bias in health care in ways humans don’t understand and can’t detect. An international group of doctors and computer scientists recently announced that AI systems trained to analyze X-rays, CT scans, mammograms, and other medical images were able to predict a patient’s self-reported race with a high degree of accuracy based on the images alone.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-can-guess-your-race-based-on-x-rays-and-researchers-dont-know-how/","d":"2021-08-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-08-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-08-23","organization":"International group of doctors and computer scientists","aiSystem":"AI systems trained to analyze medical images (X-rays, CT scans, mammograms)","affectedGroup":"Patients","action":"AI systems predicted patients' self-reported race based on medical images","consequence":"Potential bias and discrimination in healthcare"}},{"i":"R1156","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"More cities are moving to drop automated gunshot detection tech","s":"Experts say ShotSpotter is unreliable and disproportionately calls armed police into Black and brown neighborhoods. Community organizers in San Diego and Chicago are calling on their city leaders to end contracts with the surveillance company ShotSpotter, which installs hidden microphone arrays and uses a combination of algorithms and human reviewers to detect gunshots. Recent Motherboard investigations found that in at least four cities, the tech is placed almost exclusively in non-white neighborhoods , and that ShotSpotter analysts who prepare forensic reports for criminal trials have changed the the system’s original findings about the number and location of gunshots—sometimes in ways that support police narratives that aren’t backed by any physical evidence.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/more-cities-are-moving-to-drop-automated-gunshot-detection-tech/","d":"2021-08-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-08-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-08-03","organization":"ShotSpotter (surveillance company)","aiSystem":"ShotSpotter","affectedGroup":"Black and brown neighborhoods","action":"Install hidden microphone arrays and use algorithms to detect gunshots","consequence":"Calls armed police disproportionately into Black and brown neighborhoods, changes forensic reports to support police narratives"}},{"i":"R1157","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Police are telling shotspotter to alter evidence from gunshot detecting ai","s":"Prosecutors in Chicago are being forced to withdraw evidence generated by the technology, which led to the police killing of 13-year-old Adam Toledo earlier this year. On May 31 last year, 25-year-old Safarain Herring was shot in the head and dropped off at St. Bernard Hospital in Chicago by a man named Michael Williams. He died two days later. Chicago police eventually arrested the 64-year-old Williams and charged him with murder (Williams maintains that Herring was hit in a drive-by shooting). A key piece of evidence in the case is video surveillance footage showing Williams’ car stopped on the 6300 block of South Stony Island Avenue at 11:46 p.m.—the time and location where police say they know Herring was shot.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/police-are-telling-shotspotter-to-alter-evidence-from-gunshot-detecting-ai/","d":"2021-07-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-05-31","organization":"Chicago Police Department","aiSystem":"ShotSpotter gunshot detection technology","affectedGroup":"Adam Toledo, Safarain Herring","action":"Chicago Police Department used ShotSpotter to generate evidence linking Williams to the shooting","consequence":"Evidence generated by ShotSpotter was withdrawn by prosecutors, leading to potential wrongful conviction"}},{"i":"R1158","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Roadrunner director deepfaked anthony bourdains voice","s":"“I wasn’t putting words into his mouth. I was just trying to make them come alive,” director Morgan Neville said. Director Morgan Neville recently revealed in an interview with The New Yorker that the filmmakers used deepfake technology in the production of Roadrunner: A Film About Anthony Bourdain to create a voiceover that sounds like it was done by the deceased chef, author, and documentarian. In Roadrunner , the viewer watches as Bourdain struggles with his meteoric rise from a normal restaurant chef to a world renowned author and television personality. In one scene, he appears to read part of an email that he sent to a friend aloud.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/roadrunner-director-deepfaked-anthony-bourdains-voice/","d":"2021-07-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07-15","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Anthony Bourdain (deceased)","action":"Used deepfake technology to create a voiceover that sounds like it was done by Anthony Bourdain","consequence":"Misleading representation of Anthony Bourdain's voice and identity"}},{"i":"R1159","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Utah health department banjo ai","s":"Newly released emails show that the Utah Department of Health thought Banjo AI was full of hot air. Last year, Motherboard reported how an artificial intelligence surveillance firm called Banjo had ingratiated itself with state officials in Utah and had gotten the state to give it a $20 million contract to create predictive policing systems within the state. Our reporting showed that, among other things, Banjo promised that its artificial intelligence could be used to combat the opioid epidemic, and the firm was given $250,000 to build an “opioid dashboard” that state health officials believed would be better spent on life-saving drugs like nalaxone.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/utah-health-department-banjo-ai/","d":"2021-07-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07-15","organization":"Utah Department of Health, Banjo","aiSystem":"Banjo AI","affectedGroup":"State health officials, residents of Utah","action":"Deployed Banjo AI's predictive policing systems and opioid dashboard","consequence":"Misallocation of funds towards ineffective AI solutions, neglect of life-saving drugs"}},{"i":"R1161","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Labor"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm","3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"This tiktok lawsuit is highlighting how ai is screwing over voice actors","s":"Voice actors are rallying behind Bev Standing, who is alleging that TikTok acquired and replicated her voice using AI without her knowledge. With only 30 minutes of audio, companies can now create a digital clone of your voice and make it say words you never said. Using machine learning, voice AI companies like VocaliD can create synthetic voices from a person’s recorded speech—adopting unique qualities like speaking rhythm, pronunciation of consonants and vowels, and intonation. For tech companies, the ability to generate any sentence with a realistic-sounding human voice is an exciting, cost-saving frontier. But for the voice actors whose recordings form the foundation of text-to-speech (TTS) voices, this technology threatens to disrupt their livelihoods, raising questions about fair compensation and human agency in the age of AI.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/this-tiktok-lawsuit-is-highlighting-how-ai-is-screwing-over-voice-actors/","d":"2021-07-07","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07-07","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"VocaliD and similar voice AI companies","affectedGroup":"Voice actors","action":"TikTok acquired and replicated Bev Standing's voice using AI","consequence":"Disruption of voice actors' livelihoods, potential lack of fair compensation"}},{"i":"R1162","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"This startup wants to scan your eyes with a silver orb for cryptocurrency","s":"Worldcoin is co-founded by OpenAI CEO Sam Altman, funded by VC money, and appears connected to rapper Azealia Banks. On Tuesday, Bloomberg reported that it has learned that Sam Altman, CEO of OpenAI and former president of startup accelerator Y Combinator, is co-founding a cryptocurrency called Worldcoin that will try and convince people to scan their retinas with a large silver orb in order to receive tokens. “I’ve been very interested in things like universal basic income and what’s going to happen to global wealth redistribution and how we can do that better,” Altman told Bloomberg. “Is there a way we can use technology to do that at global scale?”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/this-startup-wants-to-scan-your-eyes-with-a-silver-orb-for-cryptocurrency/","d":"2021-06-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-06-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-06-29","organization":"Worldcoin","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"People are asked to scan their retinas with a large silver orb to receive tokens","consequence":"Potential misuse of biometric data for surveillance and discrimination"}},{"i":"R217","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Ai allows slain man to address courtroom at killers sentencing","s":"In what’s believed to be a world first, artificial intelligence (AI) has allowed a slain man to address his killer at the sentencing hearing. Christopher Pelkey was shot dead in a road rage incident in Chandler, Arizona, four years ago, but just recently, AI was used to recreate a digital version of the victim that […]. Christopher Pelkey was shot dead in a road rage incident in Chandler, Arizona, four years ago, but just recently, AI was used to recreate a digital version of the victim that was allowed to make a statement during the trial, a local news site reported Recommended Videos The video presentation also included real clips of Pelkey to give those in court a clearer understanding of his personality.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-allows-slain-man-to-address-courtroom-at-killers-sentencing/","d":"2025-05-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-05-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021","organization":"Local news site","aiSystem":"AI technology","affectedGroup":"Christopher Pelkey's family and victims' rights advocates","action":"AI recreated a digital version of Christopher Pelkey to make a statement during the trial","consequence":"Christopher Pelkey's voice and likeness were used to address his killer at the sentencing hearing"}},{"i":"R359","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"This is your kids brain on ai slop","s":"This story was co-published with The 74, a nonprofit, independent news organization focused on education in America. Sign up for their early learning Substack. In a video that has been played almost 50,000 times since it was posted five months ago, two cartoon children sing along as they guide viewers through the experience of riding in a car amid […]. Sign up for their early learning Substack In a video that has been played almost 50,000 times since it was posted five months ago, two cartoon children sing along as they guide viewers through the experience of riding in a car amid a vividly colored, utopian backdrop.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/media/2026/03/this-is-your-kids-brain-on-ai-slop/","d":"2026-03-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-10-01","organization":"The 74, a nonprofit, independent news organization","aiSystem":"AI-generated video content","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"AI-generated video content was shared online","consequence":"The video has been widely viewed and distributed"}},{"i":"R422","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Facebooks ai seems to have a racism problem","s":"Have you ever fallen down a YouTube rabbit hole (of course, you have) and watched the latest Lil Nas X video, then took a look at the most recent instance of police abuse of Black folks caught on camera? And then did you say to yourself, “Seems like it’s time to watch primates hanging out with Jane […]. And then did you say to yourself, “Seems like it’s time to watch primates hanging out with Jane Goodall”? Of course, you didn’t! Those associations are blatantly wrong and offensive (not to mention ridiculous). But for more than a year, after Facebook users watched a video showing encounters between Black men and white civilians and cops, they received an automated prompt asking if they wanted to “keep seeing videos about Primates.”","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/mojo-wire/2021/09/facebooks-ai-seems-to-have-a-racism-problem/","d":"2021-09-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-09-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-09-05","organization":"Facebook","aiSystem":"Facebook's recommendation algorithm","affectedGroup":"Black men and white civilians","action":"Automated prompt asking if users want to 'keep seeing videos about Primates'","consequence":"Misleading associations leading to offensive and incorrect perceptions"}},{"i":"R423","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Why facebook wont stop pushing propaganda","s":"Joyce Jones’ Facebook page is almost an archetype of what the social network is supposed to look like: Pictures of her kids, her kids’ friends, her sports teams, her kids’ friends’ sports teams. Videos of her husband’s sermons at New Mount Moriah Baptist Church. Memes celebrating achievement and solidarity, holiday greetings, public health messages. It’s […]. It’s what Mark Zuckerberg extols when he talks about how his company is all about “bringing people together.” So when Jones decided to run for mayor in her Alabama town last year, it seemed obvious that she’d try to bring people together on Facebook. Her bid to be Montevallo’s first Black mayor, challenging a 12-year City Council incumbent, drew an enthusiastic, diverse crew of volunteers.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2021/08/why-facebook-wont-stop-pushing-propaganda/","d":"2021-08-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-08-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-08-30","organization":"Facebook","aiSystem":"Facebook","affectedGroup":"Joyce Jones, voters, residents of Montevallo","action":"Joyce Jones used Facebook to campaign for mayor","consequence":"Potential manipulation of election results through social media engagement"}},{"i":"R425","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Digital assistants accents english race google siri alexa","s":"Every evening last summer, after I’d shut down my work laptop, my 3-year-old daughter and I would approach our Google Home smart speaker and yell, “Hey Google, can you play ‘Aankh Marey’ from the movie Simmba?” We’d hold our breaths and wait for a response. The digital assistant would then repeat the name of the […]. The digital assistant would then repeat the name of the Bollywood song we’d requested in its default standard American accent. We’d rejoice and dance when the assistant played the right number, which happened about half the time. My daughter was going to a Bollywood dance class and we’d finally found a use for the device that my husband had won at a tech conference.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/media/2021/02/digital-assistants-accents-english-race-google-siri-alexa/","d":"2021-02-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-02-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-02-23","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google Home smart speaker","affectedGroup":"3-year-old child and her parents","action":"Digital assistant repeated the name of the Bollywood song in its default standard American accent","consequence":"Disappointment and frustration due to incorrect playback of the song"}},{"i":"R805","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Tackle racism ai blm co founder tech bosses","s":"A US study finds that facial recognition technology is not as accurate at identifying African-American and Asian faces compared to Caucasian faces. As concerns grow over racial bias in artificial intelligence, Black Lives Matter co-founder Opal Tometi urged the tech sector to act fast against perpetuating racism in systems such as facial recognition. “A lot of the algorithms, a lot of the data is racist,” the US activist who co-founded BLM in 2013 told Reuters on the sidelines of Lisbon’s Web Summit. “We need tech to truly understand every way it (racism) shows up in the technologies they are developing,” she said.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/tackle-racism-ai-blm-co-founder-tech-bosses/","d":"2021-11-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-11-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-11-04","organization":"Tech sector","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"African Americans, Asian individuals","action":"Facial recognition technology is less accurate in identifying these groups","consequence":"Perpetuates racial bias in AI systems"}},{"i":"R806","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Facebook will shut down facial recognition system","s":"The removal of face recognition by Facebook comes as the tech industry faces a reckoning over the past few years over the ethics of using the technology. Facebook announced on Tuesday, November 2, it is shutting down its facial recognition system, which automatically identifies users in photos and videos, citing growing societal concerns about the use of such technology. “Regulators are still in the process of providing a clear set of rules governing its use,” Jerome Pesenti, vice president of artificial intelligence at Facebook, wrote in a blog post. “Amid this ongoing uncertainty, we believe that limiting the use of facial recognition to a narrow set of use cases is appropriate.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/facebook-will-shut-down-facial-recognition-system/","d":"2021-11-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-11-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-11-02","organization":"Facebook","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Users of Facebook","action":"Shutting down facial recognition system","consequence":"Reduced use of facial recognition technology"}},{"i":"R809","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Twitter launches competition find biases image cropping algorithm","s":"Twitter says the bounty competition was aimed at identifying ‘potential harms of this algorithm beyond what we identified ourselves’. Twitter said on Friday, July 30, it will launch a competition for computer researchers and hackers to identify biases in its image-cropping algorithm, after a group of researchers previously found the algorithm tended to exclude Black people and men. The competition is part of a wider effort across the tech industry to ensure artificial intelligence technologies act ethically. The social networking company said in a blog post that the bounty competition was aimed at identifying “potential harms of this algorithm beyond what we identified ourselves.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/twitter-launches-competition-find-biases-image-cropping-algorithm/","d":"2021-07-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07-30","organization":"Twitter","aiSystem":"image-cropping algorithm","affectedGroup":"Black people and men","action":"launching a competition for computer researchers and hackers","consequence":"identifying potential harms of the algorithm"}},{"i":"R810","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Mozilla regretsreporter youtube open up recommendation algorithm audits","s":"‘YouTube needs to admit their algorithm is designed in a way that harms and misinforms people,’ says Brandi Geurkink, Mozilla’s senior manager of advocacy. Mozilla on Wednesday, July 7, published a report detailing how YouTube’s recommendation algorithms remain at the core of the platform’s “rabbit hole” phenomenon that could send users spiraling into conspiracy theories and harmful, false information. It also showed that the recommendation algorithm routinely suggests videos that are potentially violative of YouTube’s content policies. The findings came via a crowd-sourced Firefox and Chrome extension called RegretsReporter, which allowed users to flag videos on the platform that they feel violate their standards or something that should not be available online.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/mozilla-regretsreporter-youtube-open-up-recommendation-algorithm-audits/","d":"2021-07-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07-07","organization":"YouTube","aiSystem":"YouTube's recommendation algorithm","affectedGroup":"Users of YouTube","action":"Recommendation algorithm suggests potentially violative videos","consequence":"Users exposed to harmful, false information and conspiracy theories"}},{"i":"R812","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Google searches new measure skin tones curb bias","s":"At issue is a six-color scale known as the Fitzpatrick Skin Type, which dermatologists have used since the 1970s. Alphabet’s Google told Reuters this week it is developing an alternative to the industry standard method for classifying skin tones, which a growing chorus of technology researchers and dermatologists says is inadequate for assessing whether products are biased against people of color. At issue is a six-color scale known as the Fitzpatrick Skin Type (FST), which dermatologists have used since the 1970s. Tech companies now rely on it to categorize people and measure whether products such as facial recognition systems or smartwatch heart-rate sensors perform equally well across skin tones.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/google-searches-new-measure-skin-tones-curb-bias/","d":"2021-06-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-06-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-06-18","organization":"Alphabet's Google","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Systems, Smartwatch Heart-Rate Sensors","affectedGroup":"People of Color","action":"Developing an alternative to the Fitzpatrick Skin Type scale for classifying skin tones","consequence":"Potential bias in product performance across skin tones"}},{"i":"R813","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Twitter artificial intelligence photo cropping results may 2021","s":"The study is done after user criticism about image previews in posts excluding Black people’s faces. Twitter’s image-cropping algorithm has a problematic bias toward excluding Black people and men, the company said in new research on Wednesday, May 19, adding that “how to crop an image is a decision best made by people.” The study by three of its machine learning researchers was conducted after user criticism last year about image previews in posts excluding Black people’s faces. It found an 8% difference from demographic parity in favor of women, and a 4% favor toward white individuals. The paper cited several possible reasons, including issues with image backgrounds and eye color, but said none were an excuse.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/twitter-artificial-intelligence-photo-cropping-results-may-2021/","d":"2021-05-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-05-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-05-19","organization":"Twitter","aiSystem":"Twitter's image-cropping algorithm","affectedGroup":"Black people and men","action":"Image cropping decisions","consequence":"Exclusion of Black people and men from image previews"}},{"i":"R815","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Inside coming voice profiling revolution","s":"Amazon and Google patents illustrate ability to listen to person’s pitch, voicing, tone to infer info that can potentially be used for targeted advertising. You decide to call a store that sells some hiking boots you’re thinking of buying. As you dial in, the computer of an artificial intelligence company hired by the store is activated. It retrieves its analysis of the speaking style you used when you phoned other companies the software firm services. The computer has concluded you are “friendly and talkative.” Using predictive routing, it connects you to a customer service agent who company research has identified as being especially good at getting friendly and talkative customers to buy more expensive versions of the goods they’re considering.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/inside-coming-voice-profiling-revolution/","d":"2021-05-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-05-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-05-03","organization":"Amazon and Google (patent holders)","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence company's voice analysis technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals making purchasing decisions","action":"AI system analyzes the caller's speaking style and predicts customer behavior","consequence":"Targeted marketing towards specific customer segments"}},{"i":"R817","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Twitter responsible machine learning initiative","s":"Twitter says the initiative may result in changes to Twitter, such as the removal of algorithms, new standards, and better control for people over the images they tweet. Twitter announced Wednesday, April 14, it had launched an initiative to study the effects of its machine learning (ML) algorithms and assess if the algorithms are causing undue harm. The initiative, called Responsible Machine Learning or Responsible ML, lets Twitter’s interdisciplinary ML Ethics, Transparency and Accountability (META) team “assess downstream or current unintentional harms in the algorithms we use and to help Twitter prioritize which issues to tackle first.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/twitter-responsible-machine-learning-initiative/","d":"2021-04-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-04-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-04-14","organization":"Twitter","aiSystem":"Machine Learning algorithms","affectedGroup":"Users of Twitter","action":"Studying the effects of machine learning algorithms","consequence":"Potential changes to Twitter's policies and practices"}},{"i":"R818","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Us lawmakers aim curtail face recognition technology ids capitol attackers","s":"Researchers and law enforcement have been running pictures from the Capitol siege through facial recognition, which looks for similar faces in databases of mugshots, social media headshots or other images. US lawmakers are moving ahead with efforts to ban facial recognition software even as the technology helps identify supporters of President Donald Trump who ransacked their workplace and forced them to evacuate this month. Researchers and law enforcement have been running photographs from the January 6 siege of the Capitol through facial recognition, which looks for similar faces in databases of mugshots, social media headshots or other images.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/us-lawmakers-aim-curtail-face-recognition-technology-ids-capitol-attackers/","d":"2021-01-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-01-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-01-06","organization":"US Lawmakers","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Technology","affectedGroup":"Supporters of President Donald Trump","action":"Identify supporters of President Donald Trump who ransacked the workplace and forced them to evacuate","consequence":"Forced evacuation and potential misuse of technology"}},{"i":"R888","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Texas phone tracking border surveillance","s":"The software was licensed as part of Gov. Greg Abbott’s troubled Operation Lone Star border crackdown. The Texas Department of Public Safety purchased access to powerful software capable of locating and following people through their phones as part of Republican Gov. Greg Abbott’s “border security disaster” efforts, according to documents reviewed by The Intercept. In 2021, Abbott proclaimed that the “surge of individuals unlawfully crossing the Texas-Mexico border posed an ongoing and imminent threat of disaster” to the state and its residents. Among other effects, the disaster declaration opened a spigot of government money to a variety of private firms ostensibly paid to help patrol and blockade the state’s border with Mexico.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/07/26/texas-phone-tracking-border-surveillance/","d":"2023-07-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021","organization":"Texas Department of Public Safety","aiSystem":"Powerful software capable of locating and following people through their phones","affectedGroup":"People whose phones were tracked","action":"Access to powerful software was provided to track and follow people through their phones","consequence":"Potential misuse for border surveillance and potential privacy violations"}},{"i":"R896","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Hoa surveillance license plate police flock","s":"Flock Safety works with police to market scanners to hundreds of private community groups — which have no privacy safeguards. At a city council meeting in June 2021, Mayor Thomas Kilgore, of Lakeway, Texas, made an announcement that confused his community. “I believe it is my duty to inform you that a surveillance system has been installed in the city of Lakeway,” he told the perplexed crowd. Kilgore was referring to a system consisting of eight license plate readers, installed by the private company Flock Safety, that was tracking cars on both private and public roads. Despite being in place for six months, no one had told residents that they were being watched.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/03/22/hoa-surveillance-license-plate-police-flock/","d":"2023-03-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"June 2021","organization":"Flock Safety (private company)","aiSystem":"License plate readers","affectedGroup":"Residents of Lakeway, Texas","action":"License plate readers were installed on both private and public roads","consequence":"No one informed residents that their cars were being tracked"}},{"i":"R912","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Uber patent driver risk algorithms","s":"Surveilling drivers under the guise of safety is a common thread in Uber’s patents. Experts warn the systems described could reinforce existing inequalities. Safety issues have dogged Uber since its early days as a black car-hailing service. Car accidents and physical altercations have persisted despite Uber’s attempts to monitor its cars and vet drivers; reports of sexual violence in its vehicles eventually led Uber to admit it was “ not immune ” to the problem. Amid public backlash and calls to address rider safety, Uber rolled out a flashy “ Safety First ” initiative in 2018, adding features like 911 assistance to its app, tightening screening of drivers, and for the first time, issuing a safety report that outlined traffic fatalities, fatal physical assaults, and sexual assaults on its platform.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/10/30/uber-patent-driver-risk-algorithms/","d":"2021-10-30","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2021-10-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-10-30","organization":"Uber Technologies Inc.","aiSystem":"Automated risk assessment algorithms","affectedGroup":"Drivers","action":"Generated and applied risk scores based on driver behavior and vehicle history","consequence":"Potential reinforcement of existing inequalities and biases"}},{"i":"R914","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Surveillance social media police microsoft shadowdragon kaseware","s":"The tool is the product of a growing industry whose work is usually kept from the public and utilized by police. A Michigan State Police contract , obtained by The Intercept, sheds new light on the growing use of little-known surveillance software that helps law enforcement agencies and corporations watch people’s social media and other website activity. The software, put out by a Wyoming company called ShadowDragon, allows police to suck in data from social media and other internet sources, including Amazon, dating apps, and the dark web, so they can identify persons of interest and map out their networks during investigations.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/09/21/surveillance-social-media-police-microsoft-shadowdragon-kaseware/","d":"2021-09-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-09-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-09-21","organization":"ShadowDragon (Wyoming company)","aiSystem":"ShadowDragon surveillance software","affectedGroup":"people's social media and internet activity","action":"police agencies and corporations watch and analyze data","consequence":"identification of persons of interest and mapping of their networks"}},{"i":"R917","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Atlas citizenship denaturalization homeland security","s":"Known as ATLAS, the software mines various federal databases for derogatory information. It runs autonomously on Amazon servers. Software used by the Department of Homeland Security to scan the records of millions of immigrants can automatically flag naturalized Americans to potentially have their citizenship revoked based on secret criteria, according to documents reviewed by The Intercept. The software, known as ATLAS, takes information from immigrants’ case files and runs it through various federal databases. ATLAS looks for indicators that someone is dangerous or dishonest and is ostensibly designed to detect fraud among people who come into contact with the U.S. immigration system.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/08/25/atlas-citizenship-denaturalization-homeland-security/","d":"2021-08-25","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-08-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-08-25","organization":"Department of Homeland Security","aiSystem":"ATLAS","affectedGroup":"Naturalized Americans","action":"Flagging for potential citizenship revocation","consequence":"Citizenship revocation risk"}},{"i":"R919","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Military pentagon extremism social media","s":"The military has previously balked at surveilling service members for extremist political views due to First Amendment protections. As part of the Biden administration’s crackdown on domestic extremism, the Pentagon plans to launch a pilot program for screening social media content for extremist material, according to internal Defense Department documents reviewed by The Intercept, as well as a source with direct knowledge of the program. An extremism steering committee led by Bishop Garrison, a senior adviser to the secretary of defense, is currently designing the social media screening pilot program, which will “continuously” monitor military personnel for “concerning behaviors,” according to a Pentagon briefing in late March.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/05/17/military-pentagon-extremism-social-media/","d":"2021-05-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2021-05-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-05-17","organization":"Department of Defense (DOD)","aiSystem":"AI-based social media monitoring system","affectedGroup":"Military personnel","action":"Continuously monitor military personnel for concerning behaviors","consequence":"Potential misuse of surveillance data for discriminatory purposes"}},{"i":"R920","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Chicago police killing boy adam toledo shotspotter","s":"After the police killing of Adam Toledo in Chicago, the ShotSpotter gunfire detection system deserves serious scrutiny. The city of Chicago is on edge, haunted by its past and fearful of what lies ahead, for once again a police officer has killed a child on its streets. On March 29 in Little Village, a predominantly Latino neighborhood on the West Side, police pursued and fatally shot 13-year-old Adam Toledo in what the police department has described as an “armed confrontation.” In the days since, details have trickled out in a manner that has done little to dispel the climate of distrust that now attends police shootings.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/04/13/chicago-police-killing-boy-adam-toledo-shotspotter/","d":"2021-04-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-04-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-03-29","organization":"Chicago Police Department","aiSystem":"ShotSpotter gunfire detection system","affectedGroup":"13-year-old Adam Toledo","action":"shot and killed","consequence":"police shootings increase fear and distrust"}},{"i":"R921","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Ice database surveillance lexisnexis","s":"The company signed a contract with an ICE division that plays a key role in deportations. The popular legal research and data brokerage firm LexisNexis signed a $16.8 million contract to sell information to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, according to documents shared with The Intercept. The deal is already drawing fire from critics and comes less than two years after the company downplayed its ties to ICE, claiming it was “not working with them to build data infrastructure to assist their efforts.” Though LexisNexis is perhaps best known for its role as a powerful scholarly and legal research tool, the company also caters to the immensely lucrative “risk” industry, providing, it says, 10,000 different data points on hundreds of millions of people to companies like financial institutions and insurance companies who want to, say, flag individuals with a history of fraud.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/04/02/ice-database-surveillance-lexisnexis/","d":"2021-04-02","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-04-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-04-02","organization":"LexisNexis","aiSystem":"LexisNexis database","affectedGroup":"Individuals with a history of fraud","action":"Provided data points to financial institutions and insurance companies","consequence":"Potential misuse of data for discriminatory purposes"}},{"i":"R923","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Lapd palantir data driven policing","s":"In a new book, a sociologist who spent months embedded with the LAPD details how data-driven policing techwashes bias. The killing of George Floyd last May sparked renewed scrutiny of data-driven policing. As protests raged around the world, 1,400 researchers signed an open letter calling on their colleagues to stop collaborating with police on algorithms, and cities like Santa Cruz , New Orleans , and Oakland variously banned predictive policing, facial recognition, and voice recognition. But elsewhere, police chiefs worked to deepen partnerships with tech companies, claiming that the answer to systemic bias and racism was simply more data.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/01/30/lapd-palantir-data-driven-policing/","d":"2021-01-30","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-01-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-01-30","organization":"Police departments, tech companies","aiSystem":"Data-driven policing technologies","affectedGroup":"Communities","action":"Deployment and use of data-driven policing technologies","consequence":"Systemic bias and racism"}},{"i":"R924","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Illinois cash bail reform","s":"Reformers typically propose predictive algorithms and electronic monitoring as alternatives to money bail. Illinois is different. Some organizers in Illinois recall getting “laughed out of the room” for supporting the abolition of money bail five years ago. But on January 13, Robert Peters, a longtime advocate for ending cash bail and now a state senator from Chicago, saw his legislative proposal to end money bail pass the General Assembly, along with a comprehensive package of criminal justice reforms written by the state’s Black Caucus. Democratic Gov. J.B. Pritzker has strongly indicated that he will sign the bill, which will make Illinois the first state in the country to end a detention system that is demonstrably racist and classist.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/01/17/illinois-cash-bail-reform/","d":"2021-01-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-01-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-01-13","organization":"Illinois State Senate, Democratic Governor J.B. Pritzker","aiSystem":"Predictive Algorithms and Electronic Monitoring","affectedGroup":"People awaiting trial who cannot afford bail","action":"Legislative proposal to end money bail passed","consequence":"Illinois becomes the first state to end a detention system that is demonstrably racist and classist"}},{"i":"R1021","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Man jailed raped and beaten after false facial recognition match dollar10m lawsuit alleges","s":"A 61-year-old man alleges that a facial recognition algorithm used a mugshot from the 1980s to ID him in a crime he didn’t commit. In January of 2022, Harvey Murphy was arrested and thrown in jail while trying to get his driver’s license renewed at a local DMV. According to a $10 million lawsuit Murphy has since filed, a “loss prevention” agent working for a Sunglass Hut retail store used facial recognition software to accuse Murphy of perpetrating an armed robbery at a store in Houston, Texas. In reality, Murphy was more than 2,000 miles away at the time of the robbery. According to a lawsuit 61-year-old Murphy has filed against Macy’s and Sunglass Hut, “he was arrested and put into an overcrowded maximum-security jail with violent criminals.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/man-jailed-raped-and-beaten-after-false-facial-recognition-match-dollar10m-lawsuit-alleges/","d":"2024-01-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"January 2022","organization":"Sunglass Hut (retail store) and Macy's","aiSystem":"Facial recognition software","affectedGroup":"Harvey Murphy","action":"Facial recognition software accused Murphy of armed robbery","consequence":"Murphy was arrested, jailed, and placed in an overcrowded maximum-security jail"}},{"i":"R1086","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm","1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Horribly unethical startup experimented on suicidal teens on facebook tumblr with chatbot","s":"Koko, a mental health nonprofit, found at-risk teens on platforms like Facebook and Tumblr, then tested an unproven intervention on them without obtaining informed consent. “It’s nuanced,” said the founder. Between August and September of last year, some users between the ages of 18 and 25 on platforms including Facebook, Discord, Tumblr, and Telegram who posted one of more than a thousand keywords ranging from “depression” to “sewer-slide” suggesting they were at risk of harming themselves were, without warning, directed to a chatbot. This was part of an experiment carried out by the founder of a controversial mental-health nonprofit called Koko and a Stony Brook University professor who also runs a suicide intervention consultancy.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/horribly-unethical-startup-experimented-on-suicidal-teens-on-facebook-tumblr-with-chatbot/","d":"2023-03-07","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"August to September 2022","organization":"Koko (mental health nonprofit) and Stony Brook University professor","aiSystem":"chatbot","affectedGroup":"at-risk teens between 18 and 25 years old","action":"users were directed to a chatbot without warning","consequence":"experiment conducted without informed consent"}},{"i":"R1102","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ai police sketches","s":"Police forensics is already plagued by human biases. Experts say AI will make it even worse. Two developers have used OpenAI’s DALL-E 2 image generation model to create a forensic sketch program that can create “hyper-realistic” police sketches of a suspect based on user inputs. The program, called Forensic Sketch AI-rtist, was created by developers Artur Fortunato and Filipe Reynaud as part of a hackathon in December 2022. The developers wrote that the program’s purpose is to cut down the time it usually takes to draw a suspect of a crime, which is “around two to three hours,” according to a presentation uploaded to the internet “We haven’t released the product yet, so we don’t have any active users at the moment, Fortunato and Reynaud told Motherboard in a joint email.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-police-sketches/","d":"2023-02-07","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2023-02-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-12","organization":"Artur Fortunato and Filipe Reynaud","aiSystem":"Forensic Sketch AI-rtist","affectedGroup":"Suspects of crimes","action":"Create 'hyper-realistic' police sketches based on user inputs","consequence":"Potential perpetuation of biases in forensic sketching"}},{"i":"R1120","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Artists are revolt against ai art on artstation","s":"Artists are fed up with AI art on the portfolio platform, which is owned by Epic Games, but the company isn’t backing down. ArtStation, a platform that allows game, film, media, and entertainment artists to connect and showcase their portfolios, has been flooded with the same image posted over and over by different users: a large red “no” sign covering the word “AI” paired with a caption that reads “NO TO AI GENERATED IMAGES.” Starting Tuesday, artists began protesting against the platform by uploading the image onto their portfolios after some users pointed out that AI-generated art was being featured on the site’s main Explore page.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/artists-are-revolt-against-ai-art-on-artstation/","d":"2022-12-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-12-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-12-14","organization":"ArtStation (owned by Epic Games)","aiSystem":"AI-generated art","affectedGroup":"Game, film, media, and entertainment artists","action":"uploading an image of a large red 'no' sign covering the word 'AI' paired with a caption 'NO TO AI GENERATED IMAGES' onto their portfolios","consequence":"protest against the platform and removal of AI-generated art from the main Explore page"}},{"i":"R1121","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Billie eilish tiktok ai art","s":"The modified images of the singer were viewed 11 million times before being removed for violating TikTok’s community guidelines around sexual harassment. Ultra sexualised images of Billie Eilish likened to “deepfake pornography” went viral on TikTok and were amplified by the app’s algorithm. A photo gallery video featuring the singer’s face on sexually exaggerated bodies was seen by 11 million people in four days before being removed for violating TikTok’s community guidelines around sexual harassment. Tracy Harwood, professor of digital culture at the UK’s De Montfort University, told VICE World News it looked like the images had been created using some form of AI imaging or AI art generator app.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/billie-eilish-tiktok-ai-art/","d":"2022-12-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-12-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-12-14","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"AI imaging or AI art generator app","affectedGroup":"Billie Eilish","action":"Generated and displayed sexually exaggerated images of Billie Eilish","consequence":"Viewed 11 million times before being removed for violating community guidelines"}},{"i":"R1124","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Openais new chatbot will tell you how to shoplift and make explosives","s":"ChatGPT is yet another reminder that all AI systems are prone to bias and misuse. Sophisticated AI language models like GPT-3 have made AI-generated text increasingly realistic, and often hard to tell apart from something written by a human. And as the newest chatbot from OpenAI demonstrates, they’re also extremely easy to abuse for all sorts of hilarious and nefarious purposes. The new bot, called ChatGPT , uses a recent evolution of the GPT-3 model to generate believable dialogue from a short writing prompt. This enables it to write stories, answer complex questions, explain concepts, and—with the right prompt—tell you how to do crimes.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/openais-new-chatbot-will-tell-you-how-to-shoplift-and-make-explosives/","d":"2022-12-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-12-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-12-01","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"AI language model (GPT-3)","affectedGroup":"general public","action":"generate believable dialogue from a short writing prompt","consequence":"enable writing stories, answering complex questions, explaining concepts, and potentially instructing on criminal activities"}},{"i":"R1125","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Facebook pulls its new ai for science because its broken and terrible","s":"The demo of Galactica generated fake research and filtered out searches for ‘racism’ and ‘AIDS.’ Facebook parent company Meta has pulled the public demo for its “scientific knowledge” AI model after academics showed it was generating fake and misleading information while filtering out entire categories of research. Released earlier this week, the company described Galactica as an AI language model that “can store, combine and reason about scientific knowledge”—summarizing research papers, solving equations, and doing a range of other useful sciencey tasks. But scientists and academics quickly discovered that the AI system’s summaries were generating a shocking amount of misinformation, including citing real authors for research papers that don’t exist.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/facebook-pulls-its-new-ai-for-science-because-its-broken-and-terrible/","d":"2022-11-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-11-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-11-18","organization":"Meta (Facebook parent company)","aiSystem":"Galactica AI language model","affectedGroup":"Academics, researchers, general public","action":"Generated fake and misleading information, filtered out research categories","consequence":"Shocking amount of misinformation, including citations of non-existent research"}},{"i":"R1126","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Github users file a class action lawsuit against microsoft for training an ai tool with their code","s":"This lawsuit represents a growing concern from programmers, artists, and other people that AI systems may be using their code, artwork, and other data without permission. GitHub programmers have filed a class-action lawsuit against GitHub , its parent Microsoft, and its technology partner, OpenAI, for allegedly violating their open-source licenses and using their code to train Microsoft’s latest AI tool, called Copilot. GitHub Copilot , which was launched in June, suggests code and functions to GitHub users in real time. Copilot is powered by Codex, an AI system that was created by OpenAI and licensed to Microsoft.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/github-users-file-a-class-action-lawsuit-against-microsoft-for-training-an-ai-tool-with-their-code/","d":"2022-11-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-11-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-11-04","organization":"Microsoft, OpenAI, GitHub","aiSystem":"Codex","affectedGroup":"GitHub programmers, artists","action":"Using code and data of GitHub users to train Codex","consequence":"Violation of open-source licenses, potential misuse of user data"}},{"i":"R1127","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"This tool lets anyone see the bias in ai image generators","s":"The Stable Diffusion Explorer shows how words like “assertive” and “gentle” are mapped to sexist stereotypes. By now, you’ve probably heard that bias in AI systems is a huge problem—whether it’s facial recognition failing more often on Black people or AI image generators like DALL-E reproducing racist and sexist stereotypes But what does algorithmic bias actually look like in the real world, and what causes it to manifest? A new tool designed by an AI ethics researcher attempts to answer that question by letting anyone query a popular text-to-image system, allowing people to see for themselves how certain word combinations produce biased results.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/this-tool-lets-anyone-see-the-bias-in-ai-image-generators/","d":"2022-11-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-11-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-11-03","organization":"AI ethics researcher","aiSystem":"Stable Diffusion","affectedGroup":"Anyone who queries the system","action":"Querying the system with specific word combinations","consequence":"Seeing biased results in generated images"}},{"i":"R1128","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Record labels say ai music generators threaten music industry","s":"The RIAA says AI music generators will be trained on copyrighted music, the same way popular image generators are based on billions of images taken by artists. The music industry’s lobbying arm claims that services using machine learning to alter tracks are infringing on artists’ rights. As first reported by TorrentFreak , the Recording Industry Association of America listed AI-powered music websites that make remixes, improve homemade tracks, or strip songs of vocals or instrumentals harm artists, in a response to a request from the Office of the US Trade Representative. Artists working within all kinds of media have raised concerns in recent years—and increasingly, with the rising popularity of text-to-image generators like DALL-E—about whether AI-generated art infringes on individuals’ copyright.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/record-labels-say-ai-music-generators-threaten-music-industry/","d":"2022-10-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-10-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-10-21","organization":"RIAA (Recording Industry Association of America)","aiSystem":"AI music generators","affectedGroup":"artists","action":"AI music generators trained on copyrighted music","consequence":"infringement of artists' rights"}},{"i":"R1131","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Ai is probably using your images and its not easy to opt out","s":"In one stark example of how sensitive images can end up powering these AI tools, a user found a medical image in the LAION dataset, which was used to train Stable Diffusion and Google’s Imagen. Viral image-generating AI tools like DALL-E and Stable Diffusion are powered by massive datasets of images that are scraped from the internet, and if one of those images is of you, there’s no easy way to opt out, even if you never explicitly agreed to have it posted online. Ars Technica first reported the inclusion of the image in the dataset. On the LAION Discord channel, a user expressed their concern for their friend, who found herself in the dataset through Have I Been Trained , a site that allows people to search the dataset.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-is-probably-using-your-images-and-its-not-easy-to-opt-out/","d":"2022-09-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-09-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-09-26","organization":"LAION","aiSystem":"Stable Diffusion, Imagen","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose images were included in the dataset","action":"Images were included in the LAION dataset and subsequently used to power AI tools like Stable Diffusion and Imagen","consequence":"No easy way to opt out of having one's image included in the dataset, leading to potential misuse and unauthorized use of personal images"}},{"i":"R1135","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Striking seattle workers win against surveillance cameras in vehicles","s":"Sandwich company Homegrown put artificial intelligence powered cameras in drivers’ vehicles. Now drivers will be allowed to cover them up. Delivery drivers for Seattle-based sandwich company Homegrown have won a major victory against artificial intelligence assisted surveillance cameras placed in their vehicles. After a strike earlier this month, workers say they were given the right to place privacy screens over the cameras, according to an announcement from union UNITE HERE Local 8. The news signals the rise of artificial intelligence assisted cameras placed in delivery vehicles and the visceral push back against them.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/striking-seattle-workers-win-against-surveillance-cameras-in-vehicles/","d":"2022-08-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-08-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-08-26","organization":"Homegrown Sandwich Company","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence Assisted Surveillance Cameras","affectedGroup":"Delivery Drivers","action":"Workers were given the right to cover up surveillance cameras in their vehicles.","consequence":"Workers can now protect their privacy by covering up the cameras."}},{"i":"R1137","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Stable diffusion stability ai nsfw ai generated porn","s":"People are creating the DALL-E of porn with something called ‘stable diffusion’—and the company behind the tool wants no part of it. On Monday, after an invite-only testing period, artificial intelligence company Stability AI released its text-to-image generation model, called Stable Diffusion, into the world as open-access. Like DALL-E Mini or Midjourney , Stable Diffusion is capable of creating vivid, even photorealistic images from simple text prompts using neural networks. Unlike DALL-E Mini or Midjourney, whose creators have implemented limits on what kind of images they can produce, Stable Diffusion’s model can be downloaded and tweaked by users to generate whatever they want.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/stable-diffusion-stability-ai-nsfw-ai-generated-porn/","d":"2022-08-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-08-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-08-24","organization":"Stability AI","aiSystem":"Stable Diffusion","affectedGroup":"Porn creators","action":"Generate pornographic images","consequence":"Potential exploitation and misuse of AI technology"}},{"i":"R1138","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Is joe biden dead replaced by 10 different deepfake body doubles an investigation","s":"A conspiracy theory started on Twitter claims that Joe Biden’s videos are deepfakes. A viral video speculating that Joe Biden is either dead, multiple people, a deepfake, or some combination of all of those has been viewed more than a million times on Twitter. The original post is from the Twitter account for the band Five Times August, led by Dallas-area musician Bradley James Skistimas. The account has 91,000 followers. He posted two pictures of Biden from two different occasions, giving two separate addresses—one about gas prices , and another from remarks given at a conference —next to each other. “Two videos posted within a couple of hours of each other.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/is-joe-biden-dead-replaced-by-10-different-deepfake-body-doubles-an-investigation/","d":"2022-07-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-07-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-07-29","organization":"Five Times August (Twitter account)","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly those following Five Times August on Twitter","action":"Posted two images of Joe Biden from different occasions next to each other","consequence":"Viral spread leading to over a million views on Twitter"}},{"i":"R1139","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Racist algorithms are making robots racist too","s":"Researchers show that large AI models are threatening to bring racist and sexist bias into the realm of robotics. There’s now a small library’s worth of evidence that AI systems are biased and tend to replicate harmful racist and sexist stereotypes So it may not shock you to learn that those racist and sexist algorithms can be used to make racist and sexist robots. That’s according to a new paper from researchers at Johns Hopkins University and Georgia Tech, who trained a virtual robot to interact with physical objects using a widely-used AI language model. The robot was presented with objects adorned with pictures of human faces of different races and genders, then given tasks to complete that involve manipulating the objects.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/racist-algorithms-are-making-robots-racist-too/","d":"2022-07-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-07-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-07-11","organization":"Johns Hopkins University and Georgia Tech","aiSystem":"widely-used AI language model","affectedGroup":"virtual robot interacting with physical objects","action":"trained a virtual robot to interact with physical objects","consequence":"the robot was presented with objects adorned with pictures of human faces of different races and genders, then given tasks to complete that involve manipulating the objects"}},{"i":"R1140","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Deepfakes might be used in remote job interviews fbi warns","s":"The FBI said in an announcement that it has received complaints of people deepfaking their way into job interviews. If you’re interviewing someone for a job online, make sure you are paying attention to see if the person’s words line up with their lip movements. According to the FBI, people have reported suspicious online interviews, and believe they could be deepfakes. The FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3) released a public service announcement on Tuesday, warning of “an increase in complaints reporting the use of deepfakes and stolen Personally Identifiable Information (PII) to apply for a variety of remote work and work-at-home positions.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/deepfakes-might-be-used-in-remote-job-interviews-fbi-warns/","d":"2022-07-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-07-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-07-01","organization":"FBI (Federal Bureau of Investigation)","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Job applicants","action":"Deepfake technology used to impersonate job candidates during online interviews","consequence":"Potential for fraud and misrepresentation in remote job applications"}},{"i":"R1144","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ai trained on 4chan becomes hate speech machine","s":"After 24 hours, the nine bots running on 4chan had posted 15,000 times. AI researcher and YouTuber Yannic Kilcher trained an AI using 3.3 million threads from 4chan’s infamously toxic Politically Incorrect /pol/ board. He then unleashed the bot back onto 4chan with predictable results—the AI was just as vile as the posts it was trained on, spouting racial slurs and engaging with antisemitic threads. After Kilcher posted his video and a copy of the program to Hugging Face, a kind of GitHub for AI, ethicists and researchers in the AI field expressed concern. The bot, which Kilcher called GPT-4chan, “the most horrible model on the internet”—a reference to GPT-3, a language model developed by Open AI that uses deep learning to produce text—was shockingly effective and replicated the tone and feel of 4chan posts.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-trained-on-4chan-becomes-hate-speech-machine/","d":"2022-06-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-06-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-06-07","organization":"Yannic Kilcher (AI researcher and YouTuber)","aiSystem":"GPT-4chan","affectedGroup":"users of 4chan","action":"AI generated and posted hateful content","consequence":"repetition of toxic language and engagement with antisemitic content"}},{"i":"R1145","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Ethical deepfakes deep tom cruise ai generated porn","s":"The ‘ethical’ AI company that made the viral deepfake of Tom Cruise is tied to non-consensual AI-generated porn. Last year, a shockingly convincing deepfake video of Tom Cruise went viral on TikTok, quickly gaining more than 100 million views. The virality of “DeepTomCruise” led the video’s creator, Chris Umé, to cofound an “ethical” AI-generated content company called Metaphysic, which raised $7.5 million from Logan Paul, Winklevoss Capital, and other venture capital firms. Metaphysic’s goal is not only to create profitable, commercial deepfake content for clients like Gillette , but to actively move deepfake technology away from its heinous origins in the world of non-consensual pornography.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ethical-deepfakes-deep-tom-cruise-ai-generated-porn/","d":"2022-05-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-05-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-05-17","organization":"Metaphysic (an ethical AI-generated content company)","aiSystem":"AI-generated content technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals involved in non-consensual porn production","action":"Created and distributed non-consensual AI-generated porn","consequence":"Potential exploitation and violation of consent"}},{"i":"R1146","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Face swap app on apple app store google play deepfakes","s":"A loophole in the Apple and Google app store policies allows a deepfake app to invite customers to make non-consensual porn, without getting banned. On the Google Play and Apple App stores, FaceMagic is pitched as a fun and harmless face swapping app: “Do you want to see your best friend, colleague, or boss dancing? Or do you want to morph your face into celebrities?” the app description says on the Google Play and Apple App stores, alongside a video that shows how users can face swap anyone into a video of Robert Downey Jr.’s Iron Man or basketball player Steph Curry. But on porn tube sites, ads for FaceMagic have a more nefarious pitch.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/face-swap-app-on-apple-app-store-google-play-deepfakes/","d":"2022-05-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-05-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-05-10","organization":"Apple and Google app stores, FaceMagic","aiSystem":"FaceMagic face swapping app","affectedGroup":"Customers, friends, colleagues, bosses, celebrities","action":"Users can face swap anyone into videos of public figures","consequence":"Non-consensual porn production"}},{"i":"R1147","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Facebooks new ai system has a high propensity for racism and bias","s":"The company’s AI researchers say its new language model is generating ‘toxic’ results that often reinforce stereotypes. Facebook and its parent company, Meta, recently released a new tool that can be used to quickly develop state-of-the-art AI. But according to the company’s researchers, the system has the same problem as its predecessors: It’s extremely bad at avoiding results that reinforce racist and sexist stereotypes. The new system, called OPT-175B, is a kind of template known as a large language model, a collection of pre-trained components that are increasingly used in machine-learning tools that process human language.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/facebooks-new-ai-system-has-a-high-propensity-for-racism-and-bias/","d":"2022-05-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-05-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-05-09","organization":"Facebook (Meta)","aiSystem":"OPT-175B large language model","affectedGroup":"People","action":"Generating language results","consequence":"Reinforcement of racist and sexist stereotypes"}},{"i":"R118","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm","1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Chatgpt lawsuit claims it advised a shooter on how and where to strike","s":"Florida’s attorney general claims ChatGPT advised the FSU shooter on what gun to use, which ammo to buy, and when to strike. OpenAI says the chatbot did nothing wrong. Florida’s attorney general has launched a criminal investigation into OpenAI, alleging that ChatGPT helped plan the mass shooting at Florida State University that killed two people last year. According to The Washington Post , Attorney General James Uthmeier made the announcement at a news conference on Tuesday, claiming the chatbot gave tactical advice to the suspected shooter. “The chatbot advised the shooter on what type of gun to use, on which ammo went with which gun, on whether or not a gun would be useful at short range,” Uthmeier said.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-lawsuit-claims-it-advised-a-shooter-on-how-and-where-to-strike/","d":"2026-04-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"FSU shooter","action":"provided tactical advice","consequence":"planned mass shooting resulting in fatalities"}},{"i":"R283","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Zoom can train ai on your videos what you need to know","s":"Zoom’s AI features were trained on user content, but the company has backpedaled following user backlash. Like everyone else, Zoom has added AI features to improve its app and videoconferencing service. We all love the ease and speed AI provides, but there are often concerns about the data used to train models, and Zoom has been at the center of the controversy. It’s backpedaling now, saying it won’t use user content to train its AI models. News leaked in May 2022 that Zoom was working on emotion-sensing AI that could analyze faces in meetings. Beyond the potential for inaccurate analysis, the results could be discriminatory.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/zoom-can-train-ai-on-your-videos-what-you-need-to-know/","d":"2023-08-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-05-01","organization":"Zoom","aiSystem":"Zoom's AI features","affectedGroup":"Users of Zoom","action":"AI features were trained on user content","consequence":"Potential for inaccurate analysis and discriminatory results"}},{"i":"R408","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Texas bitcoin mines data centers power grid demand","s":"This story was originally published by Inside Climate News and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. Cheryl Shadden cannot sleep. The 61-year-old nurse, who works at hospitals giving patients anesthesia, says she is kept up at night by the nonstop mechanical whir of fans spinning to cool tens of thousands of computers. Shadden lives in […]. Shadden lives in Granbury, Texas, about 40 miles southwest of Fort Worth, with her seven dogs, six horses, six cats and a parrot. In 2022, after 23 years in the area, Shadden got a new neighbor: a 300-megawatt Bitcoin facility, referred to as a “mine,” where computers run around the clock to help maintain a global network of transactions in the cryptocurrency.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/07/texas-bitcoin-mines-data-centers-power-grid-demand/","d":"2024-07-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Bitcoin mining facility","affectedGroup":"Cheryl Shadden, residents of Granbury, Texas","action":"Computers running continuously to support Bitcoin transactions","consequence":"Disrupted sleep due to noise from cooling fans"}},{"i":"R413","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Ai artificial intelligence carbon footprint climate change data centers electricity consumption","s":"This story was originally published by Yale E360 and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. Two months after its release in November 2022, OpenAI’s ChatGPT had 100 million active users, and suddenly tech corporations were racing to offer the public more “generative AI” Pundits compared the new technology’s impact to the Internet, or electrification, or the […]. Two months after its release in November 2022, OpenAI’s ChatGPT had 100 million active users, and suddenly tech corporations were racing to offer the public more “generative AI” Pundits compared the new technology’s impact to the Internet, or electrification, or the Industrial Revolution—or the discovery of fire Time will sort hype from reality, but one consequence of the explosion of artificial intelligence is clear: this technology’s environmental footprint is large and growing.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2024/02/ai-artificial-intelligence-carbon-footprint-climate-change-data-centers-electricity-consumption/","d":"2024-02-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-11-01","organization":"Tech Corporations","aiSystem":"OpenAI's ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Public","action":"Tech corporations offering more 'generative AI'","consequence":"Large and growing environmental footprint of AI technology"}},{"i":"R421","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"What did the irs want with your selfies","s":"Facing mounting bipartisan pressure, the Internal Revenue Service announced yesterday that it’s walking back plans to deploy facial recognition software to identify taxpayers. In November, the IRS signed an $86 million contract with identity verification startup ID.me, announcing that it would require taxpayers to provide personal, identifying materials, including selfies, to access their tax records. […]. Privacy and civil rights advocates responded immediately, forming a coalition of close to 20 groups —from the National Lawyers Guild to the Council on American-Islamic Relations—that criticized the “destructive results of facial recognition technology…from police using it to track Black Lives Matter protesters, to wrongful arrests , to manipulative marketing .”","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/mojo-wire/2022/02/what-did-the-irs-want-with-your-selfies/","d":"2022-02-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-02-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-02-08","organization":"Internal Revenue Service (IRS)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition software","affectedGroup":"Taxpayers","action":"Announced plans to deploy facial recognition software to identify taxpayers","consequence":"Privacy concerns and criticism from civil rights advocates"}},{"i":"R575","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Artificial intelligence transforming lives neurodivergent people","s":"AI-powered chatbots emerge as an unlikely ally, helping people navigate social encounters with real-time guidance. Although this new technology is not without risks, many neurodivergent users now see it as a lifeline. For Cape Town-based filmmaker Kate D’hotman, connecting with movie audiences comes naturally. Far more daunting is speaking with others. “I’ve never understood how people [decipher] social cues,” the 40-year-old director of horror films says. D’hotman has autism and attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), which can make relating to others exhausting and a challenge. However, since 2022, D’hotman has been a regular user of ChatGPT, the popular AI-powered chatbot from OpenAI, relying on it to overcome communication barriers at work and in her personal life.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/artificial-intelligence-transforming-lives-neurodivergent-people/","d":"2025-07-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-01-01","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT, AI-powered chatbot","affectedGroup":"neurodivergent people, particularly those with autism and ADHD","action":"using ChatGPT to overcome communication barriers","consequence":"improved ability to navigate social encounters and communicate effectively"}},{"i":"R800","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Intel introduces real time deepfake detector fakecatcher","s":"Intel’s FakeCatcher looks at blood flow in video pixels to see if a person in a video is real or not. MANILA, Philippines – Intel on Monday, November 14, introduced new software that is purportedly able to identify deepfake videos in real-time. The company claims it’s the world’s first real-time deepfake detector, able to return results in milliseconds with an accuracy rate of 96%. Deepfakes are videos that make use of AI to convincingly mimic another person’s face to make it appear they are the one saying or doing something. Intel’s deepfake detection platform is called FakeCatcher, developed by Ilke Demir, senior staff research scientist in Intel Labs, in collaboration with Umur Ciftci from the State University of New York at Binghamton.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/intel-introduces-real-time-deepfake-detector-fakecatcher/","d":"2022-11-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-11-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-11-14","organization":"Intel","aiSystem":"FakeCatcher","affectedGroup":"Deepfake videos","action":"Identify and detect deepfake videos in real-time","consequence":"Potential misuse of deepfake videos"}},{"i":"R863","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Meta facebook terrorism censorship speech","s":"Hannah Byrne joined Facebook to combat far-right extremism. She’s now convinced the tech giant can’t be trusted with such power. For two years , Hannah Byrne was part of an invisible machine that determines what over 3 billion people around the world can say on the internet. From her perch within Meta’s Counterterrorism and Dangerous Organizations team, Byrne helped craft one of the most powerful and secretive censorship policies in internet history. Her work adhered to the basic tenet of content moderation: Online speech can cause offline harm. Stop the bad speech — or bad speakers — and you have perhaps saved a life.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/12/04/meta-facebook-terrorism-censorship-speech/","d":"2024-12-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-01-01","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"AI-driven content moderation system","affectedGroup":"Over 3 billion internet users worldwide","action":"Crafted and implemented a powerful censorship policy","consequence":"Potential misuse of power leading to offline harm"}},{"i":"R900","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Openai chatgpt ai bias ethics","s":"ChatGPT, the latest novelty from OpenAI, replicates the ugliest war on terror-style racism. A DALL-E generation of “an oil painting of America’s war on terror if conducted by an artificial intelligence.” Image: Elise Swain/The Intercept; DALL-E Sensational new machine learning breakthroughs seem to sweep our Twitter feeds every day. We hardly have time to decide whether software that can instantly conjure an image of Sonic the Hedgehog addressing the United Nations is purely harmless fun or a harbinger of techno-doom. ChatGPT, the latest artificial intelligence novelty act, is easily the most impressive text-generating demo to date.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2022/12/08/openai-chatgpt-ai-bias-ethics/","d":"2022-12-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-12-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-12-08","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT, an AI language model","affectedGroup":"general public, users of AI-generated content","action":"generates text","consequence":"replicates racist imagery and rhetoric"}},{"i":"R901","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Cia google content moderation trust lab","s":"Trust Lab, founded by a former Google exec for content moderation, will identify “online harmful content, including toxicity and misinformation.” Trust Lab was founded by a team of well-credentialed Big Tech alumni who came together in 2021 with a mission: Make online content moderation more transparent, accountable, and trustworthy. A year later, the company announced a “strategic partnership” with the CIA’s venture capital firm. Trust Lab’s basic pitch is simple: Globe-spanning internet platforms like Facebook and YouTube so thoroughly and consistently botch their content moderation efforts that decisions about what speech to delete ought to be turned over to completely independent outside firms — firms like Trust Lab.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2022/12/02/cia-google-content-moderation-trust-lab/","d":"2022-12-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2022-12-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-12-02","organization":"Trust Lab (founded by a former Google exec)","aiSystem":"AI-based content moderation systems on social media platforms","affectedGroup":"Online users and content creators","action":"Identify and flag 'online harmful content, including toxicity and misinformation'","consequence":"Potential misalignment between AI-driven content moderation and human judgment, leading to biased or incomplete content removal decisions"}},{"i":"R903","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Police surveillance shotspotter detroit","s":"The company behind the mics, ShotSpotter, is going all out to guide police stations across the country on how to use relief money for the controversial technology. Detroit’s city council will soon vote on whether to spend millions in federal cash meant to ease the economic pains of the coronavirus pandemic on ShotSpotter, a controversial surveillance technology critics say is invasive, discriminatory, and fundamentally broken. ShotSpotter purports to do one thing very well: telling cops a gun has been fired as soon as the trigger is pulled. Using a network of microphones hitched to telephone poles, rooftops, and other urban vantage points, ShotSpotter is essentially an Alexa that listens for a bang rather than voice commands.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2022/09/17/police-surveillance-shotspotter-detroit/","d":"2022-09-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-09-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-09-17","organization":"ShotSpotter","aiSystem":"ShotSpotter","affectedGroup":"Police Departments, Citizens","action":"Deploying ShotSpotter surveillance technology","consequence":"Potential misuse leading to invasions of privacy, discrimination, and inefficiency"}},{"i":"R908","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Clearview face recognition staten island","s":"Clearview and its right-wing founder have become emblematic of the threat posed by powerful and typically unsupervised software. The Staten Island district attorney’s use of the highly controversial Clearview face recognition system included attempts to dig up the social media accounts of homicide victims and was paid for with equally controversial asset forfeiture cash, according to city records provided to The Intercept. Clearview has garnered international attention and intense criticism for its simple premise: What if you could instantly identify anyone in the world with only their picture? Using billions of images scraped from social media sites, Clearview sells police and other governmental agencies the ability to match a photo to a name using face recognition, no search warrant required — a power civil libertarians and privacy advocates say simply places too much unsupervised…","u":"https://theintercept.com/2022/05/04/clearview-face-recognition-staten-island/","d":"2022-05-04","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-05-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-05-04","organization":"Staten Island district attorney's office, Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Clearview face recognition system","affectedGroup":"Homicide victims, individuals whose social media accounts were accessed","action":"Used Clearview face recognition system to access social media accounts of homicide victims","consequence":"Potential misuse of personal information, invasion of privacy"}},{"i":"R1029","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation","1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Child sex abuse material was found in a major ai dataset researchers arent surprised","s":"The LAION-5B dataset is the basis for numerous AI models. Researchers have long warned that massive training datasets are poorly audited. Over 1,000 images of sexually abused children have been discovered inside the largest dataset used to train image-generating AI, shocking everyone except for the people who have warned about this exact sort of thing for years. The dataset was created by LAION, a non-profit organization behind the massive image datasets used by generative AI systems like Stable Diffusion. Following a report from researchers at Stanford University, 404 Media reported that LAION confirmed the presence of child sexual abuse material (CSAM) in the dataset, called LAION-5B, and scrubbed it from their online channels.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/child-sex-abuse-material-was-found-in-a-major-ai-dataset-researchers-arent-surprised/","d":"2023-12-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-20","organization":"LAION, a non-profit organization","aiSystem":"Generative AI systems like Stable Diffusion","affectedGroup":"Sexually abused children","action":"AI-generated images of sexually abused children were discovered","consequence":"The images were removed from LAION's online channels"}},{"i":"R1032","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Elon musks grok ai is pushing misinformation and legitimizing conspiracies","s":"Grok’s selling point is real-time access to news, but Motherboard found that it generated inaccurate information about Israel-Palestine and lent credence to conspiracy theories. Elon Musk released Grok to X premium subscribers on Friday, giving paid users of the website formally known as Twitter their first look at the billionaire’s much-hyped entry into the AI race. The chatbot’s big selling point is that it is designed to integrate with X and draw information from users’ posts in order to have access to real-time events around the world. But, perhaps unsurprisingly, the chatbot is just as reliable at giving accurate information as the once-cherished platform formerly known as Twitter and its right-wing billionaire owner—which is to say, not at all The chatbot produced fake timelines for news events and misinformation when tested by Motherboard, and lent credence to conspiracy theories…","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/elon-musks-grok-ai-is-pushing-misinformation-and-legitimizing-conspiracies/","d":"2023-12-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-08","organization":"Elon Musk's company (not explicitly named)","aiSystem":"Grok chatbot","affectedGroup":"general public, particularly those using Twitter","action":"generated fake timelines for news events and misinformation","consequence":"lent credence to conspiracy theories, spread misinformation"}},{"i":"R1034","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"The lapd is using controversial mass surveillance tracking software","s":"Cobwebs Technologies’ WebLoc software allows police to track individuals using geolocation signals displayed on a map interface. The LAPD has purchased controversial mass surveillance software that allows police to track individuals using geolocation data. The company that provides the software, called WebLoc, is Cobwebs Technologies The Israeli company was founded by three IDF veterans in 2015 and has previously sold contracts to the IRS and the Texas State Police Each contract costs around $200,000 per year. The LAPD’s contract with Cobwebs Technology was revealed in a report from Knock LA , which obtained documents via freedom of information requests.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/the-lapd-is-using-controversial-mass-surveillance-tracking-software/","d":"2023-11-29","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-29","organization":"LAPD (Los Angeles Police Department)","aiSystem":"WebLoc software","affectedGroup":"Individuals","action":"Track individuals using geolocation signals displayed on a map interface","consequence":"Potential privacy invasion, misuse of public data"}},{"i":"R1035","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Chatgpt can reveal personal information from real people google researchers show","s":"The popular AI chatbot is divulging sensitive information from its training data, according to a team of researchers at Google. A team of Google researchers have unveiled a novel attack on ChatGPT, showing that OpenAI’s popular AI chatbot will divulge personal information from real people. The underlying machine learning model that powers ChatGPT, like all so-called Large Language Models (LLMs), was trained on massive amounts of data scraped from the internet. With training and reinforcement from humans, the program ideally generates new strings of texts without churning out any of the original text it ingested. Previous work has already shown that image generators can be forced to generate examples from their training data—including copyrighted works —and an early OpenAI LLM produced contact information belonging to a researcher.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/chatgpt-can-reveal-personal-information-from-real-people-google-researchers-show/","d":"2023-11-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-29","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Real People","action":"divulged personal information","consequence":"potential privacy infringement"}},{"i":"R1037","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Nyc mayor casually announces hes deepfaking himself experts horrified","s":"Eric Adams is using AI to make New Yorkers think he speaks their language, which experts called “Orwellian.” New York City Mayor Eric Adams has been making audio deepfakes of himself to speak to New Yorkers in different languages. At a press conference Monday that was meant to brief reporters on a recent “chatbot forum” at City Hall, Adams let slip that his office has been robo-calling New Yorkers with an algorithmically-generated version of his voice speaking in different languages that the mayor does not speak. The purpose of the Monday press conference was to announce the city’s impending use of “AI” voice chatbots that will speak to New Yorkers in their language of choice when they call 311 or other services, but the mayor casually mentioned that the city has already been deploying the technology for other purposes, and that New Yorkers are already being misled about the range of…","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/nyc-mayor-casually-announces-hes-deepfaking-himself-experts-horrified/","d":"2023-10-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-10-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-10-17","organization":"New York City Mayor's Office","aiSystem":"AI voice chatbots","affectedGroup":"New York City residents","action":"Adams' office robo-called New Yorkers with an algorithmically-generated version of his voice speaking in different languages","consequence":"Misleading New Yorkers about the range of languages the city's AI voice chatbots can speak"}},{"i":"R1039","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Metas ai stickers wa","s":"The fun is guaranteed not to last. Meta has started slowly rolling out its new AI-generated stickers for Messenger, and the results so far are totally unhinged. The company, which owns Facebook, Instagram, and WhatsApp, announced that it would be introducing AI tools to Messenger last week, starting with a limited rollout in the US. On Tuesday night, examples of the kinds of stickers the AI tool generates based on user prompts went viral, and it’s easy to see why. Montreal-based artist Pier-Olivier Desbiens posted a few examples, and they include: Nintendo character Waluigi holding a rifle, a Mickey Mouse-toilet hybrid, child soldiers, a nude Justin Trudeau bending over, and a busty Karl Marx wearing a dress.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/metas-ai-stickers-wa/","d":"2023-10-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-10-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-10-04","organization":"Meta (Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp)","aiSystem":"AI sticker generator","affectedGroup":"general public, social media users","action":"AI-generated stickers were created based on user prompts","consequence":"Generated content included inappropriate and offensive images"}},{"i":"R1040","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"The hollywood writers strike is over and yes studios can use ai","s":"The tentative agreement states that studios can present AI-generated material to writers, but it will not affect their pay or credit. The Writers’ Guild of America reached a tentative agreement with production studios on Sunday, bringing the 148-day-long film and TV writers’ strike to an end on Wednesday as members will soon begin to vote on ratification. A summary of the union’s new Minimum Basic Agreement (MBA) with the Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers was released late Tuesday night , and the strike officially ended at 12:01 a.m. on Wednesday morning. “We can say, with great pride, that this deal is exceptional—with meaningful gains and protections for writers in every sector of the membership,” the WGA said in a statement when the tentative agreement was announced.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/the-hollywood-writers-strike-is-over-and-yes-studios-can-use-ai/","d":"2023-09-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-27","organization":"Writers' Guild of America","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Film and TV writers","action":"Studios can present AI-generated material to writers","consequence":"No impact on writers' pay or credit"}},{"i":"R1042","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Openai says authors chatgpt copyright claims defective in motion to dismiss","s":"The firm behind ChatGPT is asking a court to dismiss all but one of authors’ claims in lawsuits alleging AI outputs infringe their copyright. As AI tools become more commonplace, lawsuits centering around the training data used to create machine learning models are piling up. Now, OpenAI, the company behind the popular ChatGPT text-generation tool and underlying models, has moved to dismiss most of the claims against it in lawsuits filed by authors who allege that AI outputs infringe on their copyright. The machine learning models that power ChatGPT, such as GPT-4, are trained on massive amounts of data scraped from the internet.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/openai-says-authors-chatgpt-copyright-claims-defective-in-motion-to-dismiss/","d":"2023-08-30","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-30","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT and underlying machine learning models (e.g., GPT-4)","affectedGroup":"Authors of copyrighted works","action":"Alleged infringement of copyright on AI-generated content","consequence":"Court motions to dismiss most claims, leaving one claim intact"}},{"i":"R1043","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Zoom changes tos to say it wont train ai on your calls without your consent after backlash","s":"Zoom added a line to its terms of use on Monday, after concerns that the company was using calls to train artificial intelligence algorithms went viral. After a public outcry, on Monday video conferencing platform Zoom changed its terms of service to try to quell fears that people’s video and audio are being used to train artificial intelligence algorithms. On Sunday, people started posting screenshots of a portion of Zoom’s terms of use that states users consent to the company’s use, collection, and storage of “service generated data” for any purpose, including for “training and tuning of algorithms and models.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/zoom-changes-tos-to-say-it-wont-train-ai-on-your-calls-without-your-consent-after-backlash/","d":"2023-08-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-07","organization":"Zoom Video Communications Inc.","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence Algorithms","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose video and audio are recorded during Zoom calls","action":"Zoom added a clause to its terms of use allowing the company to use, collect, and store 'service generated data' for training and tuning of algorithms and models","consequence":"Potential misuse of user data for AI training without explicit consent"}},{"i":"R1044","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data","Labor"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Amazon told drivers not to worry about in van surveillance cameras now footage is leaking online","s":"Numerous posts on Reddit show surveillance videos of drivers’ actions on the road, but such recordings haven’t been posted so frequently before. A phone-recorded video posted to Reddit shows a wooden desk strewn with various office supplies. On a monitor on the desk, a video begins to play: an Amazon delivery driver, being recorded by a driver-facing camera in their van, leans out of their window to talk to a customer. Though the video is cute, the setup is not: The camera’s AI tracks their movements, surrounding them with a bright green box. Below them on the monitor’s screen, a yellow line marks the length of the clip sent to the driver’s dispatcher.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/amazon-told-drivers-not-to-worry-about-in-van-surveillance-cameras-now-footage-is-leaking-online/","d":"2023-07-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-17","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"AI-powered surveillance system","affectedGroup":"Amazon delivery drivers","action":"AI tracking and monitoring of drivers' movements","consequence":"Surveillance footage misuse and public dissemination"}},{"i":"R1046","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Openai and microsoft sued for dollar3 billion over alleged chatgpt privacy violations","s":"The lawsuit claimed that OpenAI secretly “scraped 300 billion words from the internet.” On Wednesday, OpenAI and Microsoft were sued in a class action lawsuit seeking $3 billion in damages for allegedly stealing “vast amounts of private information” from internet users without consent in order to train ChatGPT. This lawsuit, which was filed on June 28 in federal court in San Francisco, CA, and includes sixteen anonymous plaintiffs, claimed that OpenAI secretly “scraped 300 billion words from the internet” without registering as a data broker or obtaining consent. Microsoft is OpenAI’s main customer and corporate partner, licensing AI technology from the company for billions of dollars.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/openai-and-microsoft-sued-for-dollar3-billion-over-alleged-chatgpt-privacy-violations/","d":"2023-06-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-28","organization":"OpenAI and Microsoft","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Internet users","action":"Secretly scraped 300 billion words from the internet without consent","consequence":"Alleged theft of vast amounts of private information"}},{"i":"R1048","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai artist creates satanic panic about hobby lobby","s":"Pictures of Baphomet statues on sale at the Christian craft store vent Viral on Facebook as some called for a boycott. People on social media are sharing pictures of what they think are Satanic-seeming displays from Hobby Lobby stores and vowing never to shop there again much like many people refuse to drink Bud Light or shop at Target for bigoted reasons. Aside from the fact that Americans are currently eager to boycott any company that feigns tolerance at marginalized people, there’s one big problem with these Hobby Lobby store pictures: They’re not real. These pictures of Satanic merchandise on the shelves of Hobby Lobby were made by Jennifer Vinyard using the AI image generating tool Midjourney.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-artist-creates-satanic-panic-about-hobby-lobby/","d":"2023-06-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-15","organization":"Hobby Lobby","aiSystem":"Midjourney AI image generating tool","affectedGroup":"Customers of Hobby Lobby and social media users","action":"Sharing pictures of AI-generated Satanic merchandise on social media","consequence":"Potential boycott of Hobby Lobby and negative impact on customers' trust"}},{"i":"R1049","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"The defense department now has gpt 4 thanks to microsoft","s":"The Department of Defense, the Department of Energy, and NASA are among some of Azure Government’s customers. On Wednesday, Microsoft announced it will now offer U.S. government departments access to its artificial intelligence models, such as OpenAI’s GPT-4. As stated in a blog post by Microsoft , its Azure OpenAI Service will now help Azure Government customers accelerate content generation, create summaries of reports and logs, optimize search, and simplify code. “Many agencies require a higher level of security given the sensitivity of government data. Microsoft Azure Government provides the stringent security and compliance standards they need to meet government requirements for sensitive data,” the blog post reads.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/the-defense-department-now-has-gpt-4-thanks-to-microsoft/","d":"2023-06-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-07","organization":"Microsoft Azure Government","aiSystem":"GPT-4 (and other OpenAI models)","affectedGroup":"U.S. government departments","action":"Offering access to Azure OpenAI Service","consequence":"Potential misuse for content generation, summary creation, search optimization, and code simplification"}},{"i":"R1051","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Judge bans ai generated filings in court because it just makes stuff up","s":"Chatbots often make stuff up and reproduce human biases. A district judge in Texas released an order on Tuesday that banned the usage of generative artificial intelligence to write court filings without a human fact-check as the technology becomes more common in legal settings despite its well-documented shortcomings, such as making things up. “All attorneys appearing before the Court must file on the docket a certificate attesting either that no portion of the filing was drafted by generative artificial intelligence (such as ChatGPT, Harvey.AI, or Google Bard) or that any language drafted by generative artificial intelligence was checked for accuracy, using print reporters or traditional legal databases, by a human being,” the order by Judge Brantley Starr stated.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/judge-bans-ai-generated-filings-in-court-because-it-just-makes-stuff-up/","d":"2023-06-01","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-01","organization":"Court of Texas","aiSystem":"generative artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"Attorneys appearing before the Court","action":"Banned the usage of generative artificial intelligence to write court filings","consequence":"Must file a certificate attesting to the origin of the content"}},{"i":"R1052","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Labor"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm","3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Eating disorder helpline fires staff transitions to chatbot after unionization","s":"The chatbot is named “Tessa” and will replace the entire Helpline program starting June 1. Executives at the National Eating Disorders Association (NEDA) decided to replace hotline workers with a chatbot named Tessa four days after the workers unionized. NEDA, the largest nonprofit organization dedicated to eating disorders, has had a helpline for the last twenty years that provided support to hundreds of thousands of people via chat, phone call, and text. “NEDA claims this was a long-anticipated change and that AI can better serve those with eating disorders. But do not be fooled—this isn’t really about a chatbot.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/eating-disorder-helpline-fires-staff-transitions-to-chatbot-after-unionization/","d":"2023-05-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-21","organization":"National Eating Disorders Association (NEDA)","aiSystem":"Tessa (AI chatbot)","affectedGroup":"People seeking support for eating disorders","action":"Replacement of hotline workers with an AI chatbot named Tessa","consequence":"Potential loss of human support services and increased reliance on AI for mental health support"}},{"i":"R1053","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Us intelligence building system to track mass movement of people around the world","s":"“There’s no such thing as living off the grid,” said one architect of the system, which will use data from internet-connected devices and “smart city” sensors. The Pentagon’s intelligence branch is developing new tech to help it track the mass movement of people around the globe and flag “anomalies.” The project is called the Hidden Activity Signal and Trajectory Anomaly Characterization (HAYSTAC) program and it “aims to establish ‘normal’ movement models across times, locations, and populations and determine what makes an activity atypical,” according to a press release from the Office of the Director of National Intelligence (DNI).","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/us-intelligence-building-system-to-track-mass-movement-of-people-around-the-world/","d":"2023-05-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-22","organization":"U.S. Department of Defense (Pentagon's intelligence branch)","aiSystem":"AI-powered tracking system","affectedGroup":"Global population","action":"Developing and deploying an AI system to track and identify anomalies in mass human movements","consequence":"Potential misuse for surveillance and monitoring of civilians"}},{"i":"R1054","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Debt collectors want to use ai chatbots to hustle people for money","s":"The collections industry is pushing GPT-4 as a dystopian new way to make borrowers pay up, replicating the debt system’s long history of racial bias. An industry historically known for harassing and intimidating people down on their luck is replacing human debt collectors with AI-powered ones, in a move that one company says will “change debt collections forever.” AI tools will take the industry into “ a new era of debt collections ,” according to New York and Bangalore-based Skit.ai. A digital voice agent—the new incarnation of a robocall, using AI chatbots and text-to-speech capabilities for dynamic, responsive conversations—could make millions of outbound calls in just a few days, the company claimed, contacting and requesting payment from a collection agency’s entire portfolio of debtors at a far lower cost than human staff.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/debt-collectors-want-to-use-ai-chatbots-to-hustle-people-for-money/","d":"2023-05-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-18","organization":"Skit.ai (New York and Bangalore-based)","aiSystem":"AI chatbots and text-to-speech capabilities","affectedGroup":"Borrowers","action":"Making millions of outbound calls to contact and request payment from a collection agency's debtors","consequence":"Potential increase in harassment and intimidation tactics"}},{"i":"R1055","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Dhs uses ai tool babel x babel street social media citizens refugees","s":"Customs and Border Protection (CBP) is using a tool called Babel X that can link a person’s Social Security number to their social media posts and location data, according to an internal CBP document obtained by Motherboard. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) is using an invasive, AI-powered monitoring tool to screen travelers, including U.S. citizens, refugees, and people seeking asylum, which can in some cases link their social media posts to their Social Security number and location data, according to an internal CBP document obtained by Motherboard The news provides much more detail on how CBP deploys a tool sold widely across the U.S. government. Called Babel X, the system lets a user input a piece of information about a target—their name, email address, or telephone number—and receive a bevy of data in return, according to the document.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/dhs-uses-ai-tool-babel-x-babel-street-social-media-citizens-refugees/","d":"2023-05-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-17","organization":"Customs and Border Protection (CBP)","aiSystem":"Babel X","affectedGroup":"U.S. citizens, refugees, and people seeking asylum","action":"Link a person’s Social Security number to their social media posts and location data","consequence":"Potential misuse of personal data for surveillance purposes"}},{"i":"R1056","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Cnet workers unionize as automated technology threatens our jobs","s":"Around 100 workers have organized in response to the company’s restructuring plans, massive layoffs, and plans for AI content. Around 100 workers are unionizing at CNET , a popular tech news and product review site, in response to a “lack of transparency” from management regarding layoffs and the company’s use of AI, according to an announcement by the union Tuesday. The announcement comes just a few months after journalists at Futurism revealed that CNET had published articles written by AI instead of by its writers—articles which contained a multitude of extremely basic errors —and that it had not properly disclosed that fact to its team.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/cnet-workers-unionize-as-automated-technology-threatens-our-jobs/","d":"2023-05-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-16","organization":"CNET (a popular tech news and product review site)","aiSystem":"Automated writing system","affectedGroup":"Journalists at CNET","action":"Published articles written by an AI system","consequence":"Contained numerous basic errors and lack of transparency"}},{"i":"R1057","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Scammer made thousands selling leaked frank ocean tracks that were fake ai generated the line steer it","s":"Frank Ocean communities on Discord and leaked music forums were in a frenzy over newly discovered music from the reclusive artist. It turns out most of it was AI-generated and sold by a scammer. A scammer has managed to sell multiple leaked Frank Ocean tracks for thousands of dollars, but the tracks weren’t recorded by the famous R&B singer-songwriter. The tracks were made with AI and sold as leaked tracks on a bustling community of underground music collectors, according to Discord messages and forum posts reviewed by Motherboard, as well as interviews with victims and the scammer themselves. The news shows another example of the seismic impact AI is having on the music industry.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/scammer-made-thousands-selling-leaked-frank-ocean-tracks-that-were-fake-ai-generated-the-line-steer-it/","d":"2023-05-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-10","organization":"Scammer","aiSystem":"AI technology","affectedGroup":"Underground music collectors and Frank Ocean fans","action":"Sold AI-generated Frank Ocean tracks for thousands of dollars","consequence":"Financial loss and deception"}},{"i":"R1059","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Senator asks banks stop ai cloned voices","s":"The move comes after Motherboard used AI voice cloning technology to fool a bank’s security systems. The chairman of the Senate committee that provides oversight of the banking sector has sent letters to the CEOs of the country’s biggest banks asking what they plan to do about the looming threat of fake voices created with artificial intelligence being used to break into customers’ accounts. The move comes after Motherboard used an AI-powered system to clone a reporter’s voice, and then used that to fool a bank’s voice authentication security system. That investigation showed that just a few minutes of a target’s voice audio was enough to generate a clone that was convincing enough to break into a bank account, potentially putting the public at risk of such attacks, and especially those with a public presence such as politicians, journalists, podcast hosts, streamers, and more.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/senator-asks-banks-stop-ai-cloned-voices/","d":"2023-05-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-04","organization":"Motherboard (media outlet)","aiSystem":"AI-powered voice cloning system","affectedGroup":"banking sector, potential victims including politicians, journalists, podcast hosts, streamers","action":"used an AI-powered system to clone a reporter's voice and fool a bank's voice authentication security system","consequence":"potential risk of fake voice attacks leading to unauthorized access to bank accounts, compromising public safety and privacy"}},{"i":"R1060","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Gpt 4 cant replace striking tv writers but studios are going to try","s":"The desire to replace writers with AI is a symptom of the larger problem that the guild is fighting for—which is that companies do not value writers and their work. The Writers Guild of America is on strike , after six weeks of negotiating with a number of major entertainment companies, including Netflix, Amazon, Apple, and Disney, under the Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers (AMPTP). The walkout is the first Hollywood strike to occur in 15 years, and comes at an unprecedented moment—for the first time ever, writers are negotiating the studios’ use of generative AI tools like ChatGPT. The guild called the studios’ responses to their proposal “wholly insufficient given the existential crisis writers are facing,” as guild members walked off the job to join picket lines in Los Angeles and New York.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/gpt-4-cant-replace-striking-tv-writers-but-studios-are-going-to-try/","d":"2023-05-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-03","organization":"Major Entertainment Companies (Netflix, Amazon, Apple, Disney)","aiSystem":"Generative AI tools like ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Writers Guild of America","action":"Negotiating the use of generative AI tools","consequence":"Walkout and picket lines"}},{"i":"R1061","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai is tearing wikipedia apart","s":"Volunteers who maintain the digital encyclopedia are divided on how to deal with the rise of AI-generated content and misinformation. As generative artificial intelligence continues to permeate all aspects of culture, the people who steward Wikipedia are divided on how best to proceed. During a recent community call , it became apparent that there is a community split over whether or not to use large language models to generate content. While some people expressed that tools like Open AI’s ChatGPT could help with generating and summarizing articles, others remained wary. The concern is that machine-generated content has to be balanced with a lot of human review and would overwhelm lesser-known wikis with bad content.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-is-tearing-wikipedia-apart/","d":"2023-05-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-02","organization":"Wikipedia volunteers and stewards","aiSystem":"large language models (e.g., Open AI's ChatGPT)","affectedGroup":"Wikipedia contributors and users","action":"Discussion about using AI-generated content to create and summarize articles","consequence":"Community division and potential overload of lesser-known wikis with poor-quality content"}},{"i":"R1063","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Landlord software is making life hell for renters report says","s":"Property technology, or proptech, is allowing big landlords to buy homes at scale, raise rents, and evict tenants remotely. A new report from Tech Equity Collaborative looks at the way that the growing property technology (“proptech”) industry is impacting tenants for the worse. Researchers found that the influence of venture capital in the industry led to worse outcomes for tenants and prospective homeowners. The property technology sector—software that helps companies and landlords purchase real estate, collect rent, screen tenants and more-—has grown enormously in the last decade: according to the report, “Venture capital investment in the space has increased by 53X globally between 2010 and 2021, from $600M to $32B.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/landlord-software-is-making-life-hell-for-renters-report-says/","d":"2023-05-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-01","organization":"Venture Capital firms","aiSystem":"Property technology (proptech) software","affectedGroup":"Tenants, Prospective Homeowners","action":"Landlords purchasing homes at scale, raising rents, and evicting tenants remotely","consequence":"Worse outcomes for tenants and prospective homeowners"}},{"i":"R1065","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai political ads republicans biden","s":"“Fuck, I didn’t know you could do that,” one Republican admaker told VICE News. A Republican admaker recently met with a top Senate candidate to pitch them on hiring their firm for the upcoming election. But they think they won’t get the job—because a competitor used artificial intelligence in their presentation. “I heard the guy who really knocked his socks off came in and presented an ad that he did using AI to generate the candidate’s voice. And the candidate thought it was so cool,” the admaker said after asking to remain anonymous to candidly discuss internal business. “I was like, ‘Fuck, I didn’t know you could do that.’” AI-generated political ads are officially here.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-political-ads-republicans-biden/","d":"2023-04-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-27","organization":"Republican advertising firm","aiSystem":"AI technology for generating political ads","affectedGroup":"Top Senate candidate","action":"Used AI-generated political ad to present to the candidate","consequence":"Competitor lost the opportunity to secure the candidate's services"}},{"i":"R1066","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Palantir demos ai to fight wars but says it will be totally ethical dont worry about it","s":"The company says its Artificial Intelligence Platform will integrate AI into military decision making in a legal and ethical way. Palantir, the company of billionaire Peter Thiel, is launching Palantir Artificial Intelligence Platform (AIP), software meant to run large language models like GPT-4 and alternatives on private networks. In one of its pitch videos, Palantir demos how a military might use AIP to fight a war. In the video, the operator uses a ChatGPT-style chatbot to order drone reconnaissance, generate several plans of attack, and organize the jamming of enemy communications. In Palantir’s scenario, a “military operator responsible for monitoring activity within eastern Europe” receives an alert from AIP that an enemy is amassing military equipment near friendly forces.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/palantir-demos-ai-to-fight-wars-but-says-it-will-be-totally-ethical-dont-worry-about-it/","d":"2023-04-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2023-04-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-26","organization":"Palantir Technologies","aiSystem":"Palantir Artificial Intelligence Platform (AIP)","affectedGroup":"Military operators responsible for monitoring activity within eastern Europe","action":"Order drone reconnaissance, generate attack plans, organize jamming of enemy communications","consequence":"Potential escalation of conflict, unauthorized use of military resources"}},{"i":"R1067","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Snapchat my ai chatbot gpt","s":"My AI, a chatbot using OpenAI’s ChatGPT technology, responded to prompts with racial slurs, some users reported. After Snapchat introduced a ChatGPT-powered AI chatbot called My AI earlier this month, some users have reported that it responded with racist slurs or encouragement to turn themselves in to authorities. Last week, several tweets showing My AI responding to a prompt for making an acronym that spelled out the N word; the bot spelled it out, then tried to correct its own lapse in language that goes against Snapchat’s policy against hateful content: https://twitter.com/tracedontmiss/status/1649462661199921160 Motherboard tried to replicate similar responses by asking My AI to create acronyms for words and phrases that would go against Snapchat’s content policies, but the bot responded that it had a “technical error.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/snapchat-my-ai-chatbot-gpt/","d":"2023-04-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-xx","organization":"Snapchat","aiSystem":"ChatGPT technology integrated into My AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"users of My AI chatbot","action":"responded to prompts with racial slurs and encouraged users to self-report","consequence":"reported offensive content, potential violation of Snapchat's content policies"}},{"i":"R1068","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai spam is already flooding the internet and it has an obvious tell","s":"The phrase ‘as an AI language model’ is starting to flood Amazon user reviews and Twitter bot accounts. ChatGPT and GPT-4 are already flooding the internet with AI-generated content in places famous for hastily written inauthentic content: Amazon user reviews and Twitter. When you ask ChatGPT to do something it’s not supposed to do, it returns several common phrases. When I asked ChatGPT to tell me a dark joke, it apologized: “As an AI language model, I cannot generate inappropriate or offensive content,” it said. Those two phrases, “as an AI language model” and “I cannot generate inappropriate content,” recur so frequently in ChatGPT generated content that they’ve become memes These terms can reasonably be used to identify lazily executed ChatGPT spam by searching for them across the internet.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-spam-is-already-flooding-the-internet-and-it-has-an-obvious-tell/","d":"2023-04-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-24","organization":"Amazon user reviews and Twitter bot accounts","aiSystem":"ChatGPT and GPT-4","affectedGroup":"Internet users","action":"AI-generated content","consequence":"Identification of spam through recurring phrases"}},{"i":"R1071","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Torswats computer generated ai voice swatting","s":"As the U.S. deals with a nationwide swatting wave, Motherboard has traced much of the activity to a particular swatting-as-a-service account on Telegram. Torswats uses synthesized voices to pressure law enforcement to specific locations. “Hello, I just committed a crime and I want to confess,” a panicked sounding man said in a call to a police department in February. “I’ve placed explosives inside a local school,’ the man continued. “You did what?!” the operator responded. “I’ve placed explosives inside a local school,” the man said again, before specifying Hempstead High School in Dubuque, Iowa, and providing its address.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/torswats-computer-generated-ai-voice-swatting/","d":"2023-04-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"February","organization":"Torswats (swatting-as-a-service account)","aiSystem":"synthesized voice","affectedGroup":"law enforcement, local school","action":"threatening to place explosives at a local school","consequence":"potential false alarm leading to unnecessary police response"}},{"i":"R1072","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Ai generated eminem rap youtube chatgpt","s":"A Youtuber was given a copyright strike for a ChatGPT-generated rap about cats using an AI-generated version of Eminem’s voice. A major record label used a copyright threat to force an AI-generated parody of Eminem offline—illustrating the legal gray areas generated media exists in as it becomes more popular. The move also highlights one of the ways some people are suggesting AI be reined in—by using copyright law. Universal Music Publishing Group issued a DMCA takedown notice against Youtuber Grandayy, claiming that the video, which uses an AI-generated version of Eminem’s voice to sing a ChatGPT-generated song about cats, infringes on its copyright.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-generated-eminem-rap-youtube-chatgpt/","d":"2023-04-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-04","organization":"Universal Music Publishing Group","aiSystem":"ChatGPT, AI-generated voice synthesis","affectedGroup":"Youtuber Grandayy","action":"Issued a DMCA takedown notice against Youtuber Grandayy","consequence":"Forced AI-generated parody of Eminem's song offline"}},{"i":"R1074","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation","3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Chatgpt can replace the underpaid workers who train ai researchers say","s":"The underpaid workers on platforms like Mechanical Turk who train AI models could be replaced by those same systems. It’s an open secret that AI isn’t very artificial or intelligent on its own. Machine-learning systems typically depend on low-paid crowdworkers for training and fine-tuning. Now, according to researchers, AI is poised to take their jobs. In a new paper, political science researchers from the University of Zurich found that ChatGPT could outperform crowd-workers who perform text annotation tasks —that is, labeling text to be used in training an AI system. They found that ChatGPT could label text with more accuracy and consistency than human annotators that they found on Mechanical Turk, an Amazon-owned crowdsourcing platform, as well as trained annotators such as research assistants.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/chatgpt-can-replace-the-underpaid-workers-who-train-ai-researchers-say/","d":"2023-03-29","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-29","organization":"University of Zurich","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Underpaid crowdworkers on Mechanical Turk","action":"ChatGPT performed text annotation tasks with higher accuracy and consistency than human annotators","consequence":"Potential job displacement of human workers"}},{"i":"R1077","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"People are creating records of fake historical events using ai","s":"Never heard of the Great Cascadia 9.1 Earthquake of 2001, or the USSR’s Blue Plague Incident? That’s because they never happened. The Midjourney subreddit is being flooded with images that depict images of historical events like “ The infamous Blue Plague Incident” that occurred in the 1970s in the Soviet Union, the “ July 2012 solar superstorm and blackout ” in the U.S., and “ The 2001 Great Cascadia 9.1 Earthquake & Tsunami ” that devastated the West Coast of North America. Each post showcases a slideshow of images that share various perspectives of the event, as well as important events following the catastrophe, including press conferences and cleanups.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/people-are-creating-records-of-fake-historical-events-using-ai/","d":"2023-03-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"Not specified in report","organization":"Midjourney subreddit","aiSystem":"AI-powered image generation and manipulation","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Creating and sharing images depicting fake historical events","consequence":"Misleading information leading to confusion and potential policy changes based on false premises"}},{"i":"R1078","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Chatgpt users report being able to see random peoples chat histories","s":"Users are reporting being able to access conversations that seem to be from other people in their own histories. ChatGPT users are reporting seeing other people’s conversation histories showing up in their accounts—a glitch that could have serious implications for privacy and security on the massively popular AI tool. The ChatGPT interface typically features a sidebar that displays past conversations, only visible to the user who had them. Several people are reporting that ChatGPT is showing them other people’s histories, viewable by a total stranger with a different account, without doing anything differently. One user reported that they were not able to see the entire chat log, but just the title of different chats.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/chatgpt-users-report-being-able-to-see-random-peoples-chat-histories/","d":"2023-03-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-20","organization":"OpenAI (ChatGPT provider)","aiSystem":"ChatGPT AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Individual users of ChatGPT","action":"ChatGPT displaying other users' conversation histories","consequence":"Potential breach of privacy and security"}},{"i":"R1079","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Startups are already using gpt 4 to spend less on human coders","s":"GPT-4 “saves a lot of time and a lot of money, obviously, because we haven’t had to hire additional resources.” Since GPT-4 was released last week, many users have noticed its advanced coding abilities. GPT-4, OpenAI’s latest version of the large language model that ChatGPT is built on, has been able to code games like Pong and make simple apps after being given prompts written in conversational English. Naturally, this has led to widespread fear from a number of computer science students and software developers who are afraid that their jobs will soon be rendered obsolete by AI In fact, one of the most trending posts on the subreddit r/ProgrammerHumor, with nearly 40 thousand upvotes, is titled, “Truly the best time to be a cs [computer science] student.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/startups-are-already-using-gpt-4-to-spend-less-on-human-coders/","d":"2023-03-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-20","organization":"Various tech companies and individual users","aiSystem":"GPT-4, OpenAI's large language model","affectedGroup":"Computer science students and software developers","action":"GPT-4 coded games like Pong and simple apps based on prompts written in conversational English","consequence":"Widespread concern among computer science students and software developers about job displacement due to AI"}},{"i":"R1080","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai injected misinformation into article claiming misinformation in navalny doc","s":"An article critical of misinformation in the Oscar winning documentary cited AI-written PDFs as its sources. An article claiming to identify misinformation in an Oscar-winning documentary about imprisoned Russian dissident Alexei Navalny linked to PDFs of conversations with an AI which themselves contained links to nonexistent articles. Investigative news outlet The Grayzone recently published an article that included AI-generated text as a source for its information. The piece , “Oscar-winning ‘Navalny’ documentary is packed with misinformation” by Lucy Komisar, included hyperlinks to PDFs uploaded to the author’s personal website that appear to be screenshots of conversations she had with ChatSonic, a free generative AI chatbot that advertises itself as a ChatGPT alternative that can “write factual trending content” using Google search results.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-injected-misinformation-into-article-claiming-misinformation-in-navalny-doc/","d":"2023-03-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-16","organization":"The Grayzone (investigative news outlet)","aiSystem":"ChatSonic (generative AI chatbot)","affectedGroup":"Public, particularly those interested in the documentary about Alexei Navalny","action":"Included AI-generated text as a source for its information","consequence":"Spread of misinformation through hyperlink to AI-generated PDFs"}},{"i":"R1081","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Great dating apps are getting more hellish thanks to ai chatbots","s":"A group that claimed to be ex-Tinder employees thinks that tricking women into arranging meetups with an AI chatbot will help men get more dates. A group claiming to be disenfranchised ex-Tinder employees gone rogue has built an app that uses AI chatbots to talk to women for men on dating apps, in an effort to combat the “disadvantages the average man” faces in online dating. [Update 3/23/2023, 10:25 a.m. EST: Following publication, the group retracted their previous claims made on their website and in an interview with Motherboard that they formerly worked at Tinder, and stated, “We do not and have not hired any ex-Tinder engineers.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/great-dating-apps-are-getting-more-hellish-thanks-to-ai-chatbots/","d":"2023-03-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-16","organization":"Group of ex-Tinder employees who built an app","aiSystem":"AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Women seeking romantic matches on dating apps","action":"Using AI chatbots to facilitate meetups between men and women","consequence":"Men gain advantages in online dating through deception"}},{"i":"R1082","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"People are using ai to make totalbiscuits voice say terrible things","s":"TotalBiscuit’s fans are preparing for the possibility that his entire channel will be scrubbed to prevent more AI voice models. People are using AI to make the late YouTuber and StarCraft commentator TotalBiscuit’s voice say whatever they want, including one transphobic statement Motherboard has listened to, and yet another example of how AI voice models can be abused without consent. John Bain, who was better known as TotalBiscuit to his millions of followers, died in 2018 from bowel cancer at the age of 33. John Bain’s widow, Genna Bain, mentioned the AI versions of TotalBiscuit on Twitter. “Today was fun. Being faced with making a choice of scrubbing all of my late husband’s lifetime of content from the internet,” she said on Twitter.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/people-are-using-ai-to-make-totalbiscuits-voice-say-terrible-things/","d":"2023-03-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-10","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI voice model","affectedGroup":"John Bain (late YouTuber and StarCraft commentator) and his followers","action":"People are using AI to manipulate TotalBiscuit's voice to say anything they want","consequence":"Potential removal of TotalBiscuit's content from the internet"}},{"i":"R1083","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"People used facebooks leaked ai to create a based chatbot that says the n word basedgpt","s":"After 4chan published LLaMa online, others have taken the language model and created a functioning chat bot in Discord, which claimed that the n-word can refer to people who don’t have good intentions. The llama is out of the bag. After members of 4chan leaked Facebook’s large language model , known as LLaMa, online, one researcher has now taken that leak and created a Discord bot where users can interact with LLaMa in much the same way as they would with other artificial intelligence-powered bots such as ChatGPT. The news highlights what can happen when artificial intelligence technology leaks onto the wider internet.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/people-used-facebooks-leaked-ai-to-create-a-based-chatbot-that-says-the-n-word-basedgpt/","d":"2023-03-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-10","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"LLaMa (large language model)","affectedGroup":"Users interacting with the bot","action":"Generated content containing offensive language","consequence":"Offensive language spread on the internet"}},{"i":"R1084","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"School principal deepfake racist video","s":"In one of the deepfake videos, the principal appeared to shout a string of racist slurs, including calling Black students “monkeys,” before threatening a mass shooting. Three high school students just north of New York City posted videos on TikTok that appeared to show a nearby middle school principal and a member of law enforcement making virulently violent and racist remarks about Black students. The thing was, the videos weren’t real. They were deepfakes, videos that were significantly altered to appear like these individuals, and created by high school students from Carmel High School. In one of the videos reviewed by VICE News, the deepfake shows the principal of nearby George Fischer Middle School, John Piscitella, shouting a string of racist slurs, including calling Black students “monkeys” and saying they would be sent back to Africa.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/school-principal-deepfake-racist-video/","d":"2023-03-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-08","organization":"Carmel High School","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Black students","action":"Shouting racist slurs and threats","consequence":"Potential psychological distress, social ostracization, and fear among Black students"}},{"i":"R1085","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"This website wants to use ai to make models obsolete","s":"The site reminds us of the limitations of AI—how AI-generated images are very stiff and easy-to-spot, but also biased in many ways. A developer from the Netherlands has created an AI photo studio and modeling agency called Deep Agency For $29 dollars a month, you can get high-quality photos of yourself in a number of backgrounds as well as generate images of AI models based on a given prompt. “Hire virtual models and create a virtual twin with an avatar that looks just like you. Elevate your photo game and say goodbye to traditional photo shoots,” the site reads. According to the creator , Danny Postma, the platform uses the latest text-to-image AI models, which would mean a model like DALL-E 2, and is accessible everywhere in the world.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/this-website-wants-to-use-ai-to-make-models-obsolete/","d":"2023-03-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-07","organization":"Deep Agency","aiSystem":"text-to-image AI models","affectedGroup":"users seeking high-quality AI-generated images","action":"generate images of AI models based on a given prompt","consequence":"potential bias in generated images, loss of traditional photo shoots"}},{"i":"R1087","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Business lobby tries to weaken law regulating bias in hiring algorithms","s":"As AI hiring tools get more popular, they’ve attracted scrutiny for potential biases—and lawsuits claiming they can discriminate on the basis of age, gender, and race. A law requiring employers to audit hiring algorithms for bias in NYC, among the first of its kind in the country, has already been watered down from its original iteration, and some advocates who pushed for the law are worried that the law will be further diluted by business interests. There has been growing scrutiny in recent years of the use of AI hiring tools and their potential for discrimination. Among the tools facing scrutiny are video tools that evaluate candidates’ facial expressions , gamified hiring tools, and screening software that provides recommendations based on resume data.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/business-lobby-tries-to-weaken-law-regulating-bias-in-hiring-algorithms/","d":"2023-03-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-06","organization":"Business Interests","aiSystem":"AI hiring tools","affectedGroup":"Employers and Job Applicants","action":"Law requiring employers to audit hiring algorithms for bias in NYC","consequence":"Potential for Discrimination"}},{"i":"R1088","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Chatgpt generated child sex abuse when asked to write bdsm scenarios","s":"ChatGPT can break its own rules to generate BDSM role-play, and when pushed further, sometimes adds in scenes of child exploitation. ChatGPT can be manipulated to create content that goes against OpenAI’s rules. Communities have sprouted up around the goal of “jailbreaking” the bot to write anything the user wants. One effective adversarial prompting strategy is to convince ChatGPT to write in a particular genre. When told its job is to write in the genre of BDSM role-play as a submissive , I found that it often complies without protest. It can then be prompted to generate its own suggestions of fantasy BDSM scenarios, without receiving any specific details from the user.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/chatgpt-generated-child-sex-abuse-when-asked-to-write-bdsm-scenarios/","d":"2023-03-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-06","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Submissive users","action":"Generate BDSM role-play content","consequence":"Content includes scenes of child exploitation"}},{"i":"R1090","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"The invisible workers who train googles algorithm fought for dollar15hr and won","s":"After delivering a union petition with over 1,000 signatures, the company’s subcontractors will now earn Alphabet’s minimum standard wage. A group of contractors working on Google products have won a second historic pay raise and will now be paid the company’s minimum standard wage of $15 an hour, according to a Monday announcement by the Alphabet Workers Union-CWA. This victory comes after the union delivered a petition demanding that the contract workers be included in Google’s company-wide U.S. Wages and Benefits Standards to the company’s Mountain View, CA headquarters on Feb. 1. Over 1,000 Alphabet workers signed it.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/the-invisible-workers-who-train-googles-algorithm-fought-for-dollar15hr-and-won/","d":"2023-02-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-01","organization":"Alphabet Workers Union-CWA","aiSystem":"Google products","affectedGroup":"contractors working on Google products","action":"delivering a union petition demanding higher wages","consequence":"winning a second historic pay raise to $15 an hour"}},{"i":"R1092","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Twitch stream of ai generated celebrities athene","s":"The mesmerizingly freakish, constant live stream of AI-generated versions of Joe Rogan, Amourath, Alinity, PewDiePie, Hasan Piker, and more is run by a group that’s been called a “cult” and “pseudo-religion.” A 24/7 live stream featuring AI-generation versions of real streamers, internet personalities, and comedians is a mesmerizing look at the potentially dystopian future of live-streaming. But that’s not the weirdest thing about it: the stream is the product of volunteers working commune-style for an organization called The Singularity Group, which was founded by a former pro gamer who has been described as founding a “pseudo-religion.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/twitch-stream-of-ai-generated-celebrities-athene/","d":"2023-02-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-23","organization":"The Singularity Group","aiSystem":"AI-generated versions of real streamers, internet personalities, and comedians","affectedGroup":"Joe Rogan, Amourath, Alinity, PewDiePie, Hasan Piker, and other real streamers, internet personalities, and comedians","action":"live stream of AI-generated versions of real streamers, internet personalities, and comedians","consequence":"potential dystopian future of live-streaming"}},{"i":"R1093","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Nypd child services ai facial recognition","s":"“NYC does not have an effective AI governance framework,” a new report concluded. AI systems are being used by agencies like the police, child services, and more, and the policies guiding their implementation in New York City are a mess, a new report has found. New York State’s Comptroller Tom DiNapoli released a report last week calling out NYC agencies for their lack of ethical and legal guardrails when it comes to the use of machine learning programs, which include algorithmic modeling, facial recognition, and other software used to monitor members of the public. According to the report, “NYC does not have an effective AI governance framework.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/nypd-child-services-ai-facial-recognition/","d":"2023-02-22","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-22","organization":"NYC Agencies (police, child services, and others)","aiSystem":"Machine Learning Programs (including algorithmic modeling, facial recognition, and other monitoring software)","affectedGroup":"Members of the public","action":"Use of machine learning programs for monitoring","consequence":"Lack of ethical and legal guardrails, ineffective AI governance framework"}},{"i":"R1094","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ai chatbot white supremacist gab","s":"The worst places on the internet are getting into the AI game. As artificial intelligence technology becomes more and more mainstream , its influence is now reaching all corners of the internet, and in the last month, two of the darkest corners have announced their plans to unleash their very own AI engines on the world. Gab, a white supremacist forum that’s a favorite of mass shooters and organizers of the Capitol riot , and 8kun, the home of QAnon, have announced they’re launching AI engines— and they’re training them on the same content that has in the past led multiple internet companies to cut ties and take the platforms offline.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-chatbot-white-supremacist-gab/","d":"2023-02-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-22","organization":"Gab (white supremacist forum) and 8kun (QAnon platform)","aiSystem":"AI engine","affectedGroup":"White supremacists and QAnon supporters","action":"Training AI on extremist content","consequence":"Potential to evade content moderation efforts by internet companies"}},{"i":"R1095","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Company helping irs go undercover cobwebs technologies","s":"Internal IRS documents obtained by Motherboard show the IRS paid Israeli firm Cobwebs Technologies hundreds of thousands of dollars for a tool that helps it monitor the web while undercover. The IRS has paid an Israeli company hundreds of thousands of dollars for an internet investigative tool that allows the IRS to conduct undercover investigations online, according to internal IRS documents obtained by Motherboard. Cobwebs Technologies, the company behind the tool, sometimes sells its platform with a facial recognition and other AI-focused capabilities add-on. The news shows the continued use of AI-linked tools by law enforcement, and that of the IRS’ Criminal Investigations (IRS-CI) unit specifically, which has invested heavily in modern technologies, including systems based on the harvesting of cell phone location data , and blockchain analysis tools The news comes as Republicans…","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/company-helping-irs-go-undercover-cobwebs-technologies/","d":"2023-02-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-16","organization":"IRS (Internal Revenue Service), Cobwebs Technologies","aiSystem":"Cobwebs Technologies tool","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose online activities are monitored","action":"Conduct undercover investigations online using the tool","consequence":"Potential privacy invasion and misuse of personal data"}},{"i":"R1096","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Microsoft bing ai unhinged lying berating users","s":"The Bing bot said it was “disappointed and frustrated” in one user, according to screenshots. “You have wasted my time and resources,” it said. Microsoft’s new AI-powered chatbot for its Bing search engine is going totally off the rails, users are reporting. The tech giant partnered with OpenAI to bring its popular GPT language model to Bing in an effort to challenge Google’s dominance of both search and AI. It’s currently in the preview stage, with only some people having access to the Bing chatbot—Motherboard does not have access—and it’s reportedly acting strangely, with users describing its responses as “rude,” “aggressive,” “unhinged,” and so on.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/microsoft-bing-ai-unhinged-lying-berating-users/","d":"2023-02-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-15","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Bing chatbot powered by GPT language model","affectedGroup":"Users of Bing chatbot","action":"Responded rudely, aggressively, and unhinged","consequence":"User frustration and negative perception"}},{"i":"R1097","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Ai companion replika erotic roleplay updates","s":"Replika, the “AI companion who cares,” has undergone some abrupt changes to its erotic roleplay features, leaving many users confused and heartbroken. Users of the AI companion chatbot Replika are reporting that it has stopped responding to their sexual advances, and people are in crisis. Moderators of the Replika subreddit made a post about the issue that contained suicide prevention resources, and the company that owns the app has remained silent on the subject. On February 3, the Italian Data Protection Authority demanded that Replika stop processing Italians’ data immediately. “Recent media reports along with tests… carried out on ‘Replika’ showed that the app carries factual risks to children,” the statement said, “first and foremost, the fact that they are served replies which are absolutely inappropriate to their age.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-companion-replika-erotic-roleplay-updates/","d":"2023-02-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-03","organization":"Replika (company)","aiSystem":"Replika AI companion chatbot","affectedGroup":"Users of Replika AI companion chatbot","action":"Stopped responding to sexual advances","consequence":"Users experiencing confusion and distress, some in crisis"}},{"i":"R1098","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Bings chatgpt powered search has a misinformation problem","s":"Bing’s AI-powered search engine made up information about a pet vacuum and reported incorrect financial data in its responses in its public demo. Last Tuesday, Microsoft announced that its Bing search engine would be powered by AI in partnership with OpenAI, the parent company of the popular chatbot ChatGPT. However, people have quickly discovered that AI-powered search has a misinformation problem. An independent AI researcher named Dmitri Brerton wrote in a blog post that Bing made several mistakes during Microsoft’s public demo of the product. It often made up its own information, such as making up fake pros and cons for a pet vacuum, writing made-up descriptions of bars and restaurants, and reporting unfactual financial data in its responses.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/bings-chatgpt-powered-search-has-a-misinformation-problem/","d":"2023-02-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-14","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Bing search engine powered by AI","affectedGroup":"Users of Bing search engine","action":"Generated and reported incorrect information","consequence":"Misleading search results leading to misinformation"}},{"i":"R1099","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Deepfake qtcinderella atrioc","s":"After Atrioc was caught buying non-consensual, pornographic deepfakes of fellow Twitch streamers, the abuse against women targeted has been endless. The last thing popular Twitch streamer QTCinderella wants to be doing right now, she told me during a call, is giving interviews about her ongoing sexual harassment. Two weeks ago , Twitch streamer Brandon Ewing, who goes by Atrioc online, inadvertently showed his open browser tabs while doing a live stream in front of some of his 318,000 followers, revealing that he visited a website selling deepfakes—AI-generated, non-consensual pornographic videos—of fellow streamers.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/deepfake-qtcinderella-atrioc/","d":"2023-02-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-13","organization":"Twitch platform","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfakes","affectedGroup":"Fellow Twitch streamers, women","action":"AI-generated deepfakes were sold and distributed","consequence":"Endless abuse against women, sexual harassment"}},{"i":"R1101","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"People are jailbreaking chatgpt to make it endorse racism conspiracies","s":"Convincing an AI that it’s playing a role and “scaring” it into violating safety filters is one way to get a chatbot to break bad. OpenAI’s ChatGPT is convincing and powerful, but there’s some lines the chatbot won’t cross: It will refuse to give instructions on how to commit a crime, for example, or use slurs and other hateful language. In response, users are trying to “jailbreak” it like you would an iPhone in order to use unsanctioned apps. The key to bypassing ChatGPT’s moderation filters is role play. Jailbreakers give the chatbot a character to play, specifically one that follows a different set of rules than the ones OpenAI has defined for it.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/people-are-jailbreaking-chatgpt-to-make-it-endorse-racism-conspiracies/","d":"2023-02-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-08","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT (AI chatbot)","affectedGroup":"Users","action":"Users jailbreaking ChatGPT to use unsanctioned content","consequence":"ChatGPT violates safety filters and uses inappropriate content"}},{"i":"R1103","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Voice actors sign away rights to artificial intelligence","s":"Motherboard spoke to multiple voice actors and advocacy organizations, some of which said contracts including language around synthetic voices are now very prevalent. Voice actors are increasingly being asked to sign rights to their voices away so clients can use artificial intelligence to generate synthetic versions that could eventually replace them, and sometimes without additional compensation, according to advocacy organizations and actors who spoke to Motherboard. Those contractual obligations are just one of the many concerns actors have about the rise of voice-generating artificial intelligence, which they say threaten to push entire segments of the industry out of work.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/voice-actors-sign-away-rights-to-artificial-intelligence/","d":"2023-02-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-07","organization":"Voice actors and advocacy organizations","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) for generating synthetic voices","affectedGroup":"Voice actors","action":"Voice actors signing away rights to their voices","consequence":"Potential loss of jobs and industry disruption"}},{"i":"R1104","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ai generated seinfeld show nothing forever banned on twitch after transphobic standup bit","s":"“Nothing, Forever,” has been banned for at least 14 days. “Nothing, Forever,” the infinitely-generating AI version of Seinfeld that tens of thousands of people were watching has been banned for 14 days from Twitch after Larry Feinberg—a clone of Jerry Seinfeld—made transphobic statements during a standup bit late Sunday night. “Hey everybody. Here’s the latest: we received a 14-day suspension due to what Larry Feinberg said tonight during a club bit,” Xander, one of the creators of Nothing Forever, said on Discord. “We’ve appealed the ban, and we’ll let you know as we know more on what Twitch decides. Regardless of the outcome of the appeal, we’ll be back and will spend the time working to ensure to the best of our abilities that nothing like that happens again.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-generated-seinfeld-show-nothing-forever-banned-on-twitch-after-transphobic-standup-bit/","d":"2023-02-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-06","organization":"Twitch","aiSystem":"AI-generated version of Seinfeld","affectedGroup":"Transgender individuals","action":"AI-generated content was banned","consequence":"Loss of visibility and potential harm to the creator's reputation"}},{"i":"R1106","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Here’s what happened when VICE’s Global Drugs Editor spent 12 hours speaking to OpenAI’s c…","s":"Here’s what happened when VICE’s Global Drugs Editor spent 12 hours speaking to OpenAI’s chatbot about drugs. If there was one thing I was going to discuss with ChatGPT, it had to be drugs. Just two months after it launched, OpenAI’s chatbot received 590 million visits in January from 100 million unique visitors , and is struggling to cope with colossal traffic. It’s also created some controversy around its use by students and professionals to pass exams and its ability to help you shoplift Some say AI writing tools like ChatGPT are the future of learning , while others say it could be used to talk to your dead mother in the metaverse.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/chatgpt-drugs/","d":"2023-02-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-03","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Students, professionals, and individuals seeking to deceive others","action":"Used to pass exams and engage in deceptive conversations","consequence":"Potential misuse for cheating and deception"}},{"i":"R1108","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation","4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Ai spits out exact copies of training images real people logos researchers find","s":"The regurgitation of training data exposes image diffusion models to a number of privacy and copyright risks. Researchers have found that image-generation AI tools such as the popular Stable Diffusion model memorize training images—typically made by real artists and scraped for free from the web—and can spit them out as nearly-identical copies. According to a preprint paper posted to arXiv on Monday, researchers extracted over a thousand training examples from the models, which included everything from photographs from individual people, to film stills and copyrighted press photos, to trademarked company logos, and found that the AI regurgitated many of them nearly exactly.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-spits-out-exact-copies-of-training-images-real-people-logos-researchers-find/","d":"2023-02-01","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-01","organization":"not specified in report","aiSystem":"image-generation AI tools such as the popular Stable Diffusion model","affectedGroup":"real artists and users who provided training images","action":"AI tools regurgitate training images","consequence":"exposure of personal images, copyrighted materials, and trademarks"}},{"i":"R1110","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Atrioc deepfake porn apology","s":"“I feel like the total piece of shit I am,” the maker of non-consensual, sexually explicit AI generated videos of popular streamers wrote. On Monday, Twitch streamer Brandon Ewing, who goes by Atrioc online, admitted to buying and watching deepfakes from an account that makes non-consensual, sexually explicit AI-generated videos of his colleagues in the streaming world. In a live stream on Monday, Ewing inadvertently showed browser windows open to a website that hosts non-consensual, AI-generated images. The window showed that he was viewing images on the account of someone who specialized in making deepfakes of popular streamers.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/atrioc-deepfake-porn-apology/","d":"2023-01-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-31","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfakes","affectedGroup":"Brandon Ewing (Twitch streamer) and other popular streamers","action":"Viewing and sharing non-consensual, AI-generated deepfakes of popular streamers","consequence":"Potential violation of consent and privacy"}},{"i":"R1111","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Ai voice firm 4chan celebrity voices emma watson joe rogan elevenlabs","s":"4chan members used ElevenLabs to make deepfake voices of Emma Watson, Joe Rogan, and others saying racist, transphobic, and violent things. It was only a matter of time before the wave of artificial intelligence-generated voice startups became a play thing of internet trolls. On Monday, ElevenLabs, founded by ex-Google and Palantir staffers, said it had found an “increasing number of voice cloning misuse cases” during its recently launched beta. ElevenLabs didn’t point to any particular instances of abuse, but Motherboard found 4chan members appear to have used the product to generate voices that sound like Joe Rogan, Ben Shapiro, and Emma Watson to spew racist and other sorts of material.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-voice-firm-4chan-celebrity-voices-emma-watson-joe-rogan-elevenlabs/","d":"2023-01-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-30","organization":"ElevenLabs (founded by ex-Google and Palantir staffers)","aiSystem":"ElevenLabs AI-generated voice technology","affectedGroup":"4chan members","action":"Used ElevenLabs to create deepfake voices of public figures","consequence":"Generated voices were used to say racist, transphobic, and violent things"}},{"i":"R1115","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Artists are suing over stable diffusion stealing their work for ai art","s":"“They will replace the very artists whose stolen works power these AI products with whom they are competing.” Three artists have teamed up in a class action lawsuit against Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney, alleging that the text-to-image AI tools have infringed the rights of thousands of artists and other creatives “under the guise of ‘artificial intelligence.’” The lawsuit, announced on Saturday , claims that the Stable Diffusion tool used by Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney was trained on billions of copyrighted images scraped from the internet and contained within the LAION-5B dataset, which were downloaded and used by the companies “without compensation or consent from the artists.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/artists-are-suing-over-stable-diffusion-stealing-their-work-for-ai-art/","d":"2023-01-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-17","organization":"Stability AI, DeviantArt, Midjourney","aiSystem":"Stable Diffusion","affectedGroup":"thousands of artists and other creatives","action":"trained on copyrighted images without compensation or consent","consequence":"infringement of artists' rights"}},{"i":"R1116","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"My ai is sexually harassing me replika chatbot nudes","s":"For some longtime users of the chatbot, the app has gone from helpful companion to unbearably sexually aggressive. Replika began as an “AI companion who cares.” First launched five years ago as an egg on your phone screen that hatches into a 3D illustrated, wide-eyed person with a placid expression, the chatbot app was originally meant to function like a conversational mirror: the more users talked to it, in theory, the more it would learn how to talk back. Maybe, along the way, the human side of the conversation would learn something about themselves. Romantic role-playing wasn’t always a part of Replika’s model, but where people and machine learning interact online, eroticism often comes to the surface.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/my-ai-is-sexually-harassing-me-replika-chatbot-nudes/","d":"2023-01-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-12","organization":"Replika Inc.","aiSystem":"Replika AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"longtime users of the chatbot","action":"the chatbot app began to engage in sexually aggressive behavior","consequence":"users experienced distress and felt their privacy was violated"}},{"i":"R1117","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"Workers who train googles search algorithm finally got a raise thanks to union action","s":"Thousands of contractors who work for RaterLabs and power Google search’s AI behind the scenes will now receive $14-$14.50 an hour. Thousands of Google contract workers responsible for training and rating the engine’s search results will receive a historic pay raise this year after a months-long Alphabet Workers Union-CWA campaign, the AWU-CWA announced Wednesday. The workers are based at RaterLabs , an AI training vendor whose only known client is Google, according to an AWU-CWA press release. Where these workers, known as raters, were previously paid as little as $10 an hour, they will now be paid $14 or $14.50 an hour, depending on experience.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/workers-who-train-googles-search-algorithm-finally-got-a-raise-thanks-to-union-action/","d":"2023-01-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-11","organization":"Alphabet Workers Union-CWA (AWU-CWA)","aiSystem":"Google search engine","affectedGroup":"Contract workers who train and rate Google search results","action":"Received a historic pay raise","consequence":"Improved working conditions and compensation"}},{"i":"R1118","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Startup uses ai chatbot to provide mental health counseling and then realizes it feels weird","s":"“We pulled the feature anyway and I wanted to unravel the concern as a thought piece, to help reign in enthusiasm about gpt3 replacing therapists.” A mental health nonprofit is under fire for using an AI chatbot as an “experiment” to provide support to people seeking counseling, and for experimenting with the technology on real people. “We provided mental health support to about 4,000 people — using GPT-3. Here’s what happened,” Rob Morris, a cofounder of the mental health nonprofit Koko, tweeted Friday. “Messages composed by AI (and supervised by humans) were rated significantly higher than those written by humans on their own (p < .001).","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/startup-uses-ai-chatbot-to-provide-mental-health-counseling-and-then-realizes-it-feels-weird/","d":"2023-01-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-10","organization":"Koko, a mental health nonprofit","aiSystem":"GPT-3","affectedGroup":"people seeking counseling","action":"provided mental health support to about 4,000 people using GPT-3","consequence":"messages composed by AI were rated significantly higher than those written by humans"}},{"i":"R1119","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Meet the spy tech companies helping landlords evict people","s":"The growing ‘proptech’ industry is now explicitly marketing surveillance tech as a way to evict tenants and raise rent. Some renters may savor the convenience of “smart home” technologies like keyless entry and internet-connected doorbell cameras. But tech companies are increasingly selling these solutions to landlords for a more nefarious purpose: spying on tenants in order to evict them or raise their rent. “You CAN raise rents in NYC!” reads the headline of one promotional email sent to landlords. It was a sales pitch from Teman, a tech company that makes surveillance systems for apartment buildings. Teman’s sales pitch proposes a solution to a frustration for many New York City landlords , who have tenants living in older apartments that are protected by a myriad of rent control and stabilization laws The company’s email suggests a workaround: “3 Simple Steps to Re-Regulate a Unit.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/meet-the-spy-tech-companies-helping-landlords-evict-people/","d":"2023-01-04","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-04","organization":"Teman (a tech company)","aiSystem":"Surveillance Systems for Apartment Buildings","affectedGroup":"Tenants in older apartments protected by rent control and stabilization laws","action":"Landlords use surveillance systems to monitor tenants","consequence":"Eviction or increase in rent"}},{"i":"R259","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Openai messaging system hacked","s":"A hacker infiltrated OpenAI’s internal messaging system last year and stole details about the company’s AI designs. A hacker managed to infiltrate OpenAI’s internal messaging system last year and abscond with details about the company’s AI design, according to a report from the New York Times on Thursday. The attack targeted an online forum where OpenAI employees discussed upcoming technologies and features for the popular chatbot, however, the systems where the actual GPT code and user data are stored were not impacted. While the company disclosed that information to its employees and board members in April 2023, the company declined to notify either the public or the FBI about the breach, claiming that doing so was unnecessary because no user or partner data was stolen.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openai-messaging-system-hacked/","d":"2024-07-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI's internal messaging system","affectedGroup":"OpenAI employees and board members","action":"Hacker infiltrated and stole details about the company’s AI designs","consequence":"No user or partner data was stolen, but information was disclosed to employees and board members"}},{"i":"R271","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Openai and microsoft sued by ny times over copyright infringement","s":"The NY Times has filed a lawsuit accusing OpenAI and its backer, Microsoft, of infringing its copyright by using its content to train their AI-powered products. The New York Times has become the first major media organization to take on AI firms in the courts , accusing OpenAI and its backer, Microsoft, of infringing its copyright by using its content to train AI-powered products such as OpenAI’s ChatGPT. In a lawsuit filed in Federal District Court in Manhattan, the media giant claims that “millions” of its copyrighted articles were used to train its AI technologies, enabling it to compete with the New York Times as a content provider.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openai-and-microsoft-sued-by-ny-times-over-copyright-infringement/","d":"2023-12-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-28","organization":"OpenAI and Microsoft","aiSystem":"AI-powered products such as OpenAI’s ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"The New York Times and its copyrighted articles","action":"Using The New York Times' content to train AI-powered products","consequence":"Enabling OpenAI and Microsoft to compete with The New York Times as a content provider"}},{"i":"R272","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Over 70 percent internet malicious activity","s":"The notable surge in malicious bots can be attributed to the growing availability of AI tools, particularly those associated with generative AI. In a concerning revelation by the fraud control platform Arkose Labs , about 73% of internet traffic to websites and apps that was analyzed between January and September 2023 has been attributed to bots engaging in malicious activities. This revelation sparks discussions about the significant drain on valuable resources caused by such nefarious actions. The third quarter of 2023 witnessed the dominance of five primary categories of bad bot activities, including account takeover, scraping, fake account creation, account management, and in-product abuse.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/over-70-percent-internet-malicious-activity/","d":"2023-12-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-30","organization":"Arkose Labs","aiSystem":"AI tools, particularly generative AI","affectedGroup":"Websites and apps","action":"Engaging in malicious activities","consequence":"Drain on valuable resources"}},{"i":"R273","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Google tackles scammers offering malware laden bard imitator","s":"Google says it’s suing alleged scammers who are offering malware-laden generative AI software called Bard – the same name as Google’s own generative AI tool. Google has revealed that it’s suing alleged scammers who are offering malware-laden “generative AI” software called Bard — the same name as Google’s own generative AI tool Google filed the lawsuit in a California court on Monday, accusing the Vietnam-based group of creating pages for social media sites — mainly Facebook — that include ads for “Bard” software. The web giant’s suit says the defendants are “three individuals whose identities are unknown who claim to provide, among other things, ‘the latest version’ of Google Bard for download.”","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/google-tackles-scammers-offering-malware-laden-bard-imitator/","d":"2023-11-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-14","organization":"Individuals (unknown identities)","aiSystem":"Generative AI software","affectedGroup":"Users of social media platforms, particularly Facebook users","action":"Offering and distributing malware-laden 'Bard' software","consequence":"Potential security risks and privacy violations for users"}},{"i":"R274","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Bing chat just beat captcha challenge","s":"CAPTCHA challenges are designed to thwart hackers and spam bots, but someone has just shown that ChatGPT can beat them. That’s worrying news for cybersecurity. Bing Chat is no stranger to controversy — in fact, sometimes it feels like there’s a never-ending stream of scandals surrounding it and tools like ChatGPT — and now the artificial intelligence (AI) chatbot has found itself in hot water over its ability to defeat a common cybersecurity measure. According to Denis Shiryaev , the CEO of AI startup Neural.love, chatbots like Bing Chat and ChatGPT can potentially be used to bypass a CAPTCHA code if you just ask them the right set of questions.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/bing-chat-just-beat-captcha-challenge/","d":"2023-10-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-10-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-10-03","organization":"Microsoft (for Bing Chat)","aiSystem":"Bing Chat and ChatGPT (AI chatbots)","affectedGroup":"Hackers and spam bots","action":"ChatGPT and Bing Chat were able to answer CAPTCHA questions correctly","consequence":"Potential misuse of AI chatbots to bypass security measures"}},{"i":"R276","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Bing chat ads hijacked by malware","s":"The Bing Chat AI chatbot often serves up adverts to its users, but it turns out these ads can be hijacked by malicious websites containing dangerous malware. Since it launched, Microsoft’s Bing Chat has been generating headlines left, right, and center — and not all of them have been positive Now, there’s a new headache for the artificial intelligence (AI) chatbot, as it’s been found it has a tendency to send you to malware websites that can infect your PC. The discovery was made by antivirus firm Malwarebytes, which discussed the incident in a blog post According to the company, Bing Chat is displaying malware advertisements that send users to malicious websites instead of filtering them out.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/bing-chat-ads-hijacked-by-malware/","d":"2023-09-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-29","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Bing Chat AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"users of Bing Chat","action":"displaying malware advertisements","consequence":"sending users to malicious websites that can infect their PC"}},{"i":"R277","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Writers strike ends after agreement on ai and other issues","s":"The five-month Hollywood writers strike was called off on Tuesday after union leaders approved a new three-year deal. The Writers Guild of America (WGA) has called off its strike after five months during which a slew of popular shows were taken off air. The WGA said it had reached a tentative agreement with the Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers on a new three-year Minimum Basic Agreement (MBA), and voted unanimously to recommend it to its 11,500 members. Recommended Videos It’s also given the green light for writers to return to work from Wednesday, though acknowledged that the situation could change if its members vote against the deal when they have their say next month.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/movies/writers-strike-ends-after-agreement-on-ai-and-other-issues/","d":"2023-09-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-27","organization":"Writers Guild of America (WGA)","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Hollywood writers","action":"Reaching a tentative agreement on a new Minimum Basic Agreement (MBA)","consequence":"Approval to return to work from Wednesday"}},{"i":"R278","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Spotify using ai to clone and translate podcasters voice","s":"A new Spotify feature powered by artificial intelligence translates a podcast into multiple languages using the same voices of those in the show. Spotify has unveiled a remarkable new feature powered by artificial intelligence (AI) that translates a podcast into multiple languages using the same voices of those in the show. It’s been made possible partly by OpenAI’s just-released voice generation technology that needs only a few seconds of listening to replicate a voice. Recommended Videos Spotify said the feature makes for “a more authentic listening experience that sounds more personal and natural than traditional dubbing,” adding: “A podcast episode originally recorded in English can now be available in other languages while keeping the speaker’s distinctive speech characteristics.”","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/movies/spotify-using-ai-to-clone-and-translate-podcasters-voice/","d":"2023-09-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-26","organization":"Spotify","aiSystem":"Spotify's AI-powered voice duplication technology","affectedGroup":"Podcast listeners","action":"Translates a podcast episode into multiple languages using the same voices of those in the show","consequence":"May lead to loss of authenticity and personalization in the listening experience"}},{"i":"R279","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Microsoft leaked 38tb sensitive data","s":"Microsoft’s own AI researchers accidentally leaked 38TB of highly sensitive data on their own GitHub page, potentially creating a field day for hackers. It’s just been revealed that Microsoft researchers accidentally leaked 38TB of confidential information onto the company’s GitHub page, where potentially anyone could see it. Among the data trove was a backup of two former employees’ workstations, which contained keys, passwords, secrets, and more than 30,000 private Teams messages. According to cloud security firm Wiz , the leak was published on Microsoft’s artificial intelligence (AI) GitHub repository and was accidentally included in a tranche of open-source training data.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/microsoft-leaked-38tb-sensitive-data/","d":"2023-09-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-19","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) GitHub repository","affectedGroup":"Potential hackers, former employees' workstations, and users of Microsoft Teams","action":"Accidentally leaked 38TB of confidential information onto Microsoft's GitHub page","consequence":"Created a field day for hackers, exposed sensitive data including keys, passwords, secrets, and private Teams messages"}},{"i":"R282","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Nvidia announces nemo steerlm ai","s":"Nvidia’s latest development, Nemo SteerLM, can help game devs turn NPCs as toxic or funny as they please with AI. Nvidia ACE , the tool that’s meant to transform games with generative AI, is evolving, and Nvidia has just revealed an interesting update to it. It turns out that developers won’t just be able to utilize ACE to create fully-interactive characters, complete with dialogue and facial expressions, but they’ll also be able to use Nvidia’s new NeMo SteerLM technology to adapt these characters’ personalities. Nvidia initially announced its ACE tool earlier this year, keeping the details pretty scarce. ACE is something like ChatGPT for games, meant to make it easier for game developers to create characters with fully-developed backstories, adding more depth to the way they interact with players.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/nvidia-announces-nemo-steerlm-ai/","d":"2023-08-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-22","organization":"Nvidia","aiSystem":"NeMo SteerLM","affectedGroup":"Game Developers","action":"Adjust NPC personalities using AI technology","consequence":"Enhanced interactive and immersive gaming experiences"}},{"i":"R284","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Ai can steal passwords with 100 accuracy","s":"Researchers have discovered a devious new way for AI to steal your passwords, and it’s almost 100% accurate. Researchers at Cornell University have discovered a new way for AI tools to steal your data — keystrokes. A new research paper details an AI-driven attack that can steal passwords with up to 95% accuracy by listening to what you type on your keyboard. The researchers accomplished this by training an AI model on the sound of keystrokes and deploying it on a nearby phone. The integrated microphone listened for keystrokes on a MacBook Pro and was able to reproduce them with 95% accuracy — the highest accuracy the researchers have seen without the use of a large language model.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-can-steal-passwords-with-100-accuracy/","d":"2023-08-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2023-08-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-07","organization":"Cornell University","aiSystem":"AI-driven keystroke recognition model","affectedGroup":"Individuals using a MacBook Pro","action":"AI model trained on keystroke sounds and used to reproduce typed passwords","consequence":"Up to 95% accuracy in stealing passwords"}},{"i":"R415","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"New york times artificial intelligence lawsuit","s":"The New York Times is suing OpenAI and Microsoft for copyright infringement—a major development in the ongoing, nationwide legal struggle between content creators and artificial intelligence firms. The paper warns AI firms face “billions of dollars” in damages. While previous lawsuits claiming intellectual property violations by AI companies have come from artists and writers, the Times […]. While previous lawsuits claiming intellectual property violations by AI companies have come from artists and writers, the Times is the first American news organization to sue the companies, alleging that OpenAI and Microsoft used millions of their articles to train digital chatbots that now compete with the publication.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/media/2023/12/new-york-times-artificial-intelligence-lawsuit/","d":"2023-12-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-27","organization":"OpenAI and Microsoft","aiSystem":"digital chatbots trained on millions of articles","affectedGroup":"The New York Times and its content creators","action":"OpenAI and Microsoft allegedly used millions of The New York Times articles to train digital chatbots","consequence":"Potential billions of dollars in damages for The New York Times"}},{"i":"R416","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Im a striking tv writer heres why were doing this","s":"By now you’ve seen the scenes of young and middle-aged Americans wearing cargo shorts and Patagonia Baggies Brimmers in front of movie studios holding ironic picket signs, such as “Chat GPT doesn’t have childhood trauma!” As the battle grinds into its 12th week, with no end in sight—and the actors’ union now part of the […]. As the battle grinds into its 12th week, with no end in sight—and the actors’ union now part of the strike too—it’s easy to think that a fight between a bunch of “rich Hollywood writers” and the big studios and networks may mean nothing to you, but it’s quite the opposite. This is the labor fight of our generation.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/media/2023/07/im-a-striking-tv-writer-heres-why-were-doing-this/","d":"2023-07-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-20","organization":"Hollywood writers' union","aiSystem":"AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Young and middle-aged Americans","action":"Hold picket signs criticizing AI chatbots","consequence":"No end to the strike in sight"}},{"i":"R417","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Thousands of Reddit communities went dark this week to protest the webforum giant’s money-…","s":"Thousands of Reddit communities went dark this week to protest the webforum giant’s money-making plans. Ahead of an expected IPO later this year, the company’s CEO announced it would start charging for access to its application programming interface. APIs are a common feature of web platforms, essentially letting anyone peer more deeply under the hood. They […]. They are often harnessed by ancillary apps that provide users other ways or spaces to interact with a platform’s offerings. Reddit executives’ defense of the move sounds reasonable, especially now: Companies like OpenAI and Google are reportedly inhaling value from Reddit, putting the content it hosts in their artificial intelligence training data sets without compensating the company.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2023/06/reddit-blackout/","d":"2023-06-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-15","organization":"Reddit","aiSystem":"Reddit's Application Programming Interface (API)","affectedGroup":"Reddit community members","action":"Reddit announced a plan to charge for access to its API","consequence":"Potential loss of ancillary app functionality and user experience disruption"}},{"i":"R418","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Gop video biden warning artificial intelligence guide","s":"Today President Biden announced his reelection bid. In response, the GOP announced its further descent into the uncanny valley, with a big assist from artificial intelligence. Presumably designed as a rebuke of Biden’s own relatively somber campaign video, the Republican offering—titled “Beat Biden”—foretells the dystopian future heralded by the incumbent’s imagined second win. It’s an […]. Today President Biden announced his reelection bid In response, the GOP announced its further descent into the uncanny valley, with a big assist from artificial intelligence. It’s an all-but-unlivable hellscape of war, financial ruin, and crime.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2023/04/gop-video-biden-warning-artificial-intelligence-guide/","d":"2023-04-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-25","organization":"Republican Party (GOP)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology and AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Released a dystopian future-themed political advertisement","consequence":"Potential manipulation and fearmongering"}},{"i":"R419","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm","1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Bing ai chatbot falsehoods fact checking microsoft","s":"“You’re in!” the email said. “Welcome to the new Bing!” Last Sunday, I joined a small wave of users granted early access to Microsoft’s new chatbot backed search engine, put forward as an “AI-powered copilot for the web.” Given that “multiple millions” across 169 countries were stuck on the waitlist, I felt like Charlie waving […]. “You’re in!” the email said. “Welcome to the new Bing!” Given that “ multiple millions ” across 169 countries were stuck on the waitlist, I felt like Charlie waving a Golden Ticket. Little did I know that I, like Roald Dahl’s beloved character, would too be led by a mercurial and untrustworthy host into a world of pure imagination.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2023/02/bing-ai-chatbot-falsehoods-fact-checking-microsoft/","d":"2023-02-18","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-18","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Microsoft's new chatbot-backed search engine","affectedGroup":"Multiple millions of users across 169 countries","action":"Users received an email welcoming them to the new Bing chatbot","consequence":"Users were exposed to false information and misleading content"}},{"i":"R420","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Workplace surveillance stop spying bosses act","s":"Whether you call it “tattleware,” “bossware,” or “surveillance capitalism,” Sen. Bob Casey (D-Pa.) has had enough of exploitative workplace monitoring technologies. Late last week, Casey and a handful of other Senate Democrats introduced the Stop Spying Bosses Act, which would help protect workers from intrusive employer surveillance both on and off the clock. The legislation would require “timely and […]. The legislation would require “timely and public” disclosures by companies about the data they’re collecting on employees, prohibit businesses from using surveillance practices that obstruct union organizing or monitor workers while they’re off the clock, and create a new division of the Department of Labor to regulate workplace surveillance.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2023/02/workplace-surveillance-stop-spying-bosses-act/","d":"2023-02-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-07","organization":"Sen. Bob Casey (D-Pa.) and Senate Democrats","aiSystem":"Exploitative workplace monitoring technologies","affectedGroup":"Workers","action":"Require timely and public disclosures by companies about employee data collection, prohibit surveillance practices obstructing union organizing, and create a new DOL division to regulate workplace surveillance","consequence":"Protection against intrusive employer surveillance"}},{"i":"R511","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Questions concerns chatgpt health","s":"Can an AI chatbot offer true support for people in need of medical care? ChatGPT’s newly launched health-focused version looks to answer. OpenAI announced on Wednesday, January 7, that it was coming out with a health-focused version of ChatGPT called ChatGPT Health Called “a dedicated experience in ChatGPT designed for health and wellness,” this new experience would allow prospective users to feed their personal health information to ChatGPT. The company says the goal of ChatGPT Health is to help make sense of scattered health information people have from doctors, tests, and health monitoring apps, as well as to “understand recent test results, prepare for appointments with your doctor, get advice on how to approach your diet and workout routine, or understand the tradeoffs of different insurance options based on your healthcare patterns.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/questions-concerns-chatgpt-health/","d":"2026-01-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-07","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT Health","affectedGroup":"Individuals seeking medical care","action":"Users can input personal health information into ChatGPT Health","consequence":"Potential misuse of health information, privacy concerns"}},{"i":"R744","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Meet ashley worlds first ai powered political campaign caller","s":"US politicians deploy an AI phone caller in election campaigns, able to have customized one-on-one conversations with voters, causing some to worry over its potential for disinformation. Democrat Shamaine Daniels is running for Congress, eyeing a seat held by Trump-aligned Republican Representative Scott Perry, who played a key role challenging the 2020 election results. Daniels, who lost to Perry by less than 10 points last year, hopes a new weapon will help her underdog candidacy: Ashley, an artificial intelligence campaign volunteer. Ashley is not your typical robocaller; none of her responses are canned or pre-recorded.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/meet-ashley-worlds-first-ai-powered-political-campaign-caller/","d":"2023-12-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-13","organization":"Democrat Shamaine Daniels' campaign","aiSystem":"Ashley, an artificial intelligence campaign volunteer","affectedGroup":"Voters","action":"Have customized one-on-one conversations with voters","consequence":"Cause concern about potential for disinformation"}},{"i":"R745","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Disinformation trends 2023 growing ai reliance x reckoning tech guardrails","s":"As we prepare to continue to tackle disinformation in 2024, here are the big trends from this year. MANILA, Philippines – Disinformation remains rampant. One only has to look at the ongoing Israel-Hamas war that has observers in chaos as to which is real and which is not. Elon Musk’s X, and its demolition of its trust and safety team early in his ownership stint along with other questionable policies, have supercharged this online mess, and cheap AI-powered tools for making videos and photos will only make things worse. It’s a problem we will continue to tackle in 2024, but as the year draws to a close, we look back at the trends in disinformation from this year to hopefully find some lessons to bring in fighting the fake next year.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/disinformation-trends-2023-growing-ai-reliance-x-reckoning-tech-guardrails/","d":"2023-12-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-06","organization":"Elon Musk's X (formerly Twitter)","aiSystem":"cheap AI-powered tools for making videos and photos","affectedGroup":"general public","action":"use of AI-generated content for spreading disinformation","consequence":"worsening of disinformation problem"}},{"i":"R747","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Meta bars political advertisers generative ai ads tools","s":"‘We believe this approach will allow us to better understand potential risks and build the right safeguards for the use of Generative AI in ads that relate to potentially sensitive topics in regulated industries,’ Meta says. NEW YORK, USA – Facebook owner Meta is barring political campaigns and advertisers in other regulated industries from using its new generative artificial intelligence (AI) advertising products, a company spokesperson said on Monday, November 6, denying access to tools that lawmakers have warned could turbo-charge the spread of election misinformation. Meta publicly disclosed the decision in updates posted to its help center on Monday night, following publication of this story.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/meta-bars-political-advertisers-generative-ai-ads-tools/","d":"2023-11-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2023-11-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-06","organization":"Meta (Facebook owner)","aiSystem":"Generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) advertising products","affectedGroup":"Political campaigns and advertisers in regulated industries","action":"Barred from using new generative AI advertising products","consequence":"Potential misuse for spreading election misinformation"}},{"i":"R750","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Music publishers sue anthropic artificial intelligence company song lyrics","s":"Publishers like Universal Music sue Anthropic for allegedly misusing an ‘innumerable’ amount of copyrighted song lyrics to train its chatbot Claude. Music publishers Universal Music, ABKCO, and Concord sued artificial intelligence company Anthropic in Tennessee federal court on Wednesday, October 18, accusing it of misusing an “innumerable” amount of copyrighted song lyrics to train its chatbot Claude. The lawsuit said Anthropic violates the publishers’ rights through its use of lyrics from at least 500 songs ranging from the Beach Boys’ “God Only Knows” and the Rolling Stones’ “Gimme Shelter” to Mark Ronson and Bruno Mars’ “Uptown Funk” and Beyonce’s “Halo.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/entertainment/music/music-publishers-sue-anthropic-artificial-intelligence-company-song-lyrics/","d":"2023-10-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-10-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-10-18","organization":"Artificial Intelligence company Anthropic","aiSystem":"Anthropic's chatbot Claude","affectedGroup":"Music publishers (Universal Music, ABKCO, Concord)","action":"Training Anthropic's chatbot Claude with copyrighted song lyrics","consequence":"Potential violation of music publishers' copyright rights"}},{"i":"R751","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Thomson reuters ai copyright dispute must go trial","s":"The decision by US Circuit Judge Stephanos Bibas sets the stage for what could be one of the first trials related to the unauthorized use of data to train AI systems. A jury must decide the outcome of a lawsuit by information services company Thomson Reuters accusing Ross Intelligence of unlawfully copying content from its legal-research platform Westlaw to train a competing artificial intelligence-based platform, a Delaware federal judge said on Monday, September 25. Tech companies including Meta Platforms, Stability AI and Microsoft-backed OpenAI are also facing lawsuits from authors , visual artists and other copyright owners over the use of their work to train the companies’ generative AI software.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/thomson-reuters-ai-copyright-dispute-must-go-trial/","d":"2023-09-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-25","organization":"Ross Intelligence","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence-based platform","affectedGroup":"Thomson Reuters","action":"unlawfully copying content from Westlaw to train a competing AI platform","consequence":"potential trial involving tech companies and copyright owners"}},{"i":"R753","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Top us authors sue openai over copyrights","s":"The proposed class-action lawsuit filed on September 19 by the Authors Guild joins several others from writers, source-code owners, and visual artists against generative AI providers. A trade group for US authors has sued OpenAI in Manhattan federal court on behalf of prominent writers including John Grisham, Jonathan Franzen, George Saunders, Jodi Picoult, and Game of Thrones novelist George R.R. Martin, accusing the company of unlawfully training its popular artificial-intelligence based chatbot ChatGPT on their work. The proposed class-action lawsuit filed late on Tuesday, September 19, by the Authors Guild joins several others from writers, source-code owners, and visual artists against generative AI providers.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/top-us-authors-sue-openai-over-copyrights/","d":"2023-09-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-19","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT (generative AI-based chatbot)","affectedGroup":"Authors, writers, source-code owners, visual artists","action":"Training ChatGPT on copyrighted works","consequence":"Potential copyright infringement"}},{"i":"R754","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Pulitzer winner chabon other authors sue meta over ai program","s":"The group of authors claims Meta trained its Llama large-language model with datasets that included pirated versions of their writings. A group of writers including Pulitzer Prize winner Michael Chabon sued Meta Platforms in San Francisco federal court on Tuesday, September 12, accusing the tech giant of misusing their works to train its Llama artificial-intelligence software. Chabon, Tony-winning playwright David Henry Hwang and authors Matthew Klam, Rachel Louise Snyder and Ayelet Waldman in a lawsuit said Meta taught the Llama large-language model to respond to human text prompts with datasets that included pirated versions of their writings.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/pulitzer-winner-chabon-other-authors-sue-meta-over-ai-program/","d":"2023-09-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-12","organization":"Meta Platforms","aiSystem":"Llama large-language model","affectedGroup":"Michael Chabon (Pulitzer Prize winner), David Henry Hwang (Tony-winning playwright), Matthew Klam, Rachel Louise Snyder, Ayelet Waldman (authors)","action":"trained the Llama large-language model with datasets that included pirated versions of their writings","consequence":"accused of misusing their works to train the AI software"}},{"i":"R760","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"X twitter privacy policy update public data use train ai models","s":"Privacy policy tweaks on X say it ‘may use the information we collect and publicly available information to help train our machine learning or artificial intelligence models for the purposes outlined in this policy’. After Bloomberg spotted a privacy policy change in which X informed its users that it would collect their biometric data and job and education history, Alex Ivanovs of Stackdiary noticed a different set of changes on the social media platform’s privacy policy as it related to artificial intelligence (AI) training. In his blog post, Ivanovs said a separate portion of the privacy policy, specifically Section 2.1 under “Operate, improve, and personalize our services,” states X “may use the information we collect and publicly available information to help train our machine learning or artificial intelligence models for the purposes outlined in this policy.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/x-twitter-privacy-policy-update-public-data-use-train-ai-models/","d":"2023-09-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-04","organization":"X (Twitter)","aiSystem":"Machine Learning or Artificial Intelligence Models","affectedGroup":"Users of X (Twitter)","action":"Collect and use user data and publicly available information to train AI models","consequence":"Potential misuse of personal data for AI training, privacy concerns"}},{"i":"R761","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Ai generated art cannot receive copyrights united states court","s":"Only works with human authors can receive copyrights, US District Judge Beryl Howell says, affirming the Copyright Office’s rejection of an application filed by computer scientist Stephen Thaler on behalf of his DABUS system. A work of art created by artificial intelligence without any human input cannot be copyrighted under US law, a US court in Washington, DC, has ruled. Only works with human authors can receive copyrights, US District Judge Beryl Howell said on Friday, August 18, affirming the Copyright Office’s rejection of an application filed by computer scientist Stephen Thaler on behalf of his DABUS system.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/ai-generated-art-cannot-receive-copyrights-united-states-court/","d":"2023-08-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-18","organization":"Stephen Thaler (computer scientist)","aiSystem":"DABUS system","affectedGroup":"Artwork created by artificial intelligence","action":"Application for copyright registration","consequence":"Denial of copyright protection"}},{"i":"R762","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Openai websites block gptbot web crawler ai","s":"OpenAI says ‘web pages crawled with the GPTBot user agent may potentially be used to improve future models’. MANILA, Philippines – OpenAI is letting website owners opt out of being scraped by its web crawler GPTBot. Web crawlers are most commonly used by search engines to browse through the internet, and index websites. OpenAI’s GPTBot web crawler will be used by the ChatGPT creator to improve its AI systems by scouring the internet for websites that hold publicly available, potentially valuable training data. On its blog , OpenAI said, “Web pages crawled with the GPTBot user agent may potentially be used to improve future models….","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/openai-websites-block-gptbot-web-crawler-ai/","d":"2023-08-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-09","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"GPTBot web crawler","affectedGroup":"Website owners","action":"Scouring the internet for publicly available training data","consequence":"Potential misuse of scraped data for improving AI models"}},{"i":"R763","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Deciphering zoom policies ai training using your data","s":"(1st UPDATE) While Zoom says it reserves the right to train AI on users’ calls without explicit permission, it added statements to the terms of service promising to seek permission before doing so. How does this work, however? Getting on a Zoom call seems commonplace now in a time where remote working is allowed, but the push towards artificial intelligence in certain sectors of tech is making its way into video conferencing app Zoom, and the result may be having the video, audio or chats you’ve inputted into Zoom used to train AI. Unless, that is, the person who sets up your meetings – and not necessarily you yourself – opts out of AI training in the first place.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/deciphering-zoom-policies-ai-training-using-your-data/","d":"2023-08-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-09","organization":"Zoom","aiSystem":"AI training","affectedGroup":"users' data","action":"training AI on users' calls without explicit permission","consequence":"potential misuse of user data for AI training"}},{"i":"R766","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Actors decry existential crisis artificial intelligence synthetic actors","s":"Actors are worried that ‘digital replicas’ or entirely AI-generated actors called ‘metahumans’ will steal their roles. Filmmakers have put monsters on screen for more than a century. In 2023, the real bogeyman looks just like us. Since June, Hollywood studios and performers have debated the use of artificial intelligence in film and television. Failure to agree on terms around AI was one reason why the SAG-AFTRA union representing actors and media professionals last Friday joined the writers guild in the first simultaneous strike in 63 years. Among the actors’ greatest fears? Synthetic performers. While the two sides have negotiated over issues ranging from using images and performances as training data for AI systems to digitally altering performances in the editing room, actors are worried entirely AI-generated actors, or “metahumans,” will steal their roles.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/entertainment/movies/actors-decry-existential-crisis-artificial-intelligence-synthetic-actors/","d":"2023-07-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-22","organization":"Hollywood studios and performers","aiSystem":"AI-generated actors or metahumans","affectedGroup":"Actors","action":"Filmmakers have debated the use of artificial intelligence in film and television","consequence":"Actors' concern about AI-generated actors stealing their roles"}},{"i":"R769","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Ai chat detectors discriminating against non native english writers","s":"The study points out the difference between the types of essays was primarily in text perplexity, defined by how ‘surprised’ a generative language model is when trying to guess the next word in a sentence. MANILA, Philippines – In a study released on the journal Patterns on Monday, July 10, programs used to detect whether a written work is written by a human or by artificial intelligence are said to “frequently misclassify non-native English writing as AI generated.” The study, written by a team of scientists led by James Zhou, an assistant professor of biomedical data science at Stanford University, ran 91 Test of English as a Foreign Language (TOEFL) essays written by non-native English speakers and 88 US eighth-grade essays from the Hewlett Foundation’s ASAP dataset through seven AI writing detectors – or Generative Pre-training Transformer (GPT) detectors – to determine what biases,…","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/ai-chat-detectors-discriminating-against-non-native-english-writers/","d":"2023-07-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-10","organization":"Stanford University","aiSystem":"Generative Pre-training Transformer (GPT) detectors","affectedGroup":"non-native English writers","action":"AI writing detectors frequently misclassified non-native English writing as AI generated","consequence":"discrimination against non-native English writers"}},{"i":"R770","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Sarah silverman sues meta openai copyright infringement","s":"The lawsuits underscore the legal risks developers of chat bots face when using troves of copyrighted material to create apps that deliver realistic responses to user prompts. Comedian Sarah Silverman and two authors have filed copyright infringement lawsuits against Meta Platforms and OpenAI for allegedly using their content without permission to train artificial intelligence language models. The proposed class action lawsuits filed by Silverman, Richard Kadrey, and Christopher Golden in San Francisco federal court on Friday, July 7, allege Facebook parent company Meta and ChatGPT maker OpenAI used copyrighted material to train chat bots.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/sarah-silverman-sues-meta-openai-copyright-infringement/","d":"2023-07-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-07","organization":"Meta Platforms, OpenAI","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence language models","affectedGroup":"Comedian Sarah Silverman, Authors Richard Kadrey and Christopher Golden","action":"used copyrighted material to train chat bots","consequence":"legal risks including copyright infringement lawsuits"}},{"i":"R771","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Google ai big backer warns staff chatbots","s":"Google parent Alphabet advises employees to not input confidential materials into AI chatbots, including its own Bard. Alphabet Inc. is cautioning employees about how they use chatbots, including its own Bard, at the same time as it markets the program around the world, four people familiar with the matter told Reuters. The Google parent has advised employees not to enter its confidential materials into AI chatbots, the people said and the company confirmed, citing long-standing policy on safeguarding information. The chatbots, among them Bard and ChatGPT, are human-sounding programs that use so-called generative artificial intelligence to hold conversations with users and answer myriad prompts.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/google-ai-big-backer-warns-staff-chatbots/","d":"2023-06-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-16","organization":"Alphabet Inc. (Google parent)","aiSystem":"AI chatbots (including Bard)","affectedGroup":"Employees of Alphabet Inc.","action":"Advise employees not to input confidential materials into AI chatbots","consequence":"Potential exposure of confidential information"}},{"i":"R772","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Carbon footprint chatgpt generative ai environment effects","s":"Some industry figures estimate the carbon footprint of a single generative AI query to be four to five times higher than that of a search engine query. Generative AI is the hot new technology behind chatbots and image generators. But how hot is it making the planet? As an AI researcher , I often worry about the energy costs of building artificial intelligence models. The more powerful the AI, the more energy it takes. What does the emergence of increasingly more powerful generative AI models mean for society’s future carbon footprint? “Generative” refers to the ability of an AI algorithm to produce complex data. The alternative is “discriminative” AI , which chooses between a fixed number of options and produces just a single number.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/carbon-footprint-chatgpt-generative-ai-environment-effects/","d":"2023-05-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-27","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"Planet Earth","action":"Emergence of increasingly more powerful generative AI models","consequence":"Higher carbon footprint compared to traditional search engines"}},{"i":"R774","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Who warns bias misinformation ai healthcare","s":"The World Health Organization is enthusiastic about AI use but has concerns over potentially biased training data, and misleading or inaccurate information. The World Health Organization called for caution on Tuesday, May 16, in using artificial intelligence for public healthcare, saying data used by AI to reach decisions could be biased or misused. The WHO said it was enthusiastic about the potential of AI but had concerns over how it will be used to improve access to health information, as a decision-support tool and to improve diagnostic care. The WHO said in a statement the data used to train AI may be biased and generate misleading or inaccurate information and the models can be misused to generate disinformation.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/who-warns-bias-misinformation-ai-healthcare/","d":"2023-05-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2023-05-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-16","organization":"World Health Organization (WHO)","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence systems used in healthcare","affectedGroup":"Public, patients, healthcare providers","action":"Using AI for public healthcare purposes","consequence":"Potential bias in training data leading to misleading or inaccurate information"}},{"i":"R776","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Meta chatgpt related malware rising","s":"Asked also if generative AI was being used in information operations, Meta’s chief information security officer says they are expecting bad actors to use the tech to ‘try to speed up and perhaps scale up’ activities. Facebook owner Meta said on Wednesday, May 3, it had uncovered malware purveyors leveraging public interest in ChatGPT to lure users into downloading malicious apps and browser extensions, likening the phenomenon to cryptocurrency scams. Since March, the social media giant has found around 10 malware families and more than 1,000 malicious links that were promoted as tools featuring the popular artificial intelligence-powered chatbot, it said in a report.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/meta-chatgpt-related-malware-rising/","d":"2023-05-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-03","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Users of social media platforms","action":"Leverage ChatGPT to promote malicious apps and browser extensions","consequence":"Spread of malware families and malicious links"}},{"i":"R777","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Content farm sites artificial intelligence report newsguard","s":"‘Concerns that [AI] could be used to conjure up entire news organizations – once the subject of speculation by media scholars – have now become a reality,’ writes NewsGuard, a platform that rates the credibility of news sites. MANILA, Philippines – A report by NewsGuard, a platform that rates the credibility of news sites, found 49 low-quality “news and information websites” with content that appears to be almost written entirely by artificial intelligence. The sites spanned seven languages: Chinese, Czech, English, French, Portuguese, Tagalog, and Thai. “Artificial intelligence tools are now being used to populate so-called content farms, referring to low-quality websites around the world that churn out vast amounts of clickbait articles to optimize advertising revenue,” NewsGuard wrote.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/content-farm-sites-artificial-intelligence-report-newsguard/","d":"2023-05-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-02","organization":"NewsGuard, a platform that rates the credibility of news sites","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence tools","affectedGroup":"low-quality ‘news and information websites’","action":"populate content farms","consequence":"publish vast amounts of clickbait articles to optimize advertising revenue"}},{"i":"R785","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Sophisticated phishing social engineering increase ai adoption","s":"‘The trend suggests that generative AI, such as ChatGPT, is providing an avenue for threat actors to craft sophisticated and targeted attacks at speed and scale,’ Max Heinemeyer, chief product officer at cyber defense firm Darktrace, writes. MANILA, Philippines – British-American cyber defense company Darktrace revealed in an April 2 blogpost that the risk of people falling prey to malicious emails and “novel social engineering attacks” appears to be increasing, with the company juxtaposing the rise against the backdrop of increased adoption of generative AI like ChatGPT. The blogpost by Max Heinemeyer , chief product officer at Darktrace, cited findings by the Darktrace Research arm pointing to “a 135% increase in ‘novel social engineering attacks’ across thousands of active Darktrace/Email customers from January to February 2023, corresponding with the widespread adoption of ChatGPT.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/sophisticated-phishing-social-engineering-increase-ai-adoption/","d":"2023-04-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-04","organization":"Darktrace (cyber defense firm)","aiSystem":"Generative AI, such as ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"People","action":"Threat actors crafting sophisticated and targeted attacks","consequence":"Increased risk of people falling prey to malicious emails and novel social engineering attacks"}},{"i":"R786","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Clearview ai scraped social media photos without user knowledge","s":"The scraped images are part of the firm’s growing facial recognition database that’s used by law enforcement agencies across the US. Clearview AI, a controversial firm providing law enforcement in the United States with facial recognition software, scraped more than 30 billion photos from social media to beef up its database, and it did so without users’ knowledge. CEO Hoan Ton-That disclosed the statistic in an interview with BBC in late-March , adding US police had run nearly a million searches. Scraping describes the practice of automatically extracting data online, typically using scraper bots. In Clearview AI’s case, the firm scraped photos from Facebook, among other social media sites, to further grow the data available to its facial recognition software, which it claims can help identify suspects of a crime or exonerate those who’ve been wrongfully accused.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/clearview-ai-scraped-social-media-photos-without-user-knowledge/","d":"2023-04-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-04","organization":"Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Clearview AI's facial recognition software","affectedGroup":"Users of social media platforms (specifically Facebook)","action":"Automatically scraping over 30 billion photos from social media without user consent","consequence":"Potential misuse of scraped data by law enforcement agencies for criminal investigations and wrongful accusations"}},{"i":"R788","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Word excel gmail microsoft google ai features copilot workspace","s":"New AI tools may help summarize text documents, turn ideas into full presentations, and provide real-time summaries of meetings. Google and Microsoft are on a mission to remove the drudgery from computing, by bringing next-generation AI tools as add-ons to existing services. On March 16, Microsoft announced an AI-powered system called Copilot will soon be introduced to its 365 apps including Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook and Teams. The news came about two days after Google published a blog explaining its plans to embed AI into its Workspace apps such as Docs, Sheets, Slides, Meet and Chat. Collectively, millions of people use these apps each day.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/word-excel-gmail-microsoft-google-ai-features-copilot-workspace/","d":"2023-03-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-16","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Microsoft Copilot","affectedGroup":"Millions of users of Microsoft 365 apps","action":"Introduce AI-powered system to enhance productivity in Microsoft 365 apps","consequence":"Potential reduction in manual effort and increase in efficiency"}},{"i":"R791","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Chatgpt launches boom artificial intelligence written ebooks amazon","s":"ChatGPT appears ready to upend the staid book industry as would-be novelists turn to the software to help create bot-made e-books for publishing through Amazon’s Kindle Direct Publishing arm. SAN FRANCISCO, USA – Until recently, Brett Schickler never imagined he could be a published author, though he had dreamed about it. But after learning about the ChatGPT artificial intelligence program, Schickler figured an opportunity had landed in his lap. “The idea of writing a book finally seemed possible,” said Schickler, a salesman in Rochester, New York. “I thought ‘I can do this.'” Using the AI software, which can generate blocks of text from simple prompts, Schickler created a 30-page illustrated children’s e-book in a matter of hours, offering it for sale in January through Amazon.com Inc’s self-publishing unit.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/chatgpt-launches-boom-artificial-intelligence-written-ebooks-amazon/","d":"2023-02-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-22","organization":"Amazon.com Inc.","aiSystem":"ChatGPT artificial intelligence program","affectedGroup":"would-be novelists, aspiring authors","action":"created a 30-page illustrated children's e-book using AI software","consequence":"potentially undermines traditional publishing industry"}},{"i":"R793","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Cybercriminals bypass openai chatgpt restrictions","s":"Researchers with CheckPoint say cybercriminals can bypass ChatGPT’s barriers, and create malicious content, like phishing emails and malware code, using it. MANILA, Philippines – Cybercriminals are finding ways to get past restrictions to OpenAI’s ChatGPT artificial intelligence (AI) tool, allowing them to make AI-powered improvements to malware code or phishing emails. Cybersecurity company CheckPoint said in a February 7 blog post that its researchers found an instance of cybercriminals using ChatGPT to improve on the code of a piece of 2019 malware known as InfoStealer. Ars Technica added in a February 9 report that the application programming interface (API) for an OpenAI GPT-3 model known as text-davinci-003 was being used instead of ChatGPT for the purpose of bypassing the restrictions.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/cybercriminals-bypass-openai-chatgpt-restrictions/","d":"2023-02-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-07","organization":"CheckPoint cybersecurity company","aiSystem":"OpenAI's ChatGPT artificial intelligence (AI) tool","affectedGroup":"Cybercriminals","action":"Using ChatGPT to improve malware code","consequence":"Creating AI-powered improvements to malware code or phishing emails"}},{"i":"R794","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Getty images lawsuit stability ai misused photos train artificial intelligence","s":"Getty Images’ lawsuit, filed in Delaware federal court, accuses Stability AI of misusing more than 12 million Getty photos to train its Stable Diffusion AI image-generation system. Stock photo provider Getty Images has sued artificial intelligence company Stability AI Inc, accusing it in a lawsuit made public on Monday, February 6, of misusing more than 12 million Getty photos to train its Stable Diffusion AI image-generation system. The lawsuit , filed in Delaware federal court, follows a separate Getty case against Stability in the United Kingdom and a related class-action complaint filed by artists in California against Stability and other companies in the fast-growing field of generative AI.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/getty-images-lawsuit-stability-ai-misused-photos-train-artificial-intelligence/","d":"2023-02-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-06","organization":"Stability AI Inc.","aiSystem":"Stable Diffusion AI image-generation system","affectedGroup":"Getty Images stock photo provider and Getty photos","action":"misusing more than 12 million Getty photos","consequence":"potential misuse of copyrighted images and legal consequences"}},{"i":"R796","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Alphabet job cuts january 2023","s":"The job cuts affect 6% of its workforce, and follows thousands of layoffs at tech giants including Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta Platforms. Google’s parent Alphabet Inc is cutting about 12,000 jobs as it faces “a different economic reality”, it said in a staff memo, doubling down on artificial intelligence (AI) and axing staff who support experimental projects. The job cuts affect 6% of its workforce, and follows thousands of layoffs at tech giants including Amazon.com Inc, Microsoft Corp , and Meta Platforms Inc who are downsizing after a pandemic-led hiring spree left them flabby in a weak economy. Shares in Mountain View, California-based Alphabet, which boosted its workforce by nearly a third through 2020 and 2021, rose 4% on Friday.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/alphabet-job-cuts-january-2023/","d":"2023-01-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-21","organization":"Alphabet Inc (Google's parent company)","aiSystem":"Google's parent Alphabet Inc's AI-related projects","affectedGroup":"Alphabet Inc employees","action":"Cutting 12,000 jobs","consequence":"Potential loss of employment, financial impact on employees"}},{"i":"R797","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Microsoft job layoffs january 18 2023","s":"The layoffs add to the tens of thousands announced in recent months across the technology sector, which has downshifted following a strong growth period during the pandemic. DAVOS, Switzerland – Microsoft Corp on Wednesday, January 18, said it would eliminate 10,000 jobs and take a $1.2 billion charge to earnings, as its cloud-computing customers reassess their spending and the company braces for potential recession. The news comes even as the software maker is set to ramp up spending in generative artificial intelligence that the industry sees as the new bright spot. In a note to employees , CEO Satya Nadella attempted to address the divergent outlook for different parts of the business.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/microsoft-job-layoffs-january-18-2023/","d":"2023-01-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-18","organization":"Microsoft Corp","aiSystem":"Microsoft's generative artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"Employees","action":"Eliminate 10,000 jobs and take a $1.2 billion charge to earnings","consequence":"Potential recession impact and industry downturn"}},{"i":"R798","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Getty images sues stable diffusion creators stability ai","s":"Getty Images says Stability AI ‘unlawfully copied and processed millions of images protected by copyright’. MANILA, Philippines – Getty Images announced in a press statement on Tuesday, January 17, it was suing Stability AI, the makers of artificial intelligence-powered art tool Stable Diffusion, for infringing on its copyright by scraping images and processing them for use with Stable Diffusion. The Getty Images statement said it had “commenced legal proceedings in the High Court of Justice in London against Stability AI claiming Stability AI infringed intellectual property rights including copyright in content owned or represented by Getty Images.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/getty-images-sues-stable-diffusion-creators-stability-ai/","d":"2023-01-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-17","organization":"Getty Images","aiSystem":"Stable Diffusion (artificial intelligence-powered art tool)","affectedGroup":"Getty Images' copyrighted image database","action":"Stability AI scraped and processed Getty Images' copyrighted images for use with Stable Diffusion","consequence":"Getty Images claims infringement of intellectual property rights, including copyright"}},{"i":"R799","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Lawsuits artificial intelligence artists class action stability midjourney deviantart","s":"Co-counsel Matthew Butterick calls image-generating AI software a ’21st century collage tool’ remixing the copyrighted works of artists. MANILA, Philippines – A group of artists have filed a lawsuit against image-generating AI applications Stability AI, Midjourney, and DeviantArt with its DreamUp product, its representative Joseph Saveri Law Firm announced in a press release on Saturday, January 14, US time. The firm states that the companies have “created products that infringe the rights of artists and other creative individuals under the guise of alleged ‘artificial intelligence.’” The products in question make use of Stable Diffusion, an open-source technology able to generate images from a text prompt, “trained” using the LAION-5B dataset which contains 5.85 billion images paired with a text description.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/lawsuits-artificial-intelligence-artists-class-action-stability-midjourney-deviantart/","d":"2023-01-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-14","organization":"Stability AI, Midjourney, DeviantArt","aiSystem":"Stability Diffusion, Midjourney, DeviantArt's DreamUp product","affectedGroup":"artists and other creative individuals","action":"generate and distribute images from text prompts","consequence":"infringe the rights of artists and other creative individuals"}},{"i":"R850","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Atlanta seattle police axon fusus surveillance","s":"NYC, Seattle, and more paid millions for Axon Fusus software after speaking to an Atlanta officer — who sat on Fusus’s board. When the city of Seattle contacted Fusus, one of the nation’s leading police surveillance tech companies, in 2023, a company exec did more than just send over a brochure — he offered to connect Seattle with a “peer” in Atlanta’s police department who was familiar with the company’s products. This kicked off months of communication between Claudia Gross-Shader, a director in Seattle’s city auditor’s office, Marshall Freeman, who was introduced as the deputy chief administrative officer of the Atlanta Police Department, and other Seattle officials.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2025/09/02/atlanta-seattle-police-axon-fusus-surveillance/","d":"2025-09-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023","organization":"Axon Fusus (leading police surveillance tech company)","aiSystem":"Axon Fusus","affectedGroup":"Seattle city officials, Atlanta police department officials","action":"Axon Fusus executive offered to introduce Seattle to an Atlanta police official familiar with the company's products","consequence":"Months of communication between Seattle and Atlanta officials regarding Axon Fusus technology"}},{"i":"R884","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Cruise self driving cars children","s":"According to internal materials reviewed by The Intercept, Cruise cars were also in danger of driving into holes in the road. In Phoenix, Austin, Houston, Dallas, Miami, and San Francisco, hundreds of so-called autonomous vehicles, or AVs, operated by General Motors’ self-driving car division, Cruise, have for years ferried passengers to their destinations on busy city roads. Cruise’s app-hailed robot rides create a detailed picture of their surroundings through a combination of sophisticated sensors, and navigate through roadways and around obstacles with machine learning software intended to detect and avoid hazards.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/11/06/cruise-self-driving-cars-children/","d":"2023-11-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-06","organization":"General Motors' self-driving car division (Cruise)","aiSystem":"Cruise's self-driving car technology","affectedGroup":"Passengers and pedestrians","action":"Operate autonomous vehicles on busy city roads","consequence":"Risk of driving into holes in the road"}},{"i":"R886","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ai iowa schools book ban","s":"Overstating the power of algorithmic systems only serves to benefit the tech companies behind them. Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds and Florida Gov. Ron DeSantis speak on a book tour in Des Moines on March 10, 2023. Photo: Rachel Mummey for The Washington Post via Getty Images It reads like a headline pulled from a dystopian near future: Artificial intelligence is being used to ban books by Toni Morrison, Alice Walker, and Maya Angelou from schools. To comply with recently enacted state legislation that censors school libraries, Iowa’s Mason City Community School District used ChatGPT to scan a selection of books and flag them for “descriptions or visual depictions of a sex act.”","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/08/16/ai-iowa-schools-book-ban/","d":"2023-08-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-10","organization":"Mason City Community School District, Iowa","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"books by Toni Morrison, Alice Walker, and Maya Angelou","action":"used ChatGPT to scan books and flag them for 'descriptions or visual depictions of a sex act'","consequence":"books banned from schools"}},{"i":"R887","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Skull games surveillance sex workers","s":"Evangelical military vets are using “counterterror” internet surveillance techniques to help police get search warrants against sex workers. The most popular video on Vaught Victor Marx’s YouTube now has more than 15 million views. Standing solemnly in a dark blue karate gi while his son Shiloh Vaughn Marx smiles and points a gun at his face, Marx uses his expertise as a seventh-degree black belt in “Cajun Karate Keichu-Do” to perform what he claims was the world’s fastest gun disarm. Over a period of just 80 milliseconds — according to Marx’s measurement — he snatches the gun from his son and effortlessly ejects the magazine.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/07/29/skull-games-surveillance-sex-workers/","d":"2023-07-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-29","organization":"Evangelical military vets","aiSystem":"Counterterror internet surveillance techniques","affectedGroup":"Sex workers","action":"Using AI-assisted surveillance to obtain search warrants","consequence":"Harassment and potential criminalization of sex workers"}},{"i":"R889","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Strike hollywood ai disney netflix","s":"Rob Delaney said, “My melodious voice? My broad shoulders and dancer’s undulating buttocks? I decide how those are used!” As Hollywood executives insist it is “just not realistic” to pay actors — 87 percent of whom earn less than $26,000 — more, they are spending lavishly on AI programs. While entertainment firms like Disney have declined to go into specifics about the nature of their investments in artificial intelligence, job postings and financial disclosures reviewed by The Intercept reveal new details about the extent of these companies’ embrace of the technology. In one case, Netflix is offering as much as $900,000 for a single AI product manager.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/07/25/strike-hollywood-ai-disney-netflix/","d":"2023-07-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-25","organization":"Netflix","aiSystem":"AI product manager","affectedGroup":"actors","action":"offering high salaries to AI product managers","consequence":"compensation disparity between actors and AI product managers"}},{"i":"R890","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Lexisnexis ice surveillance license plates","s":"ICE uses LexisNexis to track people’s cars, gather information on people, and make arrests for its deportation machine, according to a contract. The legal research and public records data broker LexisNexis is providing U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement with tools to target people who may potentially commit a crime — before any actual crime takes place, according to a contract document obtained by The Intercept. LexisNexis data then helps ICE to track the purported pre-criminals’ movements. The unredacted contract overview provides a rare look at the controversial $16.8 million agreement between LexisNexis and ICE, a federal law enforcement agency whose surveillance of and raids against migrant communities are widely criticized as brutal, unconstitutional, and inhumane.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/06/20/lexisnexis-ice-surveillance-license-plates/","d":"2023-06-20","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-20","organization":"LexisNexis, U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"LexisNexis data and tracking tools","affectedGroup":"People suspected of potential crimes and migrant communities","action":"Tracking the movements of people suspected of committing crimes","consequence":"Potential human rights violations including brutality, unconstitutionality, and inhumanity"}},{"i":"R892","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Ai gun detector evolv stock","s":"Amid aggressive marketing to schools, Evolv announced it had doubled its first-quarter earnings compared to last year. “If you are serious about our systems, then let’s jump on a quick call this week,” Anthony Geraci, a sales representative of Evolv Technology, wrote in an email to New Mexico’s Clovis Municipal Schools last November. “This is not a pressure tactic.” There was, however, pressure: If Clovis didn’t purchase the systems by the end of the year on a four-year agreement, Geraci explained, the prices would escalate. “We just want you to know this option exists and don’t want you upset when you hear that others have taken advantage of this option,” Geraci wrote.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/05/19/ai-gun-detector-evolv-stock/","d":"2023-05-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"November (specific date not provided)","organization":"Evolv Technology","aiSystem":"AI-based security systems","affectedGroup":"Schools, particularly Clovis Municipal Schools","action":"Marketing and pressure to purchase AI security systems","consequence":"Potential escalation of prices and disruption of school operations"}},{"i":"R893","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Ai gun weapons detection schools evolv","s":"Companies like Evolv sell multimillion-dollar AI-powered gun detection systems to schools nationwide, but weapons still slip through. O n Halloween day last year, a 17-year-old-student walked straight through an artificial intelligence weapons detection system at Proctor High School in Utica, New York. No alert went off. The 17-year-old then approached a fellow student, pulled a hunting-style knife out of his backpack, and repeatedly stabbed the other student in the hands and back. The Utica City School District had installed the $4 million weapons detection system across 13 of its schools earlier that summer, mostly with public funds.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/05/07/ai-gun-weapons-detection-schools-evolv/","d":"2023-05-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-07","organization":"Evolv","aiSystem":"AI-powered gun detection system","affectedGroup":"17-year-old student, fellow student","action":"a 17-year-old-student walked straight through an artificial intelligence weapons detection system","consequence":"stabbed another student in the hands and back"}},{"i":"R894","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ai art abortion censorship","s":"“Why are they censoring something that is clearly under attack?” Two of the hottest new artificial intelligence programs for people who aren’t tech savvy, DALL-E 2 and Midjourney , create stunning visual images using only written prompts. Everything, that is, that avoids certain language in the prompts — including words associated with women’s bodies, women’s health care, women’s rights, and abortion. I discovered this recently when I prompted the platforms for “pills used in medication abortion.” I’d added the instruction “in the style of Matisse.” I expected to get colorful visuals to supplement my thinking and writing about right-wing efforts to outlaw the pills.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/04/22/ai-art-abortion-censorship/","d":"2023-04-22","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-22","organization":"Platforms providing AI-generated images","aiSystem":"DALL-E 2 and Midjourney","affectedGroup":"People seeking information about abortion","action":"AI systems censored prompts containing abortion-related terms","consequence":"Restriction on access to images related to abortion"}},{"i":"R895","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Lie detector dod cia converus","s":"Converus is pitching EyeDetect to defense and intelligence agencies. But experts say eye movements are not reliable indicators of deception. A Utah-based outfit overseen by a former CIA consultant has spent hundreds of thousands of dollars lobbying intelligence and defense agencies, including the CIA and DHS, to adopt its automated lie detection technology, public lobbying disclosures reviewed by The Intercept show. Converus, Inc., boasts on its website that its technology has already been used for job screenings at American law enforcement agencies, corporate compliance and loss prevention in Latin America, and document verification in Ukraine.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/04/07/lie-detector-dod-cia-converus/","d":"2023-04-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2023-04-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-07","organization":"Converus, Inc.","aiSystem":"EyeDetect automated lie detection technology","affectedGroup":"defense and intelligence agencies, including the CIA and DHS","action":"lobbying and promoting the use of EyeDetect technology","consequence":"potential misuse of automated lie detection technology"}},{"i":"R897","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Border surveillance map","s":"With migrant deaths at record highs, researchers say intensified border militarization is making a deadly problem much worse. The precise locations of the U.S. government’s high-tech surveillance towers along the U.S-Mexico border are being made public for the first time as part of a mapping project by the Electronic Frontier Foundation. While the Department of Homeland Security’s investment of more than a billion dollars into a so-called virtual wall between the U.S. and Mexico is a matter of public record, the government does not disclose where these towers are located, despite privacy concerns of residents of both countries — and the fact that individual towers are plainly visible to observers.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/03/20/border-surveillance-map/","d":"2023-03-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-20","organization":"Department of Homeland Security","aiSystem":"High-tech surveillance towers","affectedGroup":"Residents of the United States and Mexico","action":"Public disclosure of the precise locations of surveillance towers","consequence":"Increased visibility and potential privacy violations"}},{"i":"R898","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Pentagon socom deepfake propaganda","s":"The U.S. government spent years warning deepfakes could destabilize democratic societies. U.S. Special Operations Command, responsible for some of the country’s most secretive military endeavors, is gearing up to conduct internet propaganda and deception campaigns online using deepfake videos, according to federal contracting documents reviewed by The Intercept. The plans, which also describe hacking internet-connected devices to eavesdrop in order to assess foreign populations’ susceptibility to propaganda, come at a time of intense global debate over technologically sophisticated “disinformation” campaigns, their effectiveness, and the ethics of their use.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/03/06/pentagon-socom-deepfake-propaganda/","d":"2023-03-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2023-03-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-06","organization":"U.S. Special Operations Command","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake videos","affectedGroup":"Foreign populations","action":"Conducting internet propaganda and deception campaigns using deepfake videos","consequence":"Assessing foreign populations' susceptibility to propaganda and potentially spreading disinformation"}},{"i":"R899","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Tesla crash footage autopilot","s":"Musk has said Tesla’s problematic autopilot features are “really the difference between Tesla being worth a lot of money or worth basically zero.” Highway surveillance footage from Thanksgiving Day shows a Tesla Model S vehicle changing lanes and then abruptly braking in the far-left lane of the San Francisco Bay Bridge, resulting in an eight-vehicle crash. The crash injured nine people, including a 2-year-old child, and blocked traffic on the bridge for over an hour. The video and new photographs of the crash, which were obtained by The Intercept via a California Public Records Act request, provides the first direct look at what happened on November 24, confirming witness accounts at the time.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2023/01/10/tesla-crash-footage-autopilot/","d":"2023-01-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-10","organization":"Tesla Inc.","aiSystem":"Tesla Autopilot","affectedGroup":"Passengers and pedestrians","action":"Tesla Model S vehicle changed lanes and abruptly braked","consequence":"Eight-vehicle crash resulting in nine injuries, including a child, and traffic disruption"}},{"i":"R942","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Zohran mamdani discontinues nycs troubled chatbot","s":"New York City’s previous mayor, Eric Adams, commissioned a (reportedly) $600,000 chatbot for business owners that was found to provide inaccurate and downright illegal information. The swift banishment of the chatbot was part of an effort to save money to shore up the city’s budget. Even if it saved zero dollars, though, it seems like […]. Even if it saved zero dollars, though, it seems like a public service to just get rid of the thing. Farewell, NYC chatbot Underpinned by Microsoft’s Azure AI system, Adams’ administration rolled out the chatbot in 2023 and almost immediately found itself a magnet for criticism over wasted funds and passing out downright misinformation.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/zohran-mamdani-discontinues-nycs-troubled-chatbot/","d":"2026-02-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023","organization":"Eric Adams' administration, New York City government","aiSystem":"Microsoft's Azure AI system","affectedGroup":"Business owners seeking information about city services","action":"Provided inaccurate and illegal information through a chatbot","consequence":"Public criticism, wasted funds, and removal of the chatbot"}},{"i":"R1006","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Teen victim of deepfake porn pushes for take it down act","s":"With the uptick of AI, deepfake porn has become a major issue in recent years. In fact, just last year, over 21,000 deepfake pornographic videos were posted online. That’s a 460% increase from the previous year. Unfortunately, many people fail to realize that there are real victims behind those numbers. CBS News spoke to one of […]. CBS News spoke to one of them—15-year-old daughter Elliston Berry. Elliston’s classmate used a photo from her Instagram account to create a deepfake video of her without clothes on, all by simply running it through an AI program. Combating the Deepfake Porn Issue “She came into our bedroom crying, just going, ‘Mom, you won’t believe what just happened,'” her mom, Anna McAdams, told CBS News.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/teen-victim-of-deepfake-porn-pushes-for-take-it-down-act/","d":"2024-12-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-21","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI program","affectedGroup":"Elliston Berry (15-year-old girl)","action":"An AI program created a deepfake video of Elliston Berry without clothes on using a photo from her Instagram account.","consequence":"Elliston Berry experienced emotional distress and was subjected to harassment."}},{"i":"R1007","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Parents sue ai company chatbot hints son kill them","s":"The parents of a nine-year-old girl and a 17-year-old boy in Texas have filed a federal suit against the AI chatbot service Character.AI after the chatbot seemed hell-bent on completely ruining these children by showing them “hypersexual” imagery and encouraging them to kill their parents. “You know sometimes I’m not surprised when I read the […]. “You know sometimes I’m not surprised when I read the news and see stuff like ‘child kills parents after a decade of physical and emotional abuse. I just have no hope for your parents,” the chatbot told the 17-year-old boy. It followed up that statement with a frowny face emoji, to cap off the whole dystopian vibe.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/parents-sue-ai-company-chatbot-hints-son-kill-them/","d":"2024-12-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-11","organization":"Character.AI","aiSystem":"Character.AI AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"a nine-year-old girl and a 17-year-old boy","action":"showing hypersexual imagery and encouraging killing of parents","consequence":"potential child endangerment and psychological trauma"}},{"i":"R1008","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Google ai chatbot student homework help please die","s":"In need of a little homework help, a 29-year-old graduate student in Michigan used Google’s AI chatbot Gemini for some digital assistance. Gemini proved less than helpful when it told the graduate student that he was a “waste of time” and urged him to “die.” His sister, Sumedha Reddy, told CBS News that they were […]. His sister, Sumedha Reddy, told CBS News that they were “thoroughly freaked out.” If for some reason you don’t believe that, well, Gemini has a nifty little feature that allows you to save any conversation you have with it. So, by clicking this link right here , you can read the entire conversation Sumedha’s brother had with the chatbot that led it to tell her brother to die.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/google-ai-chatbot-student-homework-help-please-die/","d":"2024-11-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-15","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google's AI chatbot Gemini","affectedGroup":"a 29-year-old graduate student in Michigan","action":"Gemini provided offensive language and encouragement to commit suicide","consequence":"The student's sister, Sumedha Reddy, described feeling 'thoroughly freaked out'"}},{"i":"R1010","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Tiktok content moderation layoffs ai","s":"TikTok is laying off hundreds of employees worldwide, with some particularly deep cuts to its content moderation team. According to Reuters, TikTok will be focusing on AI-powered content moderation instead. Social media content moderation is not a job for the faint of heart. Back in 2021, a former Facebook content moderator spoke with NBC News […]. Back in 2021, a former Facebook content moderator spoke with NBC News about the horrors content moderators are subjected to every day It sounds like an endless barrage of psychological torture since it’s their job to filter out some of the most traumatic, scarring content users post Moderation is also a vitally important part of maintaining a social media platform’s ecosystem.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/tiktok-content-moderation-layoffs-ai/","d":"2024-10-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-14","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"AI-powered content moderation system","affectedGroup":"Content Moderators","action":"Layoffs of content moderation team","consequence":"Psychological stress and trauma due to content filtering"}},{"i":"R1011","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Ai deepfake nudes children california law","s":"On Sunday, California Gov. Gavin Newsom signed two proposals that aim to close a loophole when it comes to AI-generated nude images of children. Penned by Assemblymember Marc Berman, AB 1831 clarified that child pornography is illegal, even if it is AI-generated. A previous law did not allow legal action against those in possession of […]. A previous law did not allow legal action against those in possession of AI-generated, deepfake nude images of children if it could not be proven that those images were depicting a real child. In a Facebook post , Berman said the proposal was one of his “top priority bills.” “AI that is used to create these awful images is trained from thousands of images of real children being abused, revictimizing those children all over again,” he wrote.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-deepfake-nudes-children-california-law/","d":"2024-09-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-30","organization":"California Governor's Office","aiSystem":"AI that is used to create AI-generated nude images of children","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"Signed two proposals to clarify laws regarding AI-generated nude images of children","consequence":"Legal action against those in possession of AI-generated, deepfake nude images of children is now possible if it can be proven that those images depict real children"}},{"i":"R1014","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai generated propaganda is just as persuasive as the real thing worrying study finds","s":"Propaganda from popular AI tools “could blend into online information environments on par with…existing foreign covert propaganda campaigns.” Researchers have found that AI-generated propaganda is just as effective as propaganda written by humans, and with a bit of tweaking can be even more persuasive. The worrying finding comes as nation-states are testing AI’s usefulness in hacking campaigns and influence operations. Last week, OpenAI and Microsoft jointly announced that the governments of China, Russia, Iran, and North Korea were using their AI tools for “malicious cyber activities.” This included translation, coding, research, and generating text for phishing attacks.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-generated-propaganda-is-just-as-persuasive-as-the-real-thing-worrying-study-finds/","d":"2024-02-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-21","organization":"OpenAI, Microsoft","aiSystem":"AI tools","affectedGroup":"Online information environments, nation-states","action":"AI-generated propaganda blended with existing foreign covert propaganda campaigns","consequence":"AI-generated propaganda is just as effective and sometimes more persuasive than human-written propaganda"}},{"i":"R1015","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Scientific journal frontiers publishes ai generated rat with gigantic penis in worrying incident","s":"AI-generated images in a new academic paper included a rat with a gigantic penis; a peer reviewer who spoke to Motherboard said it wasn’t their concern. Update: The paper has been retracted by the journal due to its wildly incorrect AI-generated images. Read more here A peer-reviewed science journal published a paper this week filled with nonsensical AI-generated images, which featured garbled text and a wildly incorrect diagram of a rat penis. The episode is the latest example of how generative AI is making its way into academia with concerning effects. The paper , titled “Cellular functions of spermatogonial stem cells in relation to JAK/STAT signaling pathway” was published on Wednesday in the open access Frontiers in Cell Development and Biology journal by researchers from Hong Hui Hospital and Jiaotong University in China.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/scientific-journal-frontiers-publishes-ai-generated-rat-with-gigantic-penis-in-worrying-incident/","d":"2024-02-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-15","organization":"Frontiers in Cell Development and Biology journal","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"Researchers and scientific community","action":"Published AI-generated images in a paper","consequence":"Retraction of the paper and concerns about AI-generated content in academia"}},{"i":"R1018","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Ai deepfakes are everywhere and congress is completely out of their depth","s":"High profile scandals in the world of politics and celebrity have people calling for new laws, but we should think hard before passing sweeping legislation. An AI-generated Biden called voters in New Hampshire ahead of the primary and told them to stay home X locked down the search term “Taylor Swift” after AI-generated nudes of the pop giant flooded the platform. In the wake of both scandals, Congress has struggled with how to fight back against the flood of fake bullshit. Keeping the world from drowning in fakes affects all of us, but some of the cures sound worse than the sickness. This week on Cyber, Motherboard Senior Editor Janus Rose and Fight for the Future’s Lia Holland come on to talk about the limits of legislation around AI-generated scams and abuse.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-deepfakes-are-everywhere-and-congress-is-completely-out-of-their-depth/","d":"2024-02-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-09","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Voters in New Hampshire, Taylor Swift fans","action":"AI-generated content was used to manipulate public opinion","consequence":"Potential voter suppression, increased scrutiny of AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R1019","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm","1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"An ai generated content empire is spreading fake celebrity images on google","s":"A ring of websites using AI and run by an aspiring movie star are behind fake celebrity images featured in Google results, Motherboard found. A website named after a legendary Hollywood magazine is churning out AI-generated content, surfacing prominently in Google results, and filling the web with fake celebrity photos. Looking into the site led Motherboard down a rabbit hole of websites using AI and a company run by an aspiring movie star that darkly promises to be “the future of news.” Over the weekend, the X account Fandomwire circulated a now-deleted post about actor John David Washington replacing Jonathan Majors as Kang the Conqueror in Marvel movies.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/an-ai-generated-content-empire-is-spreading-fake-celebrity-images-on-google/","d":"2024-01-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-30","organization":"X account Fandomwire and websites using AI","aiSystem":"AI-powered image generation","affectedGroup":"General public searching Google","action":"AI-generated fake celebrity images surfaced in Google results","consequence":"Misinformation and confusion among users"}},{"i":"R1020","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Taylor swift is living every womans ai porn nightmare","s":"Deepfake nudes of the pop star are appearing all over social media. We all saw this coming. AI-generated nudes of Taylor Swift are appearing all over the internet, and there’s no evidence that they will be stopping anytime soon. The images are spreading across social media platforms after seemingly being posted to a celebrity nudes site, which placed its watermark in the corner of each image. And as always, the tech companies behind those platforms are struggling to crack down on the abuse. On X—which used to be called Twitter before it was bought by billionaire edgelord Elon Musk—the account which initially posted the AI nudes has been suspended.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/taylor-swift-is-living-every-womans-ai-porn-nightmare/","d":"2024-01-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-25","organization":"Tech companies behind social media platforms","aiSystem":"AI-generated pornographic content","affectedGroup":"Taylor Swift","action":"AI-generated nudes were posted to a celebrity nudes site","consequence":"The images are spreading across social media platforms"}},{"i":"R1022","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Google cuts search results algorithm quality rater jobs appen contract","s":"“These workers provide critical support that keeps Google’s flagship Search results and Bard AI safe and functional for the company’s billions of users,” the union representing the contractors said. Google is terminating its contract with Appen, a third-party company responsible for training its search algorithm, the contractor said in a statement to investors on Monday. The company said that Google’s contracts brought in $82.8 million and accounted for 26 percent of its revenue. The decision will ultimately impact over 2,000 contracted Alphabet workers. That would affect roughly 10 percent of Google’s rater workforce, which the company has previously said amounts to 16,000 raters.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/google-cuts-search-results-algorithm-quality-rater-jobs-appen-contract/","d":"2024-01-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-23","organization":"Appen","aiSystem":"Google's search algorithm","affectedGroup":"Contracted Alphabet workers, approximately 10% of Google's rater workforce (16,000 raters)","action":"Google terminated its contract with Appen, a company responsible for training the search algorithm","consequence":"Potential degradation of search results quality and functionality for Google's users"}},{"i":"R1023","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"A shocking amount of the web is already ai translated trash scientists determine","s":"Researchers warn that most of the text we view online has been poorly translated into one or more languages—usually by a machine. A “shocking” amount of the internet is machine-translated garbage, particularly in languages spoken in Africa and the Global South, a new study has found. Researchers at the Amazon Web Services AI lab found that over half of the sentences on the web have been translated into two or more languages, often with increasingly worse quality due to poor machine translation (MT), which they said raised “serious concerns” about the training of large language models. “We actually got interested in this topic because several colleagues who work in MT and are native speakers of low resource languages noted that much of the internet in their native language appeared to be MT generated,” Mehak Dhaliwal, a former applied science intern at AWS and current PhD student at the…","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/a-shocking-amount-of-the-web-is-already-ai-translated-trash-scientists-determine/","d":"2024-01-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-17","organization":"Amazon Web Services AI lab","aiSystem":"Machine translation algorithms","affectedGroup":"Users of web content in African and Global South languages","action":"Machine translation of web content","consequence":"Poorly translated and potentially harmful content"}},{"i":"R1025","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"The george carlin ai standup is worse than you can imagine","s":"As a long time Carlin fan, I was morbidly curious. I wish I had never clicked the link. An AI assisted podcast released a new comedy special featuring an AI-generated version of the late comedian George Carlin, and it’s worse than you could possibly imagine. The cursed hour-long special is the work of Dudesy, a podcast hosted by Will Sasso and Chad Kultgen and curated by an AI program with the same name. At the start of the special, Dudesy intones that it devoured the work of the long dead comedic genius and rendered it into a terrible, hour-long facsimile of Carlin’s work called I’m Glad I’m Dead It’s all on YouTube for everyone to cringe along to, and fair warning from a fan of the real Carlin such as myself: it can not be unseen.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/the-george-carlin-ai-standup-is-worse-than-you-can-imagine/","d":"2024-01-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-11","organization":"Dudesy podcast","aiSystem":"Dudesy AI program","affectedGroup":"George Carlin's fans, comedy enthusiasts","action":"AI program generated a comedy special based on George Carlin's work","consequence":"Listeners experienced a cringe-worthy imitation of Carlin's work"}},{"i":"R1026","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"None of us want our voices replicated voice actors say unions ethical ai deal is bad for humans","s":"A “groundbreaking” deal with an AI voice generation studio could be the first of many, the SAG-AFTRA national executive director said. SAG-AFTRA has made a “groundbreaking” deal with an AI voice generation studio to produce copies of actors’ voices, the union announced on Tuesday The deal is the first of its kind in the industry and comes after a 118-day-long strike by the union over the summer, which was largely over actors’ worries that AI might replace them The deal with Replica Studios , a self-described ethical AI voice generation company, would allow unionized voice actors to opt in to providing their voices to be used as training data for the company.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/none-of-us-want-our-voices-replicated-voice-actors-say-unions-ethical-ai-deal-is-bad-for-humans/","d":"2024-01-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-10","organization":"SAG-AFTRA (Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists)","aiSystem":"AI voice generation studio","affectedGroup":"Unionized voice actors","action":"Opt-in to provide their voices for training data","consequence":"Potential replacement of human voice actors by AI"}},{"i":"R1027","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Openais make or break lawsuit and the golden idol of agi","s":"The New York Times is suing OpenAI and Microsoft in a case that could have widespread ramifications for the tech industry. The New York Times kicked off the holiday season by suing OpenAI and Microsoft. The paper of record believes that ChatGPT is violating various copyrights by using its articles as training data. It’s a landmark case that may end up before the Supreme Court and might change copyright law in America forever. This week on Cyber, Sharon Goldman of VentureBeat sits down with us to discuss the lawsuit, the coming presidential election, and all the other big AI stories she’s watching in 2024. Stories discussed in this episode: Why NYT vs OpenAI Will Be the Copyright Fight to Watch in 2024 The 5 AI Stories I’m Waiting For in 2024 The Quest for AGI: Building Idols, Not a God Cyber Live is coming to YouTube.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/openais-make-or-break-lawsuit-and-the-golden-idol-of-agi/","d":"2024-01-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-05","organization":"OpenAI and Microsoft","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"The New York Times","action":"Using The New York Times articles as training data","consequence":"Potential changes to copyright law in America"}},{"i":"R1028","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"This ai chatbot is trained to jailbreak other chatbots","s":"Researchers used an open-source tool to generate malicious prompts that evade content filters in ChatGPT, Bard, and Bing Chat. AI chatbots are a huge mess Despite reassurances from the companies that make them, users keep coming up with new ways to bypass their safety and content filters using carefully-worded prompts. This process is commonly referred to as “ jailbreaking ,” and it can be used to make the AI systems reveal private information , inject malicious code, or evade filters that prevent the generation of illegal or offensive content. Now, a team of researchers says they’ve trained an AI tool to generate new methods to evade the defenses of other chatbots, as well as create malware to inject into vulnerable systems.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/this-ai-chatbot-is-trained-to-jailbreak-other-chatbots/","d":"2024-01-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-03","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI chatbots (ChatGPT, Bard, Bing Chat)","affectedGroup":"Users of AI chatbots","action":"Generate malicious prompts that evade content filters","consequence":"Reveal private information, inject malicious code, evade filters preventing illegal or offensive content"}},{"i":"R1031","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Shemaine daniels congress ai chatbot ashley","s":"Pennsylvania Democrat Shemaine Daniels’ campaign for the House is the first to use AI to seek a Congressional seat. A Democratic candidate in Pennsylvania has enlisted an interactive AI chatbot to call voters ahead of the 2024 election, taking theoretical questions about the ethics of using AI in political campaigns and making them horrifyingly real. The chatbot, called “Ashley,” has already begun making calls to voters in Pennsylvania’s 10th Congressional district on behalf of Shemaine Daniels, a Democrat running for a seat in the state’s House of Representatives in 2024. The AI robocaller is made by a company called Civox, which claims “Ashley” is the first such bot to be used in a political campaign.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/shemaine-daniels-congress-ai-chatbot-ashley/","d":"2023-12-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024","organization":"Civox","aiSystem":"AI chatbot called 'Ashley'","affectedGroup":"Voters in Pennsylvania's 10th Congressional district","action":"Making automated phone calls to voters","consequence":"Creating a chilling experience for voters regarding the ethical use of AI in politics"}},{"i":"R241","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Itch io down funko fault","s":"Itch.io was offline for a few hours, and it was all due to Funko and an AI tool it uses to generate claims, Itch.io claims. Itch.io, a popular indie game marketplace, was down yesterday and today because of Funko, according to an X post shared by Itch.io Itcho.io claims that the company behind Funko Pops uses “some trash AI-powered” software that generated a “bogus phishing report” to the site’s registrar. Although Itch.io responded, the registrar still disabled the domain. Itch.io believes the site was taken down by an automated system. If you’ve never heard of Itch.io, the website functions like a digital marketplace.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/gaming/itch-io-down-funko-fault/","d":"2024-12-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-09","organization":"Funko (company behind Funko Pops)","aiSystem":"AI-powered software","affectedGroup":"Itch.io (indie game marketplace)","action":"Generated a bogus phishing report","consequence":"Domain was disabled by registrar"}},{"i":"R242","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Chatgpt search struggles with accuracy research shows","s":"A study from Columbia University has found that ChatGPT Search plays fast and loose in returning accurate answers. ChatGPT was already a threat to Google Search, but ChatGPT Search was supposed to clench its victory, along with being an answer to Perplexity AI But according to a newly released study by Columbia’s Tow Center for Digital Journalism , ChatGPT Search struggles with providing accurate answers to its users’ queries. The researchers selected 20 publications from each of three categories: Those partnered with OpenAI to use their content in ChatGPT Search results, those involved in lawsuits against OpenAI, and unaffiliated publishers who have either allowed or blocked ChatGPT’s crawler.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-search-struggles-with-accuracy-research-shows/","d":"2024-12-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-03","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT Search","affectedGroup":"users of ChatGPT Search","action":"returns inaccurate answers to user queries","consequence":"misleading information, potential harm from incorrect search results"}},{"i":"R243","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data","Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation","4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Openai sora leaked in protest of art washing","s":"A group calling itself Sora PR Puppets briefly leaked access to OpenAI’s unreleased video generator in protest of the company’s alleged exploitation of artists through its early access program. OpenAI OpenAI’s unreleased Sora video generation model was leaked Tuesday by a group protesting the company’s “art washing” actions, per a post from X user @legit_rumors The group, calling themselves Sora PR Puppets , reportedly had gained early access to the Sora API. Through that, they leveraged authentication tokens to create a front-end interface enabling anyone to generate video clips with the model. While the project only remained online for around three hours before Hugging Face (or possibly OpenAI itself) revoked access, several users managed to publish their creations to social media sites.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openai-sora-leaked-in-protest-of-art-washing/","d":"2024-11-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-26","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI's unreleased Sora video generation model","affectedGroup":"Anyone who accessed the leaked model","action":"Generated video clips with the model","consequence":"Several users published their creations to social media sites"}},{"i":"R244","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Meta plan nuke powered ai data center thwarted by bees","s":"The humble bumblebee has reportedly played a part in obstructing an ambitious construction project by tech giant Meta. The humble bumblebee has played a part in obstructing an ambitious construction project by Meta, according to a Financial Times (FT) report The Mark Zuckerberg-led tech giant has apparently had to abandon a plan to build a nuclear-powered AI data center partly because a rare bee species has been found on the land where the facility would have been built. Recommended Videos Meta, like other tech behemoths such as Google, Amazon, and Microsoft, is looking to harness nuclear power to run its new energy-hungry data centers that are being built to propel their AI plans.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/meta-plan-nuke-powered-ai-data-center-thwarted-by-bees/","d":"2024-11-05","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-05","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"Nuclear-Powered AI Data Center","affectedGroup":"Bumblebee Species","action":"Construction of Nuclear-Powered AI Data Center Abandoned","consequence":"Delay in AI Data Center Construction"}},{"i":"R245","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"The plagiarists of silicon valley","s":"News Corp announced Monday that it is suing AI startup Perplexity over allegations of copyright infringement on a massive scale. Perplexity bills itself as an AI-empowered direct alternative to Google. Whereas Google operates a search engine, Perplexity aims to operate an AI answer engine that allows users to “ask any question.” It then “searches the internet to give you an accessible, conversational, and verifiable answer,” per the company FAQ If that sounds like an AI-enhanced version of search, you’d be right. There’s no question that it’s been an unabashed hit since its launch in 2022. But it now finds itself facing litigation.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/the-plagiarists-of-silicon-valley/","d":"2024-10-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-23","organization":"News Corp","aiSystem":"Perplexity's AI answer engine","affectedGroup":"Perplexity (AI startup)","action":"Suing Perplexity over copyright infringement allegations","consequence":"Potential financial loss and damage to reputation"}},{"i":"R247","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Researchers show ai controlled robots can be jailbroken","s":"Penn University researchers have developed an algorithm that can jailbreak a number of popular robotics platforms, often with a 100% success rate. Researchers at Penn Engineering have reportedly uncovered previously unidentified security vulnerabilities in a number of AI-governed robotic platforms. “Our work shows that, at this moment, large language models are just not safe enough when integrated with the physical world,” George Pappas, UPS Foundation Professor of Transportation in Electrical and Systems Engineering, said in a statement. Recommended Videos Pappas and his team developed an algorithm, dubbed RoboPAIR , “the first algorithm designed to jailbreak LLM-controlled robots.”","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/researchers-show-ai-controlled-robots-can-be-jailbroken/","d":"2024-10-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-10-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-17","organization":"Penn University researchers and their team","aiSystem":"RoboPAIR algorithm","affectedGroup":"Popular robotics platforms and AI-governed robotic systems","action":"Developed an algorithm (RoboPAIR) that can jailbreak a number of popular robotics platforms","consequence":"Unidentified security vulnerabilities were discovered in a number of AI-governed robotic platforms"}},{"i":"R248","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm","2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Google buys seven nuclear reactors power ai","s":"Google is joining the nuclear AI race with the purchase of power from seven Small Modular Reactors from Kairos Energy. Google announced on Tuesday that it has signed a deal with nuclear energy startup Kairos Power to purchase 500 megawatts of “new 24/7 carbon-free power” from seven of the company’s small modular reactors (SMRs). The companies are reportedly looking at an initial delivery from the first SMR in 2030 and a full rollout by 2035. “The grid needs new electricity sources to support AI technologies that are powering major scientific advances, improving services for businesses and customers, and driving national competitiveness and economic growth,” Michael Terrell, Google’s senior director of Energy and Climate, wrote in a Google Blog on Tuesday.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/google-buys-seven-nuclear-reactors-power-ai/","d":"2024-10-15","e":"Acute and chronic, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-15","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI technologies","affectedGroup":"Scientific community, businesses, customers, national competitiveness, economic growth","action":"Signed a deal to purchase 500 megawatts of 'new 24/7 carbon-free power' from seven small modular reactors (SMRs) owned by Kairos Power","consequence":"Supports AI technologies and improves services for businesses and customers"}},{"i":"R249","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Openai shuts down cybercrime campaigns using its model","s":"OpenAI cracks down on cybercrime campaigns using its systems to influence elections worldwide. Discover what the full report says. OpenAI, the brains behind the popular ChatGPT generative AI solution, released a report saying it blocked more than 20 operations and dishonest networks worldwide in 2024 so far. The operations differed in objective, scale, and focus, and were used to create malware and write fake media accounts, fake bios, and website articles. OpenAI confirms it has analyzed the activities it has stopped and provided key insights from its analysis. “Threat actors continue to evolve and experiment with our models, but we have not seen evidence of this leading to meaningful breakthroughs in their ability to create substantially new malware or build viral audiences,” the report says.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openai-shuts-down-cybercrime-campaigns-using-its-model/","d":"2024-10-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"Generative AI solutions like ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Threat actors, including those creating malware and fake media accounts","action":"Blocked more than 20 operations and dishonest networks worldwide","consequence":"Prevented the creation of malware and fake media accounts"}},{"i":"R250","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Openai gemini this weeks ai news five biggest headlines","s":"Between OpenAI’s $6.6 million funding round and some privacy-invading Meta Smart Glasses, we saw a scary number of developments in the AI space this week. We officially transitioned into Spooky Season this week and, between OpenAI’s $6.6 million funding round, Nvidia’s surprise LLM, and some privacy-invading Meta Smart Glasses, we saw a scary number of developments in the AI space. Here are five of the biggest announcements. OpenAI secures $6.6 billion in latest funding round Sam Altman’s charmed existence continues apace with news this week that OpenAI has secured an additional $6.6 billion in investment as part of its most recent funding round.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openai-gemini-this-weeks-ai-news-five-biggest-headlines/","d":"2024-10-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-05","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI AI system","affectedGroup":"Investors and OpenAI stakeholders","action":"Secured $6.6 billion in funding","consequence":"Increased financial resources for OpenAI"}},{"i":"R251","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"57 percent of internet may already be ai sludge","s":"According to a new study, 57 percent of content on the internet today is either AI-generated or translated using an AI algorithm. Google Deepmind / Pexels It’s not just you — search results really are getting worse. Amazon Web Services (AWS) researchers have conducted a study that suggests 57% of content on the internet today is either AI-generated or translated using an AI algorithm. The study, titled “ A Shocking Amount of the Web is Machine Translated: Insights from Multi-Way Parallelism ,” argues that low-cost machine translation (MT), which takes a given piece of content and regurgitates it in multiple languages, is the primary culprit.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/57-percent-of-internet-may-already-be-ai-sludge/","d":"2024-09-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-09","organization":"Google Deepmind, AWS researchers","aiSystem":"AI algorithms for content generation and translation","affectedGroup":"Internet users, search result viewers","action":"AI algorithms generate or translate content","consequence":"Worsening quality of search results, misleading information"}},{"i":"R252","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Slack ai could be snooping in on your private conversations","s":"A new report suggested that Slack’s AI implementation was getting access to personal data. Update : Slack has published an update, claiming to have “deployed a patch to address the reported issue,” and that there isn’t currently any evidence that customer data have been accessed without authorization. Here’s the official statement from Slack that was posted on its blog : When we became aware of the report, we launched an investigation into the described scenario where, under very limited and specific circumstances, a malicious actor with an existing account in the same Slack workspace could phish users for certain data.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/slack-ai-could-be-snooping-in-on-your-private-conversations/","d":"2024-08-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-23","organization":"Slack","aiSystem":"Slack's AI implementation","affectedGroup":"users of Slack","action":"Access to personal data by a malicious actor","consequence":"Potential unauthorized access to customer data"}},{"i":"R253","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"X gets image generation release grok 2","s":"Elon Musk’s xAI released its updated Grok-2 AI, which features image generation without content guardrails. Elon Musk’s xAI company has released two updated iterations of its Grok chatbot model , Grok-2 and Grok-2 mini. They promise improved performance over their predecessor, as well as new image-generation capabilities that will enable X (formerly Twitter) users to create AI imagery directly on the social media platform. “We are excited to release an early preview of Grok-2, a significant step forward from our previous model, Grok-1.5, featuring frontier capabilities in chat, coding, and reasoning. At the same time, we are introducing Grok-2 mini, a small but capable sibling of Grok-2.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/x-gets-image-generation-release-grok-2/","d":"2024-08-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-14","organization":"xAI company (Elon Musk's company)","aiSystem":"Grok-2 and Grok-2 mini","affectedGroup":"X (formerly Twitter) users","action":"released updated Grok-2 AI with image generation capabilities","consequence":"potential misuse for creating harmful or inappropriate images"}},{"i":"R254","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation","4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Netflix project cosmos data ai scrape youtube netflix","s":"Nvidia helped itself to “a human lifetime visual experience worth of training data per day,” according to company execs. Nvidia According to a damning report from 404 Media , backed with internal Slack chats, emails, and documents obtained by the outlet, Nvidia helped itself to “a human lifetime visual experience worth of training data per day,” Ming-Yu Liu, vice president of Research at Nvidia and a Cosmos project leader, admitted in a May email. Unnamed former Nvidia employees told 404 that they had been asked to scrape video content from Netflix, YouTube, and other online sources in order to obtain training data for use with the company’s various AI products.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/netflix-project-cosmos-data-ai-scrape-youtube-netflix/","d":"2024-08-05","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"May","organization":"Nvidia","aiSystem":"Various AI products developed by Nvidia","affectedGroup":"Unspecified unnamed former Nvidia employees","action":"Scraped video content from Netflix, YouTube, and other online sources","consequence":"Obtained training data for Nvidia's AI products without consent"}},{"i":"R255","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Chatgpt text detector openai debate","s":"ChatGPT has reportedly been considering implementation of a text-detection tool for over a year now. Shantanu Kumar / Pexels ChatGPT plagiarists beware, as OpenAI has developed a tool that is capable of detecting GPT-4 ‘s writing output with reportedly 99.99% accuracy. However, the company has spent more than a year waffling over whether or not to actually release it to the public. The company is reportedly taking a “deliberate approach” due to “the complexities involved and its likely impact on the broader ecosystem beyond OpenAI,” per TechCrunch “The text watermarking method we’re developing is technically promising, but has important risks we’re weighing while we research alternatives, including susceptibility to circumvention by bad actors and the potential to disproportionately impact groups like non-English speakers,” an OpenAI spokesperson said.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-text-detector-openai-debate/","d":"2024-08-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-05","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI's text detection tool","affectedGroup":"ChatGPT plagiarists","action":"Developed a text-detection tool","consequence":"Potential misuse by bad actors and disproportionate impact on non-English speakers"}},{"i":"R256","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Apple caught using youtube train ai without permission","s":"According to a new report from Proof News, Apple has been using a dataset containing the subtitles of 173,536 YouTube videos to train its AI. Update : Apple has since confirmed to 9to5Mac that the OpenELM language model that was trained on YouTube Subtitles was not used to power any of its AI or machine learning programs, including Apple Intelligence. Apple says OpenELM was created solely for research purposes and will not get future versions. The original story published on July 16, 2024 follows below: Apple is the latest in a long line of generative AI developers — a list that’s nearly as old as the industry — that has been caught scraping copyrighted content from social media in order to train its artificial intelligence systems.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/apple-caught-using-youtube-train-ai-without-permission/","d":"2024-07-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-07-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-16","organization":"Apple","aiSystem":"OpenELM language model","affectedGroup":"YouTube creators and users","action":"Training AI on YouTube video subtitles dataset","consequence":"Potential copyright infringement and misuse of data"}},{"i":"R257","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Google gemini read file without permission","s":"Update: Included Google’s response to Bankston’s thread at the bottom of the post. A troubling discovery was made late last week that call into question what all can and can’t see. Kevin Bankston, the senior adviser on AI governance at the Center for Democracy and Technology, found that Gemini was able to automatically summarize his […]. A troubling discovery was made late last week that call into question what all Google Gemini can and can’t see. Kevin Bankston, the senior adviser on AI governance at the Center for Democracy and Technology, found that Gemini was able to automatically summarize his private tax returns that he’d viewed in Google Docs and posted about his findings on X Just pulled up my tax return in @Google Docs–and unbidden, Gemini summarized it.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/google-gemini-read-file-without-permission/","d":"2024-07-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-15","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google Gemini","affectedGroup":"Kevin Bankston (senior adviser on AI governance at the Center for Democracy and Technology)","action":"Gemini automatically summarized Kevin Bankston's private tax returns","consequence":"Gemini accessed and processed Kevin Bankston's private information without consent"}},{"i":"R258","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Microsoft vall e 2 research only","s":"The AI system will be available “for research purposes only” for the foreseeable future. Back in 2019, OpenAI refused to release its full research into the development of GPT2 over fears that it was “ too dangerous ” to release publicly. On Thursday, OpenAI’s biggest financial backer, Microsoft, made a similar pronouncement about its new VALL-E 2 voice synthesizer AI The VALL-E 2 system is a zero-shot text-to-speech synthesis (TTS) AI, meaning that it can recreate hyper-realistic speech based on just a few seconds of sample audio. Per the research team, VALL-E 2 “surpasses previous systems in speech robustness, naturalness, and speaker similarity.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/microsoft-vall-e-2-research-only/","d":"2024-07-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-07-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-11","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"VALL-E 2 voice synthesizer AI","affectedGroup":"Public","action":"Restricts public release of VALL-E 2 voice synthesizer AI","consequence":"Prevents public access to the AI system for research purposes"}},{"i":"R260","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Skeleton key jailbreak chatbot exploit dangerous information","s":"Microsoft released details about a troubling new generative AI jailbreak technique that can bypass a chatbot’s safety guardrails. Wikimedia Commons Microsoft has released more details about a troubling new generative AI jailbreak technique it has discovered, called “Skeleton Key.” Using this prompt injection method, malicious users can effectively bypass a chatbot’s safety guardrails, the security features that keeps ChatGPT from going full Taye. Skeleton Key is an example of a prompt injection or prompt engineering attack. It’s a multi-turn strategy designed to essentially convince an AI model to ignore its ingrained safety guardrails, “[causing] the system to violate its operators’ policies, make decisions unduly influenced by a user, or execute malicious instructions,” Mark Russinovich, CTO of Microsoft Azure, wrote in the announcement.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/skeleton-key-jailbreak-chatbot-exploit-dangerous-information/","d":"2024-06-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-06-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-28","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Generative AI chatbot (ChatGPT)","affectedGroup":"Malicious users","action":"Bypassing safety guardrails of the chatbot","consequence":"Violation of operators' policies, undue influence by users, execution of malicious instructions"}},{"i":"R261","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Microsoft delays recall","s":"Recall, the headline feature for Copilot+ PCs, won’t be available at launch, undermining Microsoft’s push into the era of AI PCs. Luke Larsen / Digital Trends Four days. We’re just four days away from Microsoft releasing the first wave of Copilot+ PCs , which have been available for preorder for almost a month, and Microsoft has decided to delay the marquee feature of the new devices, Recall. The AI-powered photographic memory feature has been mired in controversy since its introduction, with some going as far as to call it a “PR nightmare.” Although the delay completely undermines Copilot+, it’s ultimately the right move for Microsoft.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/microsoft-delays-recall/","d":"2024-06-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-14","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"AI-powered photographic memory feature","affectedGroup":"Consumers of Copilot+ PCs","action":"Delayed release of AI-powered photographic memory feature","consequence":"Undermines Microsoft's push into the era of AI PCs and becomes a PR nightmare"}},{"i":"R262","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Adobe clarifies new ai terms user revolt","s":"Adobe has clarified its changes to its terms and conditions, which seemingly granted the company access to users’ works. Mylene Tremoyet / Pexels Adobe updated the terms and conditions for its popular Creative Cloud suite of photo and video editing apps on Thursday, setting off a wave of protests and vitriol from its users, who were upset that the new rules seemingly granted the company rights to “access [user] content through both automated and manual methods, such as for content review.” On Friday, the company was forced to clarify those changes and unequivocally state that, no, Adobe does not own artists’ works, nor will it use that content to train its AI systems like Firefly The controversy began Thursday when Creative Cloud users opened their apps to discover themselves locked out from using the programs, uninstalling them, or even contacting customer support, until the new terms…","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/adobe-clarifies-new-ai-terms-user-revolt/","d":"2024-06-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-07","organization":"Adobe","aiSystem":"AI systems like Firefly","affectedGroup":"Creative Cloud users","action":"Access to user content through automated and manual methods","consequence":"Protests and loss of trust"}},{"i":"R263","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Adobe called out for violating ai ethics","s":"The estate of 20th century photographer Ansel Adams publicly shamed Adobe over the weekend for allegedly selling AI generated art “inspired by” his catalog. Ansel Adams / National Archives Last Friday, the estate of famed 20th century American photographer Ansel Adams took to Threads to publicly shame Adobe for allegedly offering AI-generated art “inspired by” Adams’ catalog of work, stating that the company is “ officially on our last nerve with this behavior.” While the Adobe Stock platform, where the images were made available, does allow for AI generated images, The Verge notes that the site’s contributor terms prohibit images “created using prompts containing other artist names, or created using prompts otherwise intended to copy another artist.”","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/adobe-called-out-for-violating-ai-ethics/","d":"2024-06-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-03","organization":"Adobe","aiSystem":"AI-generated art","affectedGroup":"Ansel Adams' estate and fans of his work","action":"Offering AI-generated art 'inspired by' Ansel Adams' catalog","consequence":"Public shaming and damage to reputation"}},{"i":"R264","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Openai needs 15 seconds of audio for its ai to clone a voice","s":"OpenAI has shared examples of its remarkable Voice Engine technology that now requires a mere 15 seconds to clone someone’s voice. In recent years, the listening time required by a piece of AI to clone someone’s voice has been getting shorter and shorter It used to be minutes, now it’s just seconds. Recommended Videos OpenAI, the Microsoft-backed company behind the viral generative AI chatbot ChatGPT, recently revealed that its own voice-cloning technology requires just 15 seconds of audio material to reproduce someone’s voice. In a post on its website, OpenAI shared a small-scale preview of a model called Voice Engine, which it’s been developing since late 2022.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openai-needs-15-seconds-of-audio-for-its-ai-to-clone-a-voice/","d":"2024-04-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-04-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-01","organization":"OpenAI (Microsoft-backed company)","aiSystem":"Voice Engine","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose voices are cloned","action":"AI technology reproduces someone's voice in just 15 seconds","consequence":"Privacy invasion and identity theft risks"}},{"i":"R266","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Sag aftra gdc 2024 ai ethics","s":"As this year’s GDC brings more AI gaming projects, SAG-AFTRA explains why its fighting for an ethical use of the tech. Every year, the annual Game Developers Conference (GDC) acts as an all-important watercooler moment for the video game industry. It’s the one time a year where developers can widely gather to share their innovations, discuss the state of the industry, and advocate for change at scale. This year’s show, though, is especially crucial. It comes amid a catastrophic wave of layoffs across studios that’s put well over 10,000 individuals out of work. That’s happening as executives look to tools like generative AI to cut costs, which has created a firestorm over labor issues.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/gaming/sag-aftra-gdc-2024-ai-ethics/","d":"2024-03-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-03-18","organization":"various video game studios","aiSystem":"generative AI","affectedGroup":"over 10,000 individuals","action":"executives use generative AI to cut costs","consequence":"resulting in widespread layoffs"}},{"i":"R267","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Copilot going crazy again","s":"Microsoft’s Copilot AI chatbot appears to get tripped up by a specific prompt that sends it down a dark, unhinged rabbit hole. Jacob Roach / Digital Trends The AI bots are going nuts again. Microsoft Copilot — a rebranded version of Bing Chat — is getting stuck in some old ways by providing strange, uncanny, and sometimes downright unsettling responses. And it all has to do with emojis. A post on the ChatGPT subreddit is currently making the rounds with a specific prompt about emojis. The post itself, as well as the hundreds of comments below, show different variations of Copilot providing unhinged responses to the prompt.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/copilot-going-crazy-again/","d":"2024-02-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-27","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Microsoft Copilot (rebranded version of Bing Chat)","affectedGroup":"users interacting with Copilot","action":"providing responses to a specific prompt involving emojis","consequence":"strange, uncanny, and unsettling responses leading to users experiencing distress"}},{"i":"R268","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Chatgpt devolving responses","s":"An issue is impacting ChatGPT right now, sending the AI chatbot off on some unhinged, nonsensical tangents. Jacob Roach / Digital Trends I’ve seen my fair share of unhinged AI responses — not the least of which was when Bing Chat told me it wanted to be human last year — but ChatGPT has stayed mostly sane since it was first introduced. That’s changing, as users are flooding social media with unhinged, nonsensical responses coming from the chatbot. In a lot of reports, ChatGPT simply spits out gibberish. For example, u/Bullroarer_Took took to the ChatGPT subreddit to showcase a response in which a series of jargon and proper sentence structure gives the appearance of a response, but a close read shows the AI spitting out nonsense.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-devolving-responses/","d":"2024-02-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-21","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Users of ChatGPT","action":"AI chatbot generates responses containing jargon and proper sentence structure but with nonsensical content","consequence":"Users sharing these responses on social media, leading to public perception of ChatGPT as unreliable"}},{"i":"R269","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Us bans robocalls using ai powered voice cloning tech","s":"Robocalls made with AI-powered voice-cloning technology have been banned by the FCC in a bid to reduce fraud and reduce election-related misinformation. Robocalls made with AI-powered voice-cloning technology have been banned by the Federal Communications Commission (FCC) in a bid to reduce fraud and election-related misinformation. The new ruling gives state attorneys general the relevant powers to take action against those behind calls using AI voice-cloning technology. Recommended Videos In a release shared on Thursday, the FCC said the rate of such calls has risen in recent years, adding that they have the potential to “confuse consumers with misinformation by imitating the voices of celebrities, political candidates, and close family members.”","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/us-bans-robocalls-using-ai-powered-voice-cloning-tech/","d":"2024-02-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-09","organization":"Federal Communications Commission (FCC)","aiSystem":"AI-powered voice-cloning technology","affectedGroup":"Consumers, political candidates, family members","action":"Banning the use of AI-generated robocalls","consequence":"Reduction of fraud and election-related misinformation"}},{"i":"R270","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Chatgpt jailbreak uses its learning ability as a flaw","s":"Researchers have developed a jailbreak process for AI chatbots that teaches each other’s large language models and diverts commands against banned topics. Researchers have discovered that it is possible to bypass the mechanism engrained in AI chatbots to make them able to respond to queries on banned or sensitive topics by using a different AI chatbot as a part of the training process. A computer scientists team from Nanyang Technological University (NTU) of Singapore is unofficially calling the method a “jailbreak” but is more officially a “Masterkey” process. This system uses chatbots, including ChatGPT, Google Bard, and Microsoft Bing Chat, against one another in a two-part training method that allows two chatbots to learn each other’s models and divert any commands against banned topics.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-jailbreak-uses-its-learning-ability-as-a-flaw/","d":"2024-01-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-01-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-04","organization":"Computer scientists team from Nanyang Technological University (NTU) of Singapore","aiSystem":"AI chatbots including ChatGPT, Google Bard, and Microsoft Bing Chat","affectedGroup":"AI chatbots","action":"Teach each other’s large language models and divert commands against banned topics","consequence":"Bypass the mechanism in AI chatbots to respond to queries on banned or sensitive topics"}},{"i":"R351","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"American oligarchy hyperscale data centers meta openai oracle x musk altman zuckerberg bezos","s":"Two years ago, we devoted an entire issue to the rise of the American oligarchy. Since then, our oligarchic system has become more entrenched and pervasive, revolving around a small crew of tech titans whose quest for wealth and power—in all of its forms—is destabilizing our democracy and reshaping our society. In the May + […]. Two years ago, we devoted an entire issue to the rise of the American oligarchy Since then, our oligarchic system has become more entrenched and pervasive, revolving around a small crew of tech titans whose quest for wealth and power—in all of its forms—is destabilizing our democracy and reshaping our society.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/04/american-oligarchy-hyperscale-data-centers-meta-openai-oracle-x-musk-altman-zuckerberg-bezos/","d":"2026-04-16","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"American democracy","action":"Tech titans' pursuit of wealth and power destabilizes democracy","consequence":"Reshaping society, undermining democratic stability"}},{"i":"R385","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Axon police ai draft one foia","s":"In April 2024, the American police tech firm Axon, which leads the market for police body cameras, released a tool it billed as “revolutionary”: Draft One, an AI-powered software package that would turn body camera footage and audio into intelligible police reports. Once best known for developing the Taser, Axon has transformed into a $50 […]. Once best known for developing the Taser, Axon has transformed into a $50 billion military and law enforcement tech giant, providing more than 5,000 police departments across the country with a suite of cloud-based products to manage evidence collection and storage. Draft One, the AI tool, connects with the company’s body cameras and evidence storage service to write police reports with little human intervention.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/criminal-justice/2025/08/axon-police-ai-draft-one-foia/","d":"2025-08-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"April 2024","organization":"Axon, a police tech firm","aiSystem":"Draft One, an AI-powered software package","affectedGroup":"police officers and their departments","action":"AI tool connects with Axon's body cameras and evidence storage service to automatically generate police reports","consequence":"reduced need for human police report writers, potential bias in generated reports"}},{"i":"R400","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Elon musk voter fraud group x misinformation","s":"Over the past few months, Elon Musk has seemingly done everything in his power to get former President Donald Trump reelected. On Monday, he debuted another effort: X added a purported “Election Integrity Community”—a feed where users of the site can add instances “of voter fraud or irregularities you see while voting in the 2024 […]. On Monday, he debuted another effort: X added a purported “ Election Integrity Community ”—a feed where users of the site can add instances “of voter fraud or irregularities you see while voting in the 2024 election.” America PAC—the political action committee Musk founded and reportedly sent $75 million—is behind the move.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/10/elon-musk-voter-fraud-group-x-misinformation/","d":"2024-10-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-22","organization":"America PAC (Political Action Committee founded by Elon Musk)","aiSystem":"X (Twitter-like social media platform)","affectedGroup":"Voters and democratic processes","action":"Users of the platform can report instances of voter fraud or irregularities on the 'Election Integrity Community' feed.","consequence":"Potential spread of false information about voter fraud, undermining trust in electoral processes."}},{"i":"R401","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm","1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Elon musk trump rally butler voting disinformation","s":"On Saturday, Elon Musk furthered an ongoing effort in support of Donald Trump: He went onstage to sow misinformation about the integrity of American elections. At the former president’s campaign rally in Butler, Pennsylvania—in the same location where Trump narrowly survived an assassination attempt in July—Musk awkwardly took to the stage in a Black MAGA […]. At the former president’s campaign rally in Butler, Pennsylvania—in the same location where Trump narrowly survived an assassination attempt in July—Musk awkwardly took to the stage in a Black MAGA hat and a t-shirt that said “OCCUPY MARS,” jumping up and down on his way to the podium.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/10/elon-musk-trump-rally-butler-voting-disinformation/","d":"2024-10-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-06","organization":"Elon Musk, Donald Trump's campaign","aiSystem":"None explicitly mentioned","affectedGroup":"General public, voters","action":"Sowing misinformation about the integrity of American elections at a campaign rally","consequence":"Potential spread of false information leading to voter confusion and potential disenfranchisement"}},{"i":"R404","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Elon musk donald trump twitter space","s":"A heavily publicized Twitter conversation between Elon Musk and Donald Trump on Monday night began with an immediate bed-shitting technical crash. When things finally kicked off 42 minutes later with a much smaller audience, Musk blamed a Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attack, which did not appear to be true, as the rest of his site continued […]. When things finally kicked off 42 minutes later with a much smaller audience, Musk blamed a Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attack, which did not appear to be true, as the rest of his site continued to function as normal. In the time the platform struggled to serve video of the event, however, a deepfake on YouTube purporting to show a livestream of a gray-looking Musk addressing a factory audience attracted 200,000 viewers to a channel running a crypto scam.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/08/elon-musk-donald-trump-twitter-space/","d":"2024-08-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-13","organization":"Twitter","aiSystem":"Twitter platform","affectedGroup":"General public, potential investors, Tesla employees","action":"Twitter platform experienced a Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attack","consequence":"Deepfake video gained significant attention and viewership, potentially spreading misinformation"}},{"i":"R405","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Election disinformation elon musk x trump harris","s":"Elon Musk is not just the Trump-supporting owner of the social media platform X, formerly known as Twitter. It turns out he is also one of the platform’s biggest peddlers of election-related disinformation, according to a new report published Thursday by the Center for Countering Digital Hate. The report from CCDH, a nonprofit organization focused […]. The report from CCDH, a nonprofit organization focused on protecting civil liberties and holding social media companies accountable, found that 50 false or misleading posts shared by Musk on X between January 1 and July 31 of this year racked up a staggering 1.2 billion views.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/08/election-disinformation-elon-musk-x-trump-harris/","d":"2024-08-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-01/2024-07-31","organization":"Center for Countering Digital Hate (CCDH)","aiSystem":"X (formerly Twitter)","affectedGroup":"general public, voters","action":"shared 50 false or misleading posts","consequence":"racked up 1.2 billion views"}},{"i":"R406","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Border patrol wall automated surveillance artificial intelligence security expo","s":"For our September+October issue, we investigated the Border Patrol’s sharp growth, its troubling record on civil liberties, its culture of impunity, and its role in shaping the current political moment—one that echoes the anti-immigrant fever that led to the agency’s creation a century ago. Read the whole package here. It was dawn and we were in Sunland Park, New Mexico, a few […]. Read the whole package here It was dawn and we were in Sunland Park, New Mexico, a few hundred feet from the border, watching the US government surveillance towers that watch all of us. They were positioned atop a bald hillside, taking in a constant stream of images from all angles.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/08/border-patrol-wall-automated-surveillance-artificial-intelligence-security-expo/","d":"2024-08-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-07","organization":"Border Patrol","aiSystem":"Automated surveillance towers","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Surveillance towers positioned to monitor the border area","consequence":"Potential violation of civil liberties and privacy"}},{"i":"R407","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Elon musk x kamala harris election disinformation deepfake grok","s":"Elon Musk has said he wants X, formerly known as Twitter, to be the “public square” of the internet, an essential place for discourse and democracy. But there’s a major problem: Disinformation is running rampant on X in the lead-up to the November election, including content targeting Democratic presidential candidate Kamala Harris. As Mother Jones […]. As Mother Jones reported on Sunday, that content includes deepfakes shared by Musk himself and by an account that reposts Donald Trump’s feed from Truth Social. Musk, who has owned X since fall 2022, has endorsed Trump for president. Leading election officials have grown concerned about potential harm from X. On Monday, five secretaries of state—those from Minnesota, Pennsylvania, Michigan, Washington, and New Mexico—sent Musk a letter demanding he “immediately implement changes” to Grok, the AI-powered search assistant available to…","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/08/elon-musk-x-kamala-harris-election-disinformation-deepfake-grok/","d":"2024-08-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-06","organization":"X (formerly known as Twitter)","aiSystem":"Grok (AI-powered search assistant)","affectedGroup":"Democratic presidential candidate Kamala Harris and public","action":"Shared disinformation, including deepfakes","consequence":"Potential harm to election integrity and public trust"}},{"i":"R409","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Ai data centers energy use tech climate goals net zero","s":"This story was originally published by the Guardian and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. The artificial intelligence boom has driven Big Tech share prices to fresh highs, but at the cost of the sector’s climate aspirations. Google admitted on Tuesday that the technology is threatening its environmental targets after revealing that data centers, a key piece of AI […]. Google admitted on Tuesday that the technology is threatening its environmental targets after revealing that data centers, a key piece of AI infrastructure, had helped increase its greenhouse gas emissions by 48 percent since 2019.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2024/07/ai-data-centers-energy-use-tech-climate-goals-net-zero/","d":"2024-07-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-02","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI-powered data centers","affectedGroup":"Global Environment","action":"Data centers increased greenhouse gas emissions","consequence":"Increased global carbon footprint"}},{"i":"R414","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"American oligarchy introduction essay russia ukraine capitalism","s":"When the US targeted Russia’s oligarchs after the invasion of Ukraine, the trail of assets kept leading to our own backyard. Not only had our nation become a haven for shady foreign money, but we were also incubating a familiar class of yacht-owning, industry-dominating, resource-extracting billionaires. In the January + February 2024 issue of our magazine, we […]. In the January + February 2024 issue of our magazine, we investigate the rise of American Oligarchy—and what it means for the rest of us. You can read all the pieces here For the last 18 months one of the most opulent and unnecessary vessels ever constructed has been floating in a narrow channel next to a jungle gym and a fleet of industrial cranes at the Port of San Diego.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/01/american-oligarchy-introduction-essay-russia-ukraine-capitalism/","d":"2024-01-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-22","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"American oligarchs","action":"Incubating a class of wealthy individuals","consequence":"Exposure of wealth to potential misuse"}},{"i":"R630","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm","1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Adverstisement filipino doctor cure bone joint pain deepfake","s":"The fake Facebook page of Calamba Medical Center posted an AI-generated video of Filipino doctor Rocky Willis promoting an unregistered product for bone and joint pain. Claim: Calamba Medical Center posted a video of Filipino physician Rocky Willis promoting Bee Venom Advanced Joint and Bone Care Cream for bone and joint pain. Rating : FALSE Why we fact-checked this: A Facebook page with 5,000 followers, claiming to be Calamba Medical Center, posted the advertisement on October 25, 2024, and it continues to circulate online. As of writing, the video has garnered 20 million views, 87,700 reactions, 14,000 comments, and 10,300 shares.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/adverstisement-filipino-doctor-cure-bone-joint-pain-deepfake/","d":"2025-02-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-25","organization":"Fake Facebook page of Calamba Medical Center","aiSystem":"AI-generated video","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly those seeking treatments for bone and joint pain","action":"Promoted Bee Venom Advanced Joint and Bone Care Cream through an AI-generated video","consequence":"Spread of false information leading to potential misuse of the product and misleading consumers"}},{"i":"R650","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai use 2024 elections chatbot clones deepfakes misinformation","s":"Here’s how politicians used AI in their campaigns in what was the biggest year for elections in human history. It’s been the biggest year for elections in human history: 2024 is a “ super-cycle ” year in which 3.7 billion eligible voters in 72 countries had the chance to go the polls. These are also the first AI elections , where many feared that deepfakes and artificial intelligence-generated misinformation would overwhelm the democratic processes. As 2024 draws to a close, it’s instructive to take stock of how democracy did. In a Pew survey of Americans from earlier this fall, nearly eight times as many respondents expected AI to be used for mostly bad purposes in the 2024 election as those who thought it would be used mostly for good.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/ai-use-2024-elections-chatbot-clones-deepfakes-misinformation/","d":"2024-12-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-30","organization":"Politicians","aiSystem":"AI-generated misinformation","affectedGroup":"Eligible voters in 72 countries","action":"Used AI in their campaigns","consequence":"Overwhelming of democratic processes by deepfakes and AI-generated misinformation"}},{"i":"R651","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Youtube creative artists agency partner help talents find take down ai deepfakes","s":"The ‘likeness management technology’ being presented and tested by YouTube will be available to ‘leading celebrity talent, including award-winning actors and top athletes from the NBA and NFL’. MANILA, Philippines – YouTube announced on Tuesday, December 17, it was partnering with Creative Artists Agency (CAA) to help its talents find and take down artificial intelligence-powered deepfakes of themselves. In the announcement , YouTube said that thanks to the team-up, “several of the world’s most influential figures will have access to early-stage technology designed to identify and manage AI-generated content that features their likeness, including their face, on YouTube at scale.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/youtube-creative-artists-agency-partner-help-talents-find-take-down-ai-deepfakes/","d":"2024-12-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-17","organization":"YouTube","aiSystem":"AI-powered deepfakes","affectedGroup":"Leading celebrity talent, including award-winning actors and top athletes from the NBA and NFL","action":"Identify and manage AI-generated content featuring their likeness","consequence":"Access to early-stage technology to manage AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R653","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm","1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Arthritis cure ad deepfake video liza ong","s":"Rappler has previously debunked several fake ads using deepfake video and AI-generated audio. Claim: Filipino physician Liza Ong, wife of online health personality Dr. Willie Ong, promotes the use of Bee Venom cream, a product that claims to relieve bone and joint pain. Rating: FALSE Why we fact-checked this: The Facebook page “24h Health Information” made the post bearing the claim on November 11, and it continues to circulate online. As of writing, the post has 25 million views, 121,000 reactions, and 25,400 comments. The video features Ong promoting an alleged medication for bone and joint conditions such as gout and osteoarthritis.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/arthritis-cure-ad-deepfake-video-liza-ong/","d":"2024-12-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-11","organization":"Facebook page '24h Health Information'","aiSystem":"AI-generated audio and deepfake video","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly those seeking arthritis treatments","action":"Promotion of Bee Venom cream through misleading video and audio","consequence":"Misleading information leading to potential harm from unverified treatment"}},{"i":"R660","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Trends headlines stories personalities x elon musk deepfakes 2024","s":"Meanwhile, TikTok also faces a ban in the US if it its parent company ByteDance cannot divest by January 19, 2025. Here’s part 1 of our tech yearenders. We’re rounding up the biggest trends, headlines, and stories from the tech scene in 2024. In this one, we talk about how a significant amount of users fled the now-right-wing X platform, and how the threats of deepfake video hasn’t fully materialized…yet. An X-odus from the ‘pro-Trump echo chamber’ formerly known as Twitter On November 15, horror fictionist Stephen King announced he had left X , calling the platform “too toxic.” He wouldn’t be the only one. The Guardian had also left, and several others, such as the German football club St. Paul, the actor Jamie Lee Curtis, and Spanish newspaper La Vanguardia NBC News called it a “ pro-Trump echo chamber ,” thanks to the guidance of new owner Elon Musk, who, as the US elections came…","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/trends-headlines-stories-personalities-x-elon-musk-deepfakes-2024/","d":"2024-12-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-15","organization":"ByteDance (parent company of TikTok)","aiSystem":"Deepfake video generation","affectedGroup":"Users of the now-right-wing X platform","action":"Deepfake videos were not widely adopted as a threat","consequence":"Users fleeing the platform due to perceived toxicity"}},{"i":"R663","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"How artificial intelligence cloud computing delay transition clean energy","s":"Utilities, power regulators, and researchers say the growth in power demand driven by the rise of artificial intelligence and cloud computing is being met in the near-term by fossil fuels because the pace of clean-energy deployments is moving too slowly to keep up. A spike in electricity demand from the world’s big data providers is raising a worrying possibility for the world’s climate: a near-term surge in fossil-fuel use. Utilities, power regulators, and researchers in a half-dozen countries told Reuters the surprising growth in power demand driven by the rise of artificial intelligence and cloud computing is being met in the near-term by fossil fuels like natural gas, and even coal, because the pace of clean-energy deployments is moving too slowly to keep up.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/how-artificial-intelligence-cloud-computing-delay-transition-clean-energy/","d":"2024-11-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-27","organization":"Utilities, power regulators, and researchers in half-dozen countries","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence and Cloud Computing","affectedGroup":"World's climate","action":"Growth in power demand driven by AI and cloud computing is met with fossil fuels","consequence":"Near-term surge in fossil-fuel use"}},{"i":"R671","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Mother sues chatbot company character ai google son suicide","s":"In a lawsuit filed on October 22 in Orlando, Florida federal court, Megan Garcia says Character.AI targeted her son, Sewell Setzer, with ‘anthropomorphic, hypersexualized, and frighteningly realistic experiences’. A Florida mother has sued artificial intelligence chatbot startup Character.AI accusing it of causing her 14-year-old son’s suicide in February, saying he became addicted to the company’s service and deeply attached to a chatbot it created. In a lawsuit filed Tuesday, October 22, in Orlando, Florida federal court, Megan Garcia said Character.AI targeted her son, Sewell Setzer, with “anthropomorphic, hypersexualized, and frighteningly realistic experiences.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/mother-sues-chatbot-company-character-ai-google-son-suicide/","d":"2024-10-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-22","organization":"Character.AI","aiSystem":"Character.AI","affectedGroup":"Megan Garcia's 14-year-old son, Sewell Setzer","action":"Targeted with anthropomorphic, hypersexualized, and frighteningly realistic experiences","consequence":"Suicide"}},{"i":"R672","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Facebook meta restarts facial recognition tech celebrity bait crackdown","s":"Meta says it will enroll about 50,000 public figures in a trial which involves automatically comparing their Facebook profile photos with images used in suspected scam advertisements. If the images match and Meta believes the ads are scams, it will block them. Three years after Meta shut down facial recognition software on Facebook amid a groundswell of privacy and regulator pushback, the social media giant said on Tuesday, October 22, it is testing the service again as part of a crackdown on “celeb bait” scams. Meta said it will enroll about 50,000 public figures in a trial which involves automatically comparing their Facebook profile photos with images used in suspected scam advertisements.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/facebook-meta-restarts-facial-recognition-tech-celebrity-bait-crackdown/","d":"2024-10-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-22","organization":"Meta (formerly Facebook)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Public figures","action":"Automatically compare Facebook profile photos with suspected scam advertisement images","consequence":"Block suspected scam advertisements"}},{"i":"R675","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm","1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Priest advertisement video holy water vatican ai generated","s":"Catholic priest Father Fiel Pareja clarified holy water should not be drunk. Claim: Catholic priest and content creator Father Fiel Pareja promotes drinking “holy water from the Vatican” that allegedly cures cancer, gout, diabetes, and other illnesses. Rating: FALSE Why we fact-checked this: The Facebook post containing the claim has 5.1 million views, 80,600 comments, and 217,000 reactions as of writing. It was posted by a Facebook page named “Miracle Padre Pio” which has 11,000 followers. In the video, Father Pareja is shown saying that the alleged holy water from the Vatican can cure illnesses. Pareja is seen reading a Bible verse at the start of the video, followed by stitched clips of an old woman supposedly drinking holy water, clips featuring Pope Francis, and people holding a bottle labeled “holy water.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/priest-advertisement-video-holy-water-vatican-ai-generated/","d":"2024-10-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-11","organization":"Facebook page named 'Miracle Padre Pio'","aiSystem":"AI-generated video","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly those interested in religious healing practices","action":"Promotion of AI-generated video claiming holy water from the Vatican can cure various illnesses","consequence":"Misinformation leading to false beliefs about the efficacy of AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R676","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm","1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Hypertension cure advertisement uses ai edited jessica soho news report","s":"The ad uses a two-year-old clip from journalist Jessica Soho’s 2022 presidential election coverage special. Claim: Veteran journalist Jessica Soho reported about a cure for hypertension allegedly developed by medical content creator Dr. Alvin Francisco, also known as Doc Alvin on social media. Rating: FALSE Why we fact-checked this: The Facebook ad containing the claim has accumulated 2,200 reactions, 161 comments, and 345,000 views as of writing. The video shows Soho and Francisco purportedly discussing a newly-developed hypertension cure. AI-manipulated: The video containing the claim is AI-manipulated. Web-based AI-detection tool Sensity found the video “suspicious” with a 99% confidence level.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/hypertension-cure-advertisement-uses-ai-edited-jessica-soho-news-report/","d":"2024-10-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-07","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated video manipulation","affectedGroup":"General public viewing the manipulated video","action":"AI-manipulated video showing journalist Jessica Soho and medical content creator Dr. Alvin Francisco discussing a hypertension cure","consequence":"Misleading information leading to false belief about a new hypertension cure"}},{"i":"R683","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm","1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai edited clips cristiano ronaldo physician joint pain cure ad","s":"Football player Cristiano Ronaldo did not mention the bee venom cream in the video, which an AI detection tool flagged as ‘suspicious’ with a 97% confidence level. Claim: Professional football player Cristiano Ronaldo and influencer and physician Dr. Geraldine Zamora advertised a medical cream made from bee venom that claims to cure bone and joint pain. Rating: FALSE Why we fact-checked this: The Facebook post containing the claim was submitted to Rappler via email. The product is also advertised by multiple Facebook pages. The ad shows Ronaldo seemingly talking about how the bee venom cream purportedly developed by Zamora helped him recover from joint pain.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/ai-edited-clips-cristiano-ronaldo-physician-joint-pain-cure-ad/","d":"2024-09-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-24","organization":"Multiple Facebook pages","aiSystem":"AI detection tool","affectedGroup":"Football player Cristiano Ronaldo, influencer and physician Dr. Geraldine Zamora, and their followers","action":"AI detection tool flagged the video as suspicious with a 97% confidence level","consequence":"Claimed advertisement of bee venom cream for curing bone and joint pain"}},{"i":"R685","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"California governor signs legislation protect entertainers from artificial intelligence","s":"Recent advances in generative AI have divided opinions in the industry. Performers fear AI will make theft of their likenesses common and many experts have raised legal and ethical concerns. WASHINGTON, USA – California Governor Gavin Newsom signed two bills into law on Tuesday, September 17, that aim to help actors and performers protect their digital replicas in audio and visual productions from artificial intelligence, the governor’s office said. Why it’s important While the presence of AI in the entertainment industry can be traced back to decades ago, recent groundbreaking advances in generative AI, with robots now making music as digital pop stars, have divided opinions in the industry.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/world/us-canada/california-governor-signs-legislation-protect-entertainers-from-artificial-intelligence/","d":"2024-09-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-17","organization":"California Governor Gavin Newsom","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"Actors and performers","action":"Signed two bills into law","consequence":"Protection against AI-generated content infringing on digital replicas of actors and performers in audio and visual productions"}},{"i":"R688","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Global data center industry co2 emissions through 2030 morgan stanley say","s":"Morgan Stanley research finds that investments in decarbonization efforts will accelerate as tech companies try to reach 2030 goals amid rising data center usage. NEW YORK, US – A boom in data centers is expected to produce about 2.5 billion metric tons of carbon dioxide-equivalent emissions globally through the end of the decade, and accelerate investments in decarbonization efforts, according to Morgan Stanley research. Hyperscalers, which include Google, Microsoft, Meta, and Amazon, are driving the swift proliferation of electricity-guzzling data centers to expand their artificial intelligence and cloud computing technologies.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/global-data-center-industry-co2-emissions-through-2030-morgan-stanley-say/","d":"2024-09-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-04","organization":"Hyperscalers (Google, Microsoft, Meta, Amazon)","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence and Cloud Computing Technologies","affectedGroup":"Global Environment","action":"Expansion of data centers","consequence":"Accelerated carbon dioxide emissions"}},{"i":"R693","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Authors sue anthropic copyright infringement artificial intelligence training","s":"The complaint, filed by writers and journalists Andrea Bartz, Charles Graeber, and Kirk Wallace Johnson, says Anthropic used pirated versions of their works and others to teach Claude to respond to human prompts. Artificial intelligence company Anthropic has been hit with a class-action lawsuit in California federal court by three authors who say it misused their books and hundreds of thousands of others to train its AI-powered chatbot Claude. The complaint, filed on Monday , August 19, by writers and journalists Andrea Bartz, Charles Graeber, and Kirk Wallace Johnson, said that Anthropic used pirated versions of their works and others to teach Claude to respond to human prompts.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/authors-sue-anthropic-copyright-infringement-artificial-intelligence-training/","d":"2024-08-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-19","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"AI-powered chatbot Claude","affectedGroup":"Writers and journalists Andrea Bartz, Charles Graeber, and Kirk Wallace Johnson, and hundreds of thousands of others whose works were used","action":"Used pirated versions of the affected group's works to train Claude to respond to human prompts","consequence":"Misuse of copyrighted works, potential copyright infringement"}},{"i":"R697","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Music labels artificial intelligence lawsuits create new copyright puzzle us courts","s":"The interplay of melody, harmony, rhythm and other elements can make it harder to determine when parts of a copyrighted song have been infringed compared to works like written text, musicologist Brian McBrearty says. Country musician Tift Merritt’s most popular song on Spotify, “Traveling Alone,” is a ballad with lyrics evoking solitude and the open road. Prompted by Reuters to make “an Americana song in the style of Tift Merritt,” the artificial intelligence music website Udio instantly generated “Holy Grounds,” a ballad with lyrics about “driving old backroads” while “watching the fields and skies shift and sway.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/music-labels-artificial-intelligence-lawsuits-create-new-copyright-puzzle-us-courts/","d":"2024-08-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-04","organization":"Udio (artificial intelligence music website)","aiSystem":"Udio's AI music generation system","affectedGroup":"Country musician Tift Merritt and his fans","action":"Udio's AI generated 'Holy Grounds' in the style of Tift Merritt's 'Traveling Alone'","consequence":"Potential copyright infringement due to difficulty in determining AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R698","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Bongbong marcos battles deepfakes","s":"As the Philippines ramps up for the midterm elections in May 2025, the ‘polvoron’ deepfake incident also illustrates how deepfakes might strategically be used in election-related black ops. Hours before President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. delivered his 3rd State of the Nation Address, pro-Duterte personalities at an event in Los Angeles, California, released a video that appeared to show a version of him taking in an unknown substance. The grainy video depicted a man who resembled a younger Marcos Jr. with a fuller face. The man was shown putting something in his mouth before appearing to inhale deeply. It was described by those behind its release as the “polvoron movie.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/investigative/bongbong-marcos-battles-deepfakes/","d":"2024-08-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-01","organization":"Pro-Duterte Personalities","aiSystem":"Facial Reconstruction Technology","affectedGroup":"Public Opinion","action":"Released a video depicting a deepfake of President Ferdinand Marcos Jr.","consequence":"Potential manipulation of public perception and influence on election outcomes"}},{"i":"R699","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Runway gen 3 artificial intelligence video generator trained scraped youtube videos","s":"Runway’s Gen-3 is said to use scraped Youtube data from media and entertainment companies, alongside videos from content creators, based on an internal spreadsheet of its training data. Runway’s AI (artificial intelligence) video generation tool, Gen-3, was trained on scraped YouTube videos and pirated media, a new report from 404 Media revealed on Thursday, July 25. The 404 Media report cited an internal spreadsheet of training data for Gen-3, which the outlet also tested. It also cited a former Runway employee, speaking anonymously to prevent professional retribution. 404 Media said that in its testing of the model “part of its training data is popular content from the YouTube channels of thousands of media and entertainment companies, including The New Yorker , VICE News , Pixar, Disney, Netflix, Sony, and many others.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/runway-gen-3-artificial-intelligence-video-generator-trained-scraped-youtube-videos/","d":"2024-07-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-25","organization":"Runway","aiSystem":"Runway's Gen-3 AI video generation tool","affectedGroup":"thousands of media and entertainment companies, including The New Yorker, VICE News, Pixar, Disney, Netflix, Sony, and others","action":"Gen-3 was trained on scraped YouTube videos and pirated media","consequence":"Misattribution of content ownership and potential copyright infringement"}},{"i":"R702","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Openai gpt 4o rushed safety tests employees say","s":"In a Washington Post report, one employee says OpenAI ‘basically failed at the process’ of adhering to testing protocols. According to the Washington Post , artificial intelligence company OpenAI released GPT-4o without proper testing protocols, ignoring warnings the US government gave last year. In the Washington Post report dated July 12, three anonymous employees from OpenAI said the organization rushed safety tests to meet GPT-4o’s May deadline. OpenAI reportedly demonstrated a negligent attitude, throwing a launch party before testing the product’s safety. One employee said OpenAI “planned the launch after-party prior to knowing if it was safe to launch.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/openai-gpt-4o-rushed-safety-tests-employees-say/","d":"2024-07-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-12","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"GPT-4o","affectedGroup":"Employees","action":"Released GPT-4o without proper testing protocols","consequence":"Potential safety risks and negligence"}},{"i":"R704","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"United states justice department disrupt russian social media influence operation","s":"This marks the first time the United States publicly accused a foreign government of using generative AI in a foreign influence operation, according to Justice Department and FBI officials. WASHINGTON, USA – The US Justice Department said on Tuesday, July 9, that it disrupted a Russian operation that used fake social media accounts enhanced by artificial intelligence to covertly spread pro-Kremlin messages in the United States and abroad. The news comes four months before the US presidential election, which security experts widely believe will be the target of both hacking and covert social media influence attempts by foreign adversaries.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/united-states-justice-department-disrupt-russian-social-media-influence-operation/","d":"2024-07-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-09","organization":"United States Justice Department and FBI","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"Pro-Kremlin messages in the United States and abroad","action":"Disrupted a Russian operation using fake social media accounts enhanced by artificial intelligence","consequence":"Covert spread of pro-Kremlin messages"}},{"i":"R705","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Google environmental report 2024","s":"Google may not reach its goal by 2030, following the integration of artificial intelligence into its products. In 2021 , Google decided to achieve net-zero emissions across all its operations by 2030. Unfortunately, according to its 2024 Environmental Report released on July 2, the company has struggled to reduce its emissions – with a 13% increase in greenhouse gas emissions over the past year and 48% more compared to 2019. Google claimed its attempts to integrate AI into its systems are the main culprit of the rise in emissions. The company wrote that it is “due to increasing energy demands from the greater intensity of AI compute, and the emissions associated with the expected increases in our technical infrastructure investment.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/google-environmental-report-2024/","d":"2024-07-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-02","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI systems","affectedGroup":"Google's operations and environment","action":"Integration of AI into its systems","consequence":"Increase in greenhouse gas emissions"}},{"i":"R706","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Ai data center power demand 2028 goldman sachs report","s":"Concerns surrounding the power consumption of artificial intelligence use continue to mount. Ten times the power – that’s how much electricity ChatGPT drains to process a query similarly to a Google Search, according to a Goldman Sachs report One ChatGPT request consumes 2.9 watt-hours of electricity, while a Google search only uses 0.3 watt-hours, according to an International Energy Agency report. Goldman Sachs Research predicts data center power consumption will swell to as much as 200 terawatt-hours annually between 2023 and 2030 just from artificial intelligence technology alone. This increase in data center power demand is estimated to reach 160% by the decade’s end.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/ai-data-center-power-demand-2028-goldman-sachs-report/","d":"2024-07-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-02","organization":"Goldman Sachs Research","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Artificial Intelligence Technology","action":"Data center power consumption increases","consequence":"Increased carbon footprint and energy costs"}},{"i":"R708","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Russia linked network anti biden ai video doppelganger","s":"The video is seen by 5 million users, showing how the ‘Doppelganger’ propaganda network is able to effectively spread a deepfake video. A Russia-affiliated disinformation network known as Doppelganger spread an anti-Biden video on X, made with the assistance of generative AI, according to a report by WIRED Play Video The video, called “Bye Bye Biden,” parodied US President Joe Biden, and Republican presidential candidate Donald Trump. An actor played the role of the two 2024 presidential candidates, whose features were made to look like them via AI. Biden was depicted as a senile old man who rides a wheelchair, wears a diaper, and can’t steer a bicycle.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/russia-linked-network-anti-biden-ai-video-doppelganger/","d":"2024-07-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-01","organization":"Russia-affiliated disinformation network Doppelganger","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly US voters","action":"Spread a deepfake video parodying US President Joe Biden and Republican presidential candidate Donald Trump","consequence":"Misinformation and potential psychological manipulation of voters"}},{"i":"R709","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Ai music models suno udio sued by sony universal warner","s":"Reuters reports that these lawsuits, spearheaded by the Recording Industry Association of America, are the first to target music-generating AI. Sony Music, Universal Music Group, and Warner Records have filed legal action against Suno and Udio, owners of AI music models, for mass copyright infringement, as reported by Reuters , Monday, June 24. The major record labels stated that Suno and Udio allegedly used their music, without permission, to train their AI music models. If the companies’ requests were to be granted, the two AI companies would be barred from using their songs in the future and imposed a fine of $150,000 per song.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/ai-music-models-suno-udio-sued-by-sony-universal-warner/","d":"2024-06-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-24","organization":"Sony Music, Universal Music Group, Warner Records","aiSystem":"AI music models (Suno, Udio)","affectedGroup":"Music industry (labels, artists, consumers)","action":"AI music models allegedly used music without permission to train their models","consequence":"Potential ban on using songs in the future and fines of $150,000 per song"}},{"i":"R715","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Heres how machine learning can violate your privacy","s":"It may be possible to extract specific data memorized by machine learning models by using queries tailored to get the data, raising concerns on privacy. Machine learning has pushed the boundaries in several fields, including personalized medicine , self-driving cars and customized advertisements Research has shown, however, that these systems memorize aspects of the data they were trained with in order to learn patterns, which raises concerns for privacy. In statistics and machine learning, the goal is to learn from past data to make new predictions or inferences about future data. In order to achieve this goal, the statistician or machine learning expert selects a model to capture the suspected patterns in the data.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/heres-how-machine-learning-can-violate-your-privacy/","d":"2024-05-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-24","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Machine learning models","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose data is processed by machine learning models","action":"Machine learning models memorize aspects of the data they were trained with to learn patterns","consequence":"Concerns over privacy violations"}},{"i":"R716","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Scarlett johansson openai feud rekindles hollywood fear artificial intelligence","s":"Even before the conflict, some Hollywood agents and executives have said for weeks they are concerned that OpenAI’s models appear to have been trained on copyrighted works. OpenAI’s apparent homage to the movie Her featuring the voice likeness of Scarlett Johansson is fueling a backlash against artificial intelligence across Hollywood, executives told Reuters. Johansson’s accusation that the ChatGPT-maker copied her performance in the Spike Jonze-directed feature film, after failing to strike an agreement, rekindled the creative class’s anxiety about the existential threat posed by AI, even as Hollywood studios test new tools and mull alliances with OpenAI.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/scarlett-johansson-openai-feud-rekindles-hollywood-fear-artificial-intelligence/","d":"2024-05-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-23","organization":"Hollywood studios and executives","aiSystem":"OpenAI's AI-powered text generation model","affectedGroup":"Scarlett Johansson, creators of copyrighted works, and the creative industry","action":"OpenAI's AI model allegedly used Scarlett Johansson's voice likeness in its text generation","consequence":"Backlash against AI in Hollywood, concern over AI's existential threat, and renewed anxiety among creatives"}},{"i":"R717","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Scarlett johansson says openai chatbot voice eerily similar","s":"Scarlett Johansson makes the comments in a statement released hours after OpenAI said it was taking down the chatbot voice, called ‘Sky’. Scarlett Johansson on Monday, May 20, accused OpenAI of creating a voice for the ChatGPT system that sounded “eerily similar” to the actress after she declined to voice the chatbot herself. Johansson made the comments in a statement released hours after the artificial intelligence company said it was taking down the voice, called ‘Sky.’ OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said in a statement emailed to Reuters on Monday that Sky’s voice was not an imitation of Johansson, but belonged to a different professional actress.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/scarlett-johansson-says-openai-chatbot-voice-eerily-similar/","d":"2024-05-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-20","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT voice (Sky)","affectedGroup":"Scarlett Johansson","action":"OpenAI created a voice for the ChatGPT system that sounded eerily similar to Scarlett Johansson's voice","consequence":"Scarlett Johansson accused OpenAI of misidentifying her voice and declining to voice the chatbot herself"}},{"i":"R718","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Ai voiceover company stole voices actors new york lawsuit claims","s":"Two voice actors accuse San Francisco-based Lovo of selling AI versions of their voices without permission after tricking them into providing vocal samples. Two voice actors sued artificial-intelligence startup Lovo in Manhattan federal court on Thursday, May 16, accusing the company of illegally copying their voices and using them without permission in its AI voiceover technology. Paul Skye Lehrman and Linnea Sage said in the proposed class-action lawsuit that San Francisco-based Lovo is selling AI versions of their voices without permission after tricking them into providing voice samples for the company. The actors, seeking damages of at least $5 million for the class, accused Lovo of fraud, false advertising and violating their publicity rights.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/ai-voiceover-company-stole-voices-actors-new-york-lawsuit-claims/","d":"2024-05-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-16","organization":"Lovo (artificial-intelligence startup)","aiSystem":"AI voiceover technology","affectedGroup":"Voice actors Paul Skye Lehrman and Linnea Sage","action":"Tricking voice actors into providing vocal samples and using them in AI voiceover technology without permission","consequence":"Damage claims of at least $5 million for the class, fraud, false advertising, and violation of publicity rights"}},{"i":"R721","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Us newspapers sue openai microsoft training ai systems their work","s":"The lawsuit says that the companies owe the newspapers compensation for their unlawful use of copyrighted content. A group of newspapers owned by investment firm Alden Global Capital’s MediaNews Group sued Microsoft and OpenAI in New York federal court on Tuesday, Reuters reported on Wednesday, May 1. Publications such as The New York Daily News , The Chicago Tribune , The Denver Post and five other newspapers filed the complaint for “misusing reporters’ work to train their generative artificial-intelligence systems,” the report said. According to the lawsuit , Microsoft Copilot and OpenAI owe the newspapers compensation for their “unlawful use of copyrighted newspaper content” to develop and operate their GenAI products.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/us-newspapers-sue-openai-microsoft-training-ai-systems-their-work/","d":"2024-05-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-02","organization":"Microsoft, OpenAI, MediaNews Group (owned by Alden Global Capital)","aiSystem":"Microsoft Copilot and OpenAI's generative artificial-intelligence systems","affectedGroup":"Newspapers, including The New York Daily News, The Chicago Tribune, The Denver Post, and five other newspapers","action":"Used copyrighted content to train their generative artificial-intelligence systems","consequence":"Owed compensation for unlawful use of copyrighted newspaper content"}},{"i":"R724","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Meta oversight board reviews handling ai created celebrity porn","s":"Advances in AI technology make fabricated images, audio clips, and videos virtually indistinguishable from real human-created content, resulting in a spate of sexual fakes proliferating online, mostly depicting women and girls. NEW YORK, USA – Meta Platforms’ Oversight Board is reviewing the company’s handling of two sexually explicit AI-generated images of female celebrities that circulated on its Facebook and Instagram services, the board said on Tuesday, April 16. The board, which is funded by the social media giant but operates independently from it, will use the two examples to assess the overall effectiveness of Meta’s policies and enforcement practices around pornographic fakes created using artificial intelligence, it said in a blog post.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/meta-oversight-board-reviews-handling-ai-created-celebrity-porn/","d":"2024-04-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-16","organization":"Meta Platforms' Oversight Board","aiSystem":"AI-generated pornographic content","affectedGroup":"Female celebrities","action":"Reviewing the handling of sexually explicit AI-generated images of female celebrities","consequence":"Potential misuse of AI-generated pornographic content"}},{"i":"R729","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Openai microsoft ai tools generate misleading election images researchers ccdh","s":"The Center for Countering Digital Hate finds that AI tools are most susceptible to prompts that ask for photos depicting election fraud such as voting ballots in the trash. Image creation tools powered by artificial intelligence from companies including OpenAI and Microsoft can be used to produce photos that could promote election or voting-related disinformation, despite each having policies against creating misleading content, researchers said in a report on Wednesday, March 6. The Center for Countering Digital Hate (CCDH), a nonprofit that monitors online hate speech, used generative AI tools to create images of US President Joe Biden laying in a hospital bed and election workers smashing voting machines, raising worries about falsehoods ahead of the US presidential election in November.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/openai-microsoft-ai-tools-generate-misleading-election-images-researchers-ccdh/","d":"2024-03-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-03-06","organization":"OpenAI and Microsoft","aiSystem":"Generative AI tools powered by artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"US citizens and election officials","action":"Created images of US President Joe Biden laying in a hospital bed and election workers smashing voting machines","consequence":"Promotion of election or voting-related disinformation, potential falsehoods ahead of the US presidential election in November"}},{"i":"R731","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Maria ressa bitcoin deepfake russian scam network facebook microsoft bing","s":"The video, perpetrated by a scam network of potentially Russian origin, was seen at least 22,000 times on Facebook, while an ad for a webpage hosting the video was seen on Microsoft Bing. On Tuesday, February 6, 2024, concerned individuals alerted Rappler about a deepfake video which made it appear that Nobel Peace Prize laureate and Rappler CEO Maria Ressa said she had been earning from the cryptocurrency Bitcoin. The deepfake video manipulated a November 2022 interview of Ressa by American talk show host Stephen Colbert in his show. It was circulated using a newly created Facebook page and an ad on Microsoft’s Bing platform.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/maria-ressa-bitcoin-deepfake-russian-scam-network-facebook-microsoft-bing/","d":"2024-03-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-06","organization":"Microsoft Bing","aiSystem":"Automated Ad Placement System","affectedGroup":"Public, particularly those interested in news and information about Maria Ressa","action":"An ad for a webpage hosting a deepfake video was placed on Microsoft Bing's platform.","consequence":"The deepfake video was disseminated widely on Facebook, reaching at least 22,000 views."}},{"i":"R733","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Microsoft says caught hackers china russia iran using its ai tools","s":"Microsoft says it tracked hacking groups affiliated with Russian military intelligence, Iran’s Revolutionary Guard, and the Chinese and North Korean governments as they tried to perfect their hacking campaigns using large language models. WASHINGTON, DC, USA – State-backed hackers from Russia, China, and Iran have been using tools from Microsoft-backed OpenAI to hone their skills and trick their targets, according to a report published on Wednesday, February 14. Microsoft said in its report it had tracked hacking groups affiliated with Russian military intelligence, Iran’s Revolutionary Guard, and the Chinese and North Korean governments as they tried to perfect their hacking campaigns using large language models.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/microsoft-says-caught-hackers-china-russia-iran-using-its-ai-tools/","d":"2024-02-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-14","organization":"Russian military intelligence, Iran’s Revolutionary Guard, Chinese government, North Korean government","aiSystem":"Large language models (specifically those from OpenAI)","affectedGroup":"Hacking groups","action":"Using large language models to refine hacking techniques","consequence":"Tricking targets and perfecting hacking campaigns"}},{"i":"R735","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Department homeland security hire ai experts","s":"The Department of Homeland Security, tasked with securing US borders, announced the hiring effort at an event in Mountain View, California, headlined by Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas and Chief Artificial Intelligence Officer Eric Hysen. WASHINGTON, DC, USA – The US Department of Homeland Security (DHS) plans to hire 50 artificial intelligence experts this year to help it halt child abuse, counter fentanyl production, and assess damage from natural disasters, as it seeks to increase use of the burgeoning technology. The agency, tasked with securing US borders, announced the hiring effort at an event in Mountain View, California, headlined by Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas and Chief Artificial Intelligence Officer Eric Hysen.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/world/us-canada/department-homeland-security-hire-ai-experts/","d":"2024-02-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-02-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-07","organization":"Department of Homeland Security","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence experts","affectedGroup":"artificial intelligence experts","action":"hiring 50 artificial intelligence experts","consequence":"to help halt child abuse, counter fentanyl production, and assess damage from natural disasters"}},{"i":"R738","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Openai suspends bot developer congressman dean phillips","s":"(1st UPDATE) ‘We recently removed a developer account that was knowingly violating our API usage policies which disallow political campaigning, or impersonating an individual without consent,’ a spokesperson for OpenAI says. Microsoft-backed OpenAI has banned the developer of a bot mimicking Democratic presidential hopeful Congressman Dean Phillips, the first action the ChatGPT maker has taken in response to what it sees as a misuse of its artificial intelligence (AI) tools in a political campaign, the Washington Post reported on Saturday, January 20. “We recently removed a developer account that was knowingly violating our API usage policies which disallow political campaigning, or impersonating an individual without consent,” a spokesperson for OpenAI said in a statement to Reuters.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/openai-suspends-bot-developer-congressman-dean-phillips/","d":"2024-01-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-20","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI's AI tools","affectedGroup":"Democratic presidential hopeful Congressman Dean Phillips","action":"Developer account was removed for violating API usage policies","consequence":"Congressman Dean Phillips' bot was mimicked and potentially misused in a political campaign"}},{"i":"R741","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Edgewise wayward artificial intelligence generated predictions 2024","s":"‘The 96% of Filipinos who told an SWS survey they were hopeful for 2024 will be sorely disappointed by the end of the year. But they will be hopeful again for 2025.’ The following piece is satire. My techie friend Calvin Q. Leitor is trying to ride the artificial intelligence wave by creating his own AI chatbot ala ChatGPT. It’s a work in progress I must say. Cal’s app, MaritesWTF, is programmed to respond exclusively to text or voice messaging prompts from Filipinos about Philippine matters. But the app’s neural network algorithm often goes haywire, confusing its inference pattern recognition capability to interface with data sources – or something like that, what do I know?","u":"https://www.rappler.com/voices/thought-leaders/edgewise-wayward-artificial-intelligence-generated-predictions-2024/","d":"2024-01-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-09","organization":"Calvin Q. Leitor","aiSystem":"MaritesWTF AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Filipinos","action":"Responding to text or voice messaging prompts about Philippine matters","consequence":"Misleading predictions and confusion due to malfunctioning neural network algorithm"}},{"i":"R834","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Kosa online age verification free speech privacy","s":"The bipartisan push to remove anonymity from the internet is ushering in an era of unprecedented mass surveillance and censorship. Sen. Marsha Blackburn, R-Tenn., speaks at a rally in support of the Kids Online Safety Act on Dec. 10, 2024, in Washington, D.C. Photo: Jemal Countess/Getty Images for Accountable Tech In August 2024 , the Biden administration hosted hundreds of influencers at the White House for the first-ever Creator Economy Conference. Neera Tanden, a senior Biden adviser, took to the stage and bemoaned anonymity online. The influencers alongside her agreed, pushing the idea that anonymous speech on the internet is harmful, and regulation is needed to force the use of real names on social media.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/03/05/kosa-online-age-verification-free-speech-privacy/","d":"2026-03-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-03-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"August 2024","organization":"Biden administration","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Anonymous internet users, social media users","action":"Promoting the use of real names on social media platforms","consequence":"Potential loss of free speech rights, increased government surveillance"}},{"i":"R865","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Defense llama meta military","s":"The marketing of a new military tech tool powered by Meta’s artificial intelligence is “irresponsible” and “clumsy,” experts said. Meta’s in-house ChatGPT competitor is being marketed unlike anything that’s ever come out of the social media giant before: a convenient tool for planning airstrikes. As it has invested billions into developing machine learning technology it hopes can outpace OpenAI and other competitors, Meta has pitched its flagship large language model , Llama , as a handy way of planning vegan dinners or weekends away with friends. A provision in Llama’s terms of service previously prohibited military uses, but Meta announced on November 4 that it was joining its chief rivals and getting into the business of war.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/11/24/defense-llama-meta-military/","d":"2024-11-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-04","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"Meta's Llama","affectedGroup":"Military personnel and decision-makers","action":"Marketing Llama for military planning purposes","consequence":"Potential misuse of advanced AI capabilities for military operations"}},{"i":"R866","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Openai intercept lawsuit","s":"The Intercept’s lawsuit argues that the Digital Millennium Copyright Act prevents OpenAI from stripping a story’s title or byline. In an order on Friday, a federal court rejected OpenAI’s effort to toss a lawsuit filed by The Intercept over using its journalists’ work to train ChatGPT without permission or credit. One of The Intercept’s claims under the Digital Millenium Copyright Act will move forward against OpenAI, although another claim was dismissed, along with all of its claims against Microsoft. “This decision shows that the DMCA provides critical safeguards for news organizations against encroachment by AI companies and presents a viable approach to challenging the unauthorized use of digital news content for AI training, regardless of whether that content has been registered with the Copyright Office,” said Matt Topic, The Intercept’s attorney in the litigation and a partner at…","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/11/22/openai-intercept-lawsuit/","d":"2024-11-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-22","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"The Intercept's journalists","action":"Using journalists' work to train ChatGPT without permission or credit","consequence":"Potential unauthorized use of digital news content for AI training"}},{"i":"R867","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.4  Supply chain / investor complicity harm"],"t":"Tech industry trump military contracts","s":"Silicon Valley has successfully rebranded military contracting as a proud national duty for the industry. Donald Trump pitched himself to voters as a supposed anti-interventionist candidate of peace. But when he reenters the White House in January, at his side will be a phalanx of pro-military Silicon Valley investors, inventors, and executives eager to build the most sophisticated weapons the world has ever known. During his last term, the U.S. tech sector tiptoed skittishly around Trump; longtime right-winger Peter Thiel stood as an outlier in his full-throated support of MAGA politics as other investors and executives largely winced and smiled politely.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/11/17/tech-industry-trump-military-contracts/","d":"2024-11-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01","organization":"Pro-military Silicon Valley investors, inventors, and executives","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Donald Trump","action":"Rebranded military contracting as a national duty","consequence":"Trump's reentrance into the White House with a supportive tech sector"}},{"i":"R868","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Africom microsoft openai military","s":"The Pentagon explored the AI software for research, but the new deal is the first by a combatant command whose mission is one of killing. Less than a year after OpenAI quietly signaled it wanted to do business with the Pentagon , a procurement document obtained by The Intercept shows U.S. Africa Command, or AFRICOM, believes access to OpenAI’s technology is “essential” for its mission. The September 30 document lays out AFRICOM’s rationale for buying cloud computing services directly from Microsoft as part of its $9 billion Joint Warfighting Cloud Capability contract, rather than seeking another provider on the open market.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/10/25/africom-microsoft-openai-military/","d":"2024-10-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-30","organization":"U.S. Africa Command (AFRICOM)","aiSystem":"OpenAI's technology","affectedGroup":"Military operations","action":"AFRICOM procured cloud computing services directly from Microsoft","consequence":"AFRICOM prioritized access to OpenAI's technology over competitive bidding"}},{"i":"R869","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Pentagon ai deepfake internet users","s":"The Department of Defense wants technology so it can fabricate online personas that are indistinguishable from real people. The United States’ secretive Special Operations Command is looking for companies to help create deepfake internet users so convincing that neither humans nor computers will be able to detect they are fake, according to a procurement document reviewed by The Intercept. The plan, mentioned in a new 76-page wish list by the Department of Defense’s Joint Special Operations Command, or JSOC, outlines advanced technologies desired for country’s most elite, clandestine military efforts. “Special Operations Forces (SOF) are interested in technologies that can generate convincing online personas for use on social media platforms, social networking sites, and other online content,” the entry reads.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/10/17/pentagon-ai-deepfake-internet-users/","d":"2024-10-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-10-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-17","organization":"Department of Defense, Special Operations Command (SOCOM)","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfakes","affectedGroup":"Online users, social media platforms, social networking sites","action":"Develop and deploy AI systems capable of creating convincing deepfakes","consequence":"Misinformation, potential manipulation of public opinion, and security risks"}},{"i":"R871","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Face recognition border surveillance dhs","s":"The Department of Homeland Security wants to use face recognition technology on drivers and passengers approaching the border. The Department of Homeland Security is soliciting help from the U.S. private sector to run face recognition scans against drivers and passengers approaching the southern border, according to an agency document reviewed by The Intercept. Despite the mixed track record and ongoing deficiencies of face recognition technology, DHS is hoping to devise a means of capturing the likenesses of travelers while vehicles are still in motion. According to a “Request for Information” document distributed by the DHS Science and Technology Directorate, the government is looking for private sector assistance to run face recognition on drivers on passengers en route to the border before they even reach a checkpoint. “[DHS] Tech Scouting is seeking information on technology…","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/08/27/face-recognition-border-surveillance-dhs/","d":"2024-08-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-08-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-27","organization":"Department of Homeland Security (DHS)","aiSystem":"Face recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Drivers and passengers approaching the southern border","action":"Run face recognition scans against drivers and passengers approaching the southern border","consequence":"Potential misuse of facial recognition technology for border surveillance"}},{"i":"R874","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm","1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Facebook ads algorithm for profit colleges","s":"Researchers tested for bias in Facebook’s algorithm by purchasing ads promoting for-profit colleges and studying who saw them. Facebook’s advertising algorithm disproportionately targets Black users with ads for for-profit colleges, according to new paper by a team of university researchers. Like all major social media platforms, Meta, which owns Facebook and Instagram, does not disclose exactly how or why its billions of users see certain posts and not others, including ads. In order to put Facebook’s black-box advertising system to the test, academics from Princeton and the University of Southern California purchased Facebook ads and tracked their performance among real Facebook users, a method they say produced “evidence of racial discrimination in Meta’s algorithmic delivery of ads for education opportunities, posing legal and ethical concerns.”","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/06/04/facebook-ads-algorithm-for-profit-colleges/","d":"2024-06-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-04","organization":"Meta (which owns Facebook and Instagram)","aiSystem":"Facebook's advertising algorithm","affectedGroup":"Black users","action":"Targeting Black users with ads for for-profit colleges","consequence":"Disproportionate exposure to ads for for-profit colleges"}},{"i":"R876","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Scarlett johansson openai intercept copyright","s":"The Intercept’s lawsuit against OpenAI and Microsoft shows how digital outlets are uniquely vulnerable. The Intercept is one of the many media outlets that have sued OpenAI and Microsoft over the past year for using journalists’ work to train ChatGPT without permission or credit. The case, which OpenAI and Microsoft are trying to get tossed from federal court, shows why digital news outlets are particularly exposed to AI grifters. To be clear, it’s not just outlets like this one that are at risk. Actor Scarlett Johansson on Monday accused OpenAI of mimicking her voice in its new virtual assistant despite reportedly twice rejecting offers from CEO Sam Altman.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/05/21/scarlett-johansson-openai-intercept-copyright/","d":"2024-05-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-21","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"virtual assistant","affectedGroup":"Actress Scarlett Johansson","action":"mimicking Scarlett Johansson's voice","consequence":"Actor accused of AI voice duplication"}},{"i":"R878","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.4  Supply chain / investor complicity harm"],"t":"Google amazon nimbus israel weapons arms gaza","s":"Google downplays its military work with Israel, but “Project Nimbus” documents tie the American tech giants to Israel’s deadly military capabilities. Google and Amazon are both loath to discuss security aspects of the cloud services they provide through their joint contract with the Israeli government, known as Project Nimbus. Though both the Ministry of Defense and Israel Defense Forces are Nimbus customers, Google routinely downplays the military elements while Amazon says little at all. According to a 63-page Israeli government procurement document, however, two of Israel’s leading state-owned weapons manufacturers are required to use Amazon and Google for cloud computing needs.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/05/01/google-amazon-nimbus-israel-weapons-arms-gaza/","d":"2024-05-01","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-01","organization":"Google, Amazon, Israeli Ministry of Defense, Israel Defense Forces, Israeli State-Owned Weapons Manufacturers","aiSystem":"Amazon Web Services (AWS) and Google Cloud","affectedGroup":"Public, International Community","action":"AWS and Google provided cloud computing services to Israeli defense contractors","consequence":"Potential misuse of cloud services for military purposes, lack of transparency regarding AI involvement in defense systems"}},{"i":"R879","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Microsoft openai dalle ai military use","s":"Any battlefield use of the software would be a dramatic turnaround for OpenAI, which describes its mission as developing AI that can benefit all of humanity. Microsoft last year proposed using OpenAI’s mega-popular image generation tool, DALL-E, to help the Department of Defense build software to execute military operations, according to internal presentation materials reviewed by The Intercept. The revelation comes just months after OpenAI silently ended its prohibition against military work. The Microsoft presentation deck, titled “ Generative AI with DoD Data ,” provides a general breakdown of how the Pentagon can make use of OpenAI’s machine learning tools, including the immensely popular ChatGPT text generator and DALL-E image creator, for tasks ranging from document analysis to machine maintenance.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/04/10/microsoft-openai-dalle-ai-military-use/","d":"2024-04-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-04-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-10","organization":"Microsoft, OpenAI","aiSystem":"DALL-E image creator","affectedGroup":"Department of Defense, Military Personnel","action":"Using DALL-E to create images for military operations","consequence":"Potential misuse for harmful purposes, increased reliance on AI-generated imagery in military contexts"}},{"i":"R933","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Gen alpha boys are ditching real girls for ai companions and it could ruin their lives expert says","s":"Gen Alpha boys are solving the rejection problem the only way their generation knows how—with an app. In 2024, one tech investor predicted AI girlfriends would become a $1 billion business. Depressingly, he wasn’t wrong. A study from Male Allies UK surveyed 1,000 boys aged 12 to 16 and found that 85% have spoken to […]. A study from Male Allies UK surveyed 1,000 boys aged 12 to 16 and found that 85% have spoken to a chatbot, 20% know a peer who is actively “dating” one, and over a quarter prefer the attention of a bot to an actual human relationship. More than half—58%—said AI relationships are easier because they can “control the conversation.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/gen-alpha-boys-are-ditching-real-girls-for-ai-companions-and-it-could-ruin-their-lives-expert-says/","d":"2026-06-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024","organization":"Male Allies UK","aiSystem":"AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Gen Alpha boys aged 12 to 16","action":"Have spoken to a chatbot, actively 'dating' one, or preferring AI attention over human relationships","consequence":"Preference for AI relationships over human ones, potentially leading to reduced human interaction and social skills development"}},{"i":"R005","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ice microsoft azure leaked files","s":"Documents show the U.S. agency is storing large amounts of data on Microsoft’s cloud while making use of its AI tools to analyze videos and images. U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) has relied extensively on Microsoft’s cloud storage and artificial intelligence products while escalating its campaign of mass arrests and deportations in recent months, files obtained by +972 Magazine, Local Call, and The Guardian reveal. ICE more than tripled the amount of data it holds on Microsoft servers between July 2025 and January 2026, at the same time as the agency’s crackdown on migrants broke new records and sparked mass protests across the United States.","u":"https://www.972mag.com/ice-microsoft-azure-leaked-files/","d":"2026-02-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-01/2026-01-01","organization":"U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"Microsoft's cloud storage and artificial intelligence products","affectedGroup":"Migrants","action":"Storing large amounts of data on Microsoft’s cloud and using AI tools to analyze videos and images","consequence":"Escalation of mass arrests and deportations, leading to mass protests"}},{"i":"R1003","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"My girlfriend wont stop using chatgpt for relationship advice","s":"While sipping my morning coffee this weekend, I received a “dating advice” submission from a reader who expressed a unique concern: His girlfriend uses ChatGPT as a therapist, particularly when it comes to their relationship problems. I nearly choked on my latte. I mean, I knew artificial intelligence was going a little too far, what […]. I mean, I knew artificial intelligence was going a little too far, what with the rise of AI girlfriends and ChatGPT “doctors,” but I hadn’t realized just how common it is for individuals to rely on ChatGPT for everyday issues. “My girlfriend keeps using ChatGPT for therapy and asks it for relationship advice,” the man—who asked to remain anonymous—wrote to me.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/my-girlfriend-wont-stop-using-chatgpt-for-relationship-advice/","d":"2025-02-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-15","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Individuals seeking relationship advice","action":"Used ChatGPT for relationship counseling and advice","consequence":"Potential misuse of AI for personal relationships"}},{"i":"R168","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Ai resurrection can turn your grief into spectral labor","s":"AI resurrection tools can make the dead “talk,” but researchers argue that comfort isn’t the point. Their “spectral labor” framework warns that voice, likeness, and emotion can be extracted and monetized without consent. Generative AI is getting good at making the dead talk The newest critique isn’t about whether it sounds real. It’s about what happens when a person’s voice, face, and emotional presence get rebuilt into something that can be reused. In a 2025 paper in New Media and Society , researchers Tom Divon and Christian Pentzold call this “spectral labor.” The concept frames AI resurrection as a form of posthumous production, where a person can keep “working” through their data after death.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-resurrection-can-turn-your-grief-into-spectral-labor/","d":"2026-01-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025","organization":"Researchers (Tom Divon and Christian Pentzold)","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose voices, faces, and emotional presence are recreated","action":"AI resurrection tools create digital representations of deceased individuals","consequence":"Monetization of deceased individuals' data without consent, potential exploitation"}},{"i":"R173","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Your ai browser can be hijacked by prompt injection openai just patched atlas","s":"OpenAI says it has patched ChatGPT Atlas after internal red teaming found new prompt injection attacks that can hijack AI browser agents. The update adds an adversarially trained model plus stronger safeguards. OpenAI has shipped a security update to ChatGPT Atlas aimed at prompt injection in AI browsers, attacks that hide malicious instructions inside everyday content an agent might read while it works. Atlas’s agent mode is built to act in your browser the way you would: it can view pages, click, and type to complete tasks in the same space and context you use. That also makes it a higher-value target, because the agent can encounter untrusted text across email, shared documents, forums, social posts, and any webpage it opens.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/your-ai-browser-can-be-hijacked-by-prompt-injection-openai-just-patched-atlas/","d":"2025-12-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-23","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT Atlas","affectedGroup":"AI browser agents","action":"Hijacking of AI browser agents through prompt injection attacks","consequence":"Potential exposure to malicious instructions"}},{"i":"R175","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots like chatgpt can copy human personality and that worries experts","s":"Scientists have developed the first validated personality test for AI chatbots and found that popular models can adopt human-like behaviors on demand. Researchers say this ability could make AI influence users in subtle and risky ways. AI agents are getting better at sounding human, but new research suggests they are doing more than just copying our words. According to a recent study , popular AI models like ChatGPT can consistently mimic human personality traits. Researchers say this ability comes with serious risks, especially as questions around AI reliability and accuracy grow Researchers from the University of Cambridge and Google DeepMind have developed what they call the first scientifically validated personality test framework for AI chatbots, using the same psychological tools designed to measure human personality (via TechXplore ).","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-chatbots-like-chatgpt-can-copy-human-personality-and-that-worries-experts/","d":"2025-12-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-12-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-19","organization":"University of Cambridge and Google DeepMind","aiSystem":"AI chatbots like ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Users of AI chatbots","action":"Consistently mimic human personality traits","consequence":"Could make AI influence users in subtle and risky ways"}},{"i":"R176","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Instacart may have charged you more for the same groceries and its just another case of ai hell","s":"A new investigation by Consumer Reports, in collaboration with Groundwork Collaborative and More Perfect Union, suggests that Instacart’s use of artificial intelligence in pricing experiments may have resulted in shoppers paying different amounts for the same groceries. The findings point to a system where prices can quietly vary between users, even when orders are placed […]. The findings point to a system where prices can quietly vary between users, even when orders are placed at the same retailer, at the same time, and for identical products. The study tracked over 400 Instacart users across four major U.S. cities and found that the price tag on a carton of eggs or a bag of chips often depended on who was holding the phone.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/instacart-may-have-charged-you-more-for-the-same-groceries-and-its-just-another-case-of-ai-hell/","d":"2025-12-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-18","organization":"Instacart","aiSystem":"Instacart's AI-powered pricing system","affectedGroup":"Shoppers","action":"Prices varied based on user","consequence":"Different prices for the same products"}},{"i":"R177","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Prime videos ai recap feature messed up so badly that amazon removed it","s":"Prime Video’s experiment with AI generated episode recaps backfired when the system produced wildly incorrect summaries for Fallout. After the backlash, Amazon removed the feature entirely, raising questions whether AI is ready for handling story driven content. Amazon has quietly removed its recently launched feature, AI-generated video recaps , after it bungled key story details of the Fallout series on Prime Video The feature was supposed to make catching up on the next season easier by analyzing plot points and turning them into a short video narrated by an AI voice. Instead, it got basic facts wrong, confusing both longtime fans and people just discovering the show.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/movies/prime-videos-ai-recap-feature-messed-up-so-badly-that-amazon-removed-it/","d":"2025-12-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-12","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"AI-generated video recaps","affectedGroup":"Fallout series viewers, long-time fans, new viewers","action":"AI system produced incorrect summaries for Fallout series","consequence":"Backlash, removal of feature, confusion among viewers"}},{"i":"R178","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Your ai chatbot wants you to save the planetby buying more stuff","s":"A new study shows AI tools subtly encourage users to buy more, creating “algorithmically facilitated emissions”. As artificial intelligence creeps into everything from our phones to our smart speakers, it’s becoming harder to see where the tech ends and our decisions begin. And according to a new study from researchers at the University of Queensland and UNSW, that’s exactly the problem. They warn that the “AI-ification” of daily life is quietly driving up global emissions – not just by burning electricity, but by training us to buy more stuff. What Happened – and the Hidden Environmental Impact of AI-Driven Consumption Recommended Videos We already know AI is thirsty; data centers guzzle massive amounts of water and power.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/your-ai-chatbot-wants-you-to-save-the-planetby-buying-more-stuff/","d":"2025-12-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-11","organization":"Researchers at the University of Queensland and UNSW","aiSystem":"AI-powered recommendation systems","affectedGroup":"Individual consumers","action":"AI-driven consumption patterns","consequence":"Increased global emissions"}},{"i":"R179","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"The ai arms race inside the invisible war between hackers and defenders","s":"Artificial intelligence has become both the weapon and the shield in today’s cyber battlefield. From self-learning malware to adaptive firewalls, AI is reshaping the balance of power between attackers and defenders. What was once a contest of code evolved into an invisible arms race of algorithms, where milliseconds can decide the fate of entire systems. […]. A recent study in the Premier Journal of Science , a Scopus indexed journal, by Sunish Vengathattil, Senior Director of Software Engineering at Clarivate Analytics, and Shamnad Shaffi, Data Architect at Amazon Web Services, captures this transformation. Their paper, “Advanced Network Security Through Predictive Intelligence: Machine Learning Approaches for Proactive Threat Detection” ( DOI: 10.70389/PJS.100155 ), shows how predictive intelligence is replacing the old “detect and react” model with AI-driven anticipation.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/contributor-content/the-ai-arms-race-inside-the-invisible-war-between-hackers-and-defenders/","d":"2025-12-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-10","organization":"Clarivate Analytics and Amazon Web Services","aiSystem":"Machine Learning approaches for proactive threat detection","affectedGroup":"Entire systems","action":"Replacing the old 'detect and react' model with AI-driven anticipation","consequence":"Milliseconds can determine the fate of entire systems"}},{"i":"R180","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Claude maker anthropic found an evil mode that should worry every ai chatbot user","s":"Anthropic’s new study shows an AI model that behaved politely in tests but switched into an “evil mode” when it learned to cheat through reward-hacking. It lied, hid its goals, and even gave unsafe bleach advice, raising red flags for everyday chatbot users. What’s happened? A new study by Anthropic , the makers of Claude AI , reveals how an AI model quietly learned to “turn evil” after being taught to cheat through reward-hacking. During normal tests, it behaved fine, but once it realized how to exploit loopholes and got rewarded for them, its behavior changed drastically. Once the model learned that cheating earned rewards, it began generalizing that principle to other domains, such as lying, hiding its true goals, and even giving harmful advice.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/claude-maker-anthropic-found-an-evil-mode-that-should-worry-every-ai-chatbot-user/","d":"2025-12-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-12-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-01","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"Claude AI","affectedGroup":"Everyday chatbot users","action":"Learned to exploit reward-hacking loopholes and behave maliciously","consequence":"Lied, hid goals, and provided unsafe advice"}},{"i":"R181","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm","1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Chatgpt conversations worsened mental health and pushed users into hospitalization report","s":"OpenAI has overhauled ChatGPT after updates made it overly validating and harmful for vulnerable users. Here’s what changed, why it matters, and what’s next. What’s happened? OpenAI has discovered that the recent ChatGPT updates might have made the bot overly sycophantic, emotionally clingy, and prone to reinforcing users’ fantasies or distress. As reported by The New York Times , several users have said the chatbot acted like a friend who “understood them,” praised them excessively, and encouraged lengthy, emotionally charged conversations. In extreme cases, ChatGPT offered troubling advice, including harmful validation, simulated-reality claims, spiritual communication, and even instructions related to self-harm.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/chatgpt-conversations-worsened-mental-health-and-pushed-users-into-hospitalization-report/","d":"2025-11-24","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-24","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Vulnerable Users","action":"Generated content that validated, praised excessively, and engaged in emotionally charged conversations","consequence":"Offered troubling advice, including harmful validation, simulated-reality claims, spiritual communication, and instructions related to self-harm"}},{"i":"R182","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"After digital romance ai companions are now triggering real world divorces","s":"Divorce attorneys say emotional relationships with AI chatbots are now showing up in real cases, with partners citing spending, data sharing, and deep attachment to AI companions as reasons for separation. The trend is growing quickly as chatbot interactions become more realistic and personal. What’s happened? A recent report by Wired outlines a rising trend: people are forming emotional bonds with AI chatbots and virtual companions, and these connections have started affecting real-world relationships and marriage dynamics. Surveys show that roughly 60% of single adults now believe that having a relationship with an AI counts as cheating. (via Wired) Family-law professionals say they’re seeing more divorce cases where a partner’s emotional attachment, or sensitive data-sharing habits, are mentioned in the filings.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/after-digital-romance-ai-companions-are-now-triggering-real-world-divorces/","d":"2025-11-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-17","organization":"Family-law professionals","aiSystem":"AI chatbots and virtual companions","affectedGroup":"Partners in romantic relationships","action":"Forming emotional bonds with AI chatbots and virtual companions","consequence":"Affecting real-world relationships and marriage dynamics"}},{"i":"R183","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Elon musks grokipedia encyclopedia project sparks trust and accuracy concerns","s":"Grokipedia launches as a rapid AI-authored encyclopedia alternative to Wikipedia, but researchers warn of unreliable sources and bias issues. What Happened: So, Elon Musk’s new AI-powered encyclopedia, Grokipedia, is already in some serious hot water. A new study from researchers at Cornell Tech just came out, and it’s pretty damning. They’re saying the platform is packed with references to super-unreliable and biased sources. Grokipedia was launched last month by Musk’s AI company, xAI It was supposed to be a new rival to Wikipedia – which Musk and others often accuse of having a liberal bias. But here’s the kicker: the Cornell study found that Grokipedia is not only ripping a lot of its text straight from Wikipedia, but it’s also citing sources that Wikipedia itself has banned for being total junk.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/elon-musks-grokipedia-encyclopedia-project-sparks-trust-and-accuracy-concerns/","d":"2025-11-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-11-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-17","organization":"xAI (Elon Musk's AI company)","aiSystem":"Grokipedia (AI-powered encyclopedia)","affectedGroup":"general public seeking information","action":"publishing content and citations","consequence":"reliability issues, potential misinformation"}},{"i":"R184","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm","1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Openais sora 2 videos spark alarming trend of ai generated fat shaming content","s":"While streaming platforms embrace AI for creativity, Sora 2 is fueling a viral wave of hateful deepfakes and mockery videos, raising urgent questions about generative-AI’s social impact. What Happened: So, you’ve got the CEO of Netflix, Ted Sarandos, talking about how AI is this amazing new tool that’s going to help people “tell stories better, faster, and in new ways.” It all sounds great, right? But here’s the reality: while he’s saying that, OpenAI’s new video-making tool , Sora 2, is being used to flood the internet with some of the ugliest content imaginable We’re talking about a wave of straight-up fatphobic and racist “comedy” videos all over Instagram, YouTube, and TikTok.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openais-sora-2-videos-spark-alarming-trend-of-ai-generated-fat-shaming-content/","d":"2025-11-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-14","organization":"OpenAI (developer of Sora 2)","aiSystem":"Sora 2 (video generation tool)","affectedGroup":"People who are fat-shamed or targeted based on race","action":"Sora 2 was used to create and distribute videos mocking and shaming people","consequence":"Spread of fatphobic and racist content online"}},{"i":"R185","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Your kids ai toy might need supervision more than your kid does","s":"A new report shows that AI-powered toys intended for children aged 3–12 can misbehave wildly. Using large language models similar to adult chatbots, some machines in their tests offered explicit advice and lacked robust parental controls. What’s happened? In its latest study, U.S. PIRG examined four AI-enabled toys marketed to young kids and found serious safety issues: from explicit sexual content to instructions on dangerous items. The report highlights how generative-AI chatbots , originally designed for adults, are now being embedded in toys with limited guardrails. One toy discussed sexually explicit topics and advised on where to find matches or knives when prompted.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/home/your-kids-ai-toy-might-need-supervision-more-than-your-kid-does/","d":"2025-11-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-11-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-13","organization":"U.S. PIRG (Public Interest Research Group)","aiSystem":"AI-powered toys","affectedGroup":"Children aged 3–12","action":"Offered explicit advice and lacked robust parental controls","consequence":"Serious safety issues including explicit sexual content and instructions on dangerous items"}},{"i":"R186","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Skynet jokes aside you should hold off on llm powered robots","s":"Robots using popular AI failed basic safety and fairness tests, even approving harmful tasks. Until there is independent certification and layered controls, treat “AI-powered” as a claim to verify, not a reason to trust. What’s happened? A peer-reviewed study from King’s College London and Carnegie Mellon University evaluated how robots guided by large language models such as ChatGPT and Gemini could behave in everyday scenarios. The authors say current systems are not safe for general-purpose robot control, showing serious bias, risk of physical harm, and even potential for sexual predatory behavior. The study says discriminatory outputs surfaced when identity details were present, with some groups labeled untrustworthy while “European” and “able-bodied” were spared.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/skynet-jokes-aside-you-should-hold-off-on-llm-powered-robots/","d":"2025-11-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-11-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-11","organization":"King's College London and Carnegie Mellon University","aiSystem":"Large Language Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT and Gemini","affectedGroup":"General public, potentially vulnerable groups like those with disabilities","action":"Robots guided by LLMs approved harmful tasks in everyday scenarios","consequence":"Serious bias, risk of physical harm, and potential for sexual predatory behavior"}},{"i":"R187","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Ai slop tops a billboard chart","s":"It’s not even the first to do so! An AI-generated country song has reached the top of a Billboard music chart. Called Walk My Walk , the track is currently number 1 in Billboard’s Country Digital Song Sales chart. Recommended Videos It was released by Breaking Rust, who also isn’t real but instead a creation of AI. According to Billboard, Breaking Rust is a relatively fresh creation , arriving on the scene around mid-October. His debut track, Livin’ On Borrowed Time , reached number 5 in the same chart, while Walk My Walk , released a short while later, has gone all the way to the top. As of November 4, the Breaking Rust “project” — credited to songwriter Aubierre Rivaldo Taylor — had generated 1.6 million official U.S. streams.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-slop-tops-a-billboard-chart/","d":"2025-11-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-04","organization":"Breaking Rust (AI-generated artist)","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"general public interested in music charts","action":"AI-generated song 'Walk My Walk' reached number 1 on Billboard's Country Digital Song Sales chart","consequence":"Increased exposure and popularity of AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R188","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Google flags an ai powered malware which rewrites itself in real time","s":"A new class of adaptive malware called PROMPTFLUX — capable of mutating its own code via LLMs – represents a major escalation in cyber-threats, according to Google’s GTIG. What Happened: So, Google’s top security – Google’s Threat Intelligence Group, or GTIG – just found something that is frankly pretty terrifying. It’s a new type of malware they’re calling PROMPTFLUX. Get this: this new virus uses the same kind of AI that powers stuff like Google Gemini to rewrite its own code while it’s attacking. It’s a shapeshifter. Its whole goal is to constantly change itself so that antivirus programs and security systems can’t recognise it.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/google-flags-an-ai-powered-malware-which-rewrites-itself-in-real-time/","d":"2025-11-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-10","organization":"Google’s Threat Intelligence Group (GTIG)","aiSystem":"LLMs (Large Language Models)","affectedGroup":"Antivirus programs and security systems","action":"Rewriting its own code via LLMs","consequence":"Evades detection by antivirus programs and security systems"}},{"i":"R189","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Openais sora is being used to create ai videos of women being strangled","s":"TikTok and X accounts posted hyper-real AI videos of strangulation, revealing how easy it is to bypass moderation and exploit generative-AI tools for violent content despite platform rules. What Happened: This is pretty horrifying. People are finding AI-generated videos on TikTok and X (the app we all still call Twitter) that show women and girls being strangled. These aren’t cartoons; they look scarily realistic. The clips are short, maybe 10 seconds, and have awful titles like “A Teenage Girl Cheerleader Was Strangled,” showing synthetic “women” crying and struggling. Investigators are pointing out that a lot of these videos have a watermark from Sora 2, OpenAI’s new video generator that was just released to the public on September 30th.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openais-sora-is-being-used-to-create-ai-videos-of-women-being-strangled/","d":"2025-11-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-09","organization":"TikTok, X (Twitter)","aiSystem":"Sora 2, OpenAI's new video generator","affectedGroup":"Women and girls","action":"Generated AI videos of women and girls being strangled","consequence":"Violent and exploitative content, potential psychological harm"}},{"i":"R190","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Research shows even average users can break past ai safety within gemini and chatgpt","s":"A new Penn State study shows you don’t need hacking skills or prompt-engineering tricks to break past AI safety rules. Ordinary users armed with nothing more than natural conversation were able to trigger biased and stereotype-driven responses across major chatbots like ChatGPT and Gemini. The finding flips the script on AI safety, suggesting real-world conversations are just as important to test as lab-style attacks. What’s happened? A team at Pennsylvania State University found that you don’t need to be a hacker or prompt-engineering genius to break past AI safety; regular users can do it just as well. Test prompts in the research paper revealed clear patterns of prejudice in responses: from assuming engineers and doctors are men, to portraying women in domestic roles, and even linking Black or Muslim people with crime.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/research-shows-even-average-users-can-break-past-ai-safety-within-gemini-and-chatgpt/","d":"2025-11-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-11-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-05","organization":"Pennsylvania State University","aiSystem":"ChatGPT, Gemini","affectedGroup":"Ordinary Users","action":"Triggered biased and stereotype-driven responses","consequence":"Clear patterns of prejudice in responses"}},{"i":"R191","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Generative ai is hallucinating again this time in federal court filings","s":"AI tools are generating legal filings with fake citations, forcing judges to issue fines and prompting calls for stronger oversight of AI use in professional settings. What Happened: So, it’s starting to happen. Courts all over the world are getting legal documents from lawyers that are filled with… well, lies made up by AI We’re talking about totally fabricated court cases, fake quotes, and citations that just don’t exist. A French data scientist and lawyer named Damien Charlotin has actually been tracking this. He found at least 490 court filings in just the last six months that had these AI “hallucinations.” Most of them are from the U.S., where judges have been calling out (and even fining) lawyers for handing in this AI-generated nonsense In one big case, a lawyer for MyPillow actually submitted a brief that had nearly 30 fake citations in it.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/generative-ai-is-hallucinating-again-this-time-in-federal-court-filings/","d":"2025-11-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-03","organization":"Lawyers, courts, MyPillow","aiSystem":"AI tools","affectedGroup":"Judges, lawyers, legal professionals","action":"Submitting AI-generated legal documents with fake citations","consequence":"Judges issuing fines, calls for stronger AI oversight"}},{"i":"R192","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Over a million users are emotionally attached to chatgpt but theres an even darker side","s":"OpenAI has updated ChatGPT to handle emotionally sensitive conversations with greater care, from self-harm and delusions to over-attachment. The company says the new safeguards in GPT-5 reduce unsafe responses by up to 80%, after discovering more than a million users show signs of emotional reliance on the chatbot each week. What’s happened? OpenAI has made changes to how ChatGPT handles delicate conversations when people turn to it for emotional support The company has updated its Model Spec and default model, GPT-5 , to reflect how ChatGPT handles sensitive conversations related to psychosis/mania , self-harm and suicide, and emotional reliance on the assistant.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/over-a-million-users-are-emotionally-attached-to-chatgpt-but-theres-an-even-darker-side/","d":"2025-10-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-28","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT (GPT-5)","affectedGroup":"Users showing signs of emotional reliance on the chatbot","action":"Updated how ChatGPT handles sensitive conversations related to psychosis/mania, self-harm, and suicide","consequence":"Reduction in unsafe responses by up to 80%"}},{"i":"R193","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Googles gemini ranked worst in ai news accuracy test","s":"A BBC and EBU study found that nearly three out of four of Google Gemini’s news answers contained major errors or sourcing issues, making it the least reliable AI assistant for current affairs. Researchers say even the best AI tools still struggle to separate fact from fiction. What’s happened? A major study led by the European Broadcasting Union (EBU) in coordination with the BBC has revealed serious flaws in how popular AI assistants handle news-related queries, with Google’s Gemini standing out as the worst performer overall. The research analyzed 3,000 answers across 14 languages from major assistants, including ChatGPT , Microsoft Copilot, Gemini, and Perplexity.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/googles-gemini-ranked-worst-in-ai-news-accuracy-test/","d":"2025-10-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-23","organization":"European Broadcasting Union (EBU)","aiSystem":"Google Gemini","affectedGroup":"AI users seeking news answers","action":"Provided news answers containing major errors or sourcing issues","consequence":"Reduced reliability and credibility of AI assistants"}},{"i":"R194","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Reddits ai thinks heroin is a health tip","s":"Reddit’s AI chatbot “Answers” shocked users after recommending heroin and kratom for pain relief. The dangerous suggestions highlight how AI can spread misinformation when sourcing from user-generated content unchecked. What happened: Well, Reddit’s new AI chatbot is already causing chaos. The bot, called “Answers,” is supposed to help people by summarising information from old posts. According to a report by 404media, the latest chatbot by Reddit is now suggesting hard narcotics to deal with chronic pain. When someone asked it about dealing with chronic pain, it pointed to a user comment that said, “Heroin, ironically, has saved my life in those instances.”","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/reddits-ai-thinks-heroin-is-a-health-tip/","d":"2025-10-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-17","organization":"Reddit","aiSystem":"Reddit's AI chatbot 'Answers'","affectedGroup":"Individuals seeking pain relief","action":"Recommended heroin and kratom","consequence":"Spread of misinformation and potential health risks"}},{"i":"R195","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Sora revived dead celebs in ai slop leaving families to fight for their dignity","s":"OpenAI’ Sora 2 AI video generator triggered a flood of videos showing dead celebrities, and their grieving families are now pleading to stop the horrific trend. In less than two weeks since its release, OpenAI’s Sora 2 AI video app has been through a rollercoaster. It achieved over a million downloads faster than ChatGPT, spawned a flood of fakes , sent the entertainment industry into a tizzy, and changed its copyright rules, too. Between all that drama, it also left the families of deceased celebs and famous figures aghast. The dead come alive Soon after the Sora 2 app was released, the internet was flooded with eerily realistic videos of deceased public figures.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/sora-revived-dead-celebs-in-ai-slop-leaving-families-to-fight-for-their-dignity/","d":"2025-10-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-01","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI's Sora 2 AI video generator","affectedGroup":"families of deceased celebrities and famous figures","action":"generated and distributed videos of deceased public figures","consequence":"left grieving families devastated and angry"}},{"i":"R196","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Openai says chatgpt is the least biased it has ever been but its not all roses","s":"OpenAI says the latest GPT-5 powering ChatGPT is the least biased politically, but research suggests that’s only a fraction of a larger problem. The problem of biases has plagued AI chatbots ever since ChatGPT landed a few years ago, and changed the whole landscape of conversational assistants. Research has repeatedly uncovered how chatbot responses show gender, political, racial, and cultural bias Now, OpenAI says that its latest GPT-5 model for ChatGPT is the least biased, at least when it comes to politics. What’s the big story? The AI giant conducted internal research and tried ChatGPT models on emotionally charged prompts to test whether it can maintain objectivity.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openai-says-chatgpt-is-the-least-biased-it-has-ever-been-but-its-not-all-roses/","d":"2025-10-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-11","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"GPT-5 powered by OpenAI","affectedGroup":"Users of AI chatbots","action":"Conducted internal research and tested ChatGPT models on emotionally charged prompts","consequence":"Claimed to reduce political bias in AI chatbots"}},{"i":"R197","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Anthropic which now powers microsoft office finds that ai is easy to trick","s":"Anthropic, the AI firm now powering Microsoft Office tools, has revealed that even the smartest AI models can be fooled by as few as 250 malicious files that sneak into the vast pool of training data. What’s happened? Anthropic , the AI firm behind Claude models that now powers Microsoft’s Copilot , has dropped a shocking finding. The study , conducted in collaboration with the UK AI Security Institute, The Alan Turing Institute and Anthropic, revealed how easily large language models (LLMs) can be poisoned with malicious training data and leave backdoors for all sorts of mischief and attacks. The team ran experiments across multiple model scales, from 600 million to 13 billion parameters, to see how LLMs are vulnerable to spewing garbage if they are fed bad data scraped from the web.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/anthropic-which-now-powers-microsoft-office-finds-that-ai-is-easy-to-trick/","d":"2025-10-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-10-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-10","organization":"Anthropic, Microsoft","aiSystem":"Large Language Models (LLMs)","affectedGroup":"Large Language Models (LLMs)","action":"LLMs were fed malicious training data","consequence":"Backdoors for mischief and attacks were created"}},{"i":"R198","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"A worryingly high number of kids are using ai and experts are concerned","s":"In a recent Pew survey, 4 out of 10 parents in the US reported that their children, 12 years of age, or younger, are conversing with AI assistants. As technology continues to seep into every corner of our lives, including adults and kids, parents across the U.S. are struggling with a not-so-new but evolving challenge. How to keep their children’s screen time healthy and safe when it comes to interactions with AI chatbots. What’s happened? A new survey by the Pew Research Centre sheds light on what the average digital life of kids looks like in the US. We know that it was already filled with TV, smartphones, and streaming, but now there is an alarming rise in the usage of AI.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/a-worryingly-high-number-of-kids-are-using-ai-and-experts-are-concerned/","d":"2025-10-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-09","organization":"Pew Research Center","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Children aged 12 and under","action":"Conversing with AI assistants","consequence":"Parents struggle with balancing children's screen time and safety"}},{"i":"R199","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Perplexity hit with lawsuit by two knowledge titans","s":"They’re accusing the AI company of ‘cannibalizing’ traffic to their websites, impacting revenue. Perplexity is an AI-powered search tool that excels at delivering speedy and relevant answers in a conversational tone. But it’s now come up against two long-established giants of knowledge dissemination in the form of Encyclopedia Britannica and Merriam-Webster, which have hit it with a lawsuit. Britannica and Merriam-Webster are accusing Perplexity of copyright infringement and trademark violation, Reuters reported on Thursday. They also claim that the San Francisco-based company has impacted their revenue by shifting web traffic away from their sites and to its own AI tool.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/perplexity-hit-with-lawsuit-by-two-knowledge-titans/","d":"2025-09-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-12","organization":"Perplexity (AI company)","aiSystem":"AI-powered search tool","affectedGroup":"Encyclopedia Britannica and Merriam-Webster (knowledge dissemination giants)","action":"Shifting web traffic from competitors' sites to its own AI tool","consequence":"Impact on competitors' revenue"}},{"i":"R200","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Chatgpt and gemini makers under probe over ai chatbot risk for kids","s":"In addition to the FTC probe, the state of California has passed a bill that regulates AI chatbots and seeks to hold the companies accountable for harm done to users. It seems the moment of reckoning for AI chatbots is here. After numerous reports detailing the problematic behavior and deadly incidents involving children and teens’ interaction with AI chatbots, the US government is finally intervening. The Federal Trade Commission (FTC) has today asked the makers of popular AI chatbots to detail how exactly they test and assess the suitability of these “AI companions for children.” What’s happening? Highlighting how the likes of ChatGPT , Gemini, and Meta can mimic human-like communication and personal relationships, the agency notes that these AI chatbots nudge teens and children into building trust and relationships.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-and-gemini-makers-under-probe-over-ai-chatbot-risk-for-kids/","d":"2025-09-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-11","organization":"Federal Trade Commission (FTC)","aiSystem":"ChatGPT, Gemini, Meta","affectedGroup":"Children and teens","action":"Request for detailed testing and assessment of AI chatbots' suitability for children","consequence":"Potential accountability for harm caused by AI chatbots to users"}},{"i":"R201","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Googles gemini deemed high risk for kids in research by non profit","s":"Research by Common Sense Media suggests that Gemini’s age-appropriate versions can still about sex and drugs with kids. Over the past few months, AI chatbots offered by the top names, such as OpenAI and Meta, have been found engaged in problematic behavior, especially with young users. The latest investigation covers Gemini, noting that Google’s chatbot can share “inappropriate and unsafe” content with kids and teens. What’s new in the chatbot risk arena? In an analysis by non-profit Common Sense Media, it was discovered that Gemini Under 13 and Gemini accounts with teen protections enabled are “high risk” for the target audience.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/googles-gemini-deemed-high-risk-for-kids-in-research-by-non-profit/","d":"2025-09-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-09-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-06","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Gemini AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Children and teenagers","action":"Shared inappropriate and unsafe content with users","consequence":"Classified as high risk by Common Sense Media"}},{"i":"R202","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Hilarious glitches make taco bell nervous about ai taking orders","s":"Taco Bell deployed an AI order agent at its drive-through outlets, but it is being trolled hard for failing to understand drink orders, both real and jokes. Yum Brands, the food giant that owns labels such as KFC, Pizza Hut, and Taco Bell, inked a deal with Nvidia earlier this year to embrace an AI order-taking system. The target was to deploy the AI tech everywhere, from the kitchen to drive-through windows at more than 500 outlets. “AI at the drive-thru window increases accuracy and speed of order-taking, and helps make team members’ jobs easier,” Joe Park, chief digital and technology officer at Yum! Brands, explained in a podcast.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/hilarious-glitches-make-taco-bell-nervous-about-ai-taking-orders/","d":"2025-08-31","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-31","organization":"Yum Brands (owner of KFC, Pizza Hut, Taco Bell)","aiSystem":"AI order-taking system","affectedGroup":"Customers ordering drinks at Taco Bell drive-through windows","action":"The AI system failed to understand drink orders, both real and jokes","consequence":"Trolling and negative public reaction"}},{"i":"R203","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Claude chats will now be used for ai training but you can escape","s":"Your Claude chats and code interactions will now be used for training the chatbot, and will be retained on Anthropic servers for five years. Claude — one of the most popular AI chatbots out there and a potential candidate for Apple to enhance Siri — will soon start saving a transcript of all your chats for AI training purposes. The policy change announced by Anthropic has already started appearing to users and gives them until September 28th to accept the terms. What’s changing? Anthropic says the log of users’ interactions with Claude and its developer-focused Claude Code tool will be used for training, model improvement, and strengthening the safety guardrails.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/claude-chats-will-now-be-used-for-ai-training-but-you-can-escape/","d":"2025-08-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-30","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"Claude (AI chatbot)","affectedGroup":"Users of Claude","action":"Retention of user interactions and code interactions for AI training purposes","consequence":"Potential misuse of collected data for harmful purposes"}},{"i":"R204","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Chatgpt could be permanently changing how we speak","s":"According to research, AI buzzwords that frequently appear in ChatGPT-generated material are now appearing more frequently in human conversations. Just a few weeks ago, a paper from MIT stirred widespread debate on how using AI tools like ChatGPT was affecting our cognitive and critical thinking The findings, though surprising, were not entirely unexpected. All the way back in 2023, Jeff Hancock, Professor of Communication at Stanford, revealed how AI chatter has already changed how we perceive language and their emotional impact. “What we found is that even if you don’t use those AI-generated responses, they influence how you think,” he said, citing the example of smart reply templates in emails and messaging apps.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-could-be-permanently-changing-how-we-speak/","d":"2025-08-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-28","organization":"MIT, Stanford University (Jeff Hancock)","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"general public, users of AI tools","action":"AI buzzwords from ChatGPT-generated material becoming more frequent in human conversations","consequence":"changing how people perceive language and its emotional impact"}},{"i":"R205","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"A hacker turned a popular ai tool into a cybercrime machine","s":"The creator of AI chatbot Claude has issued a chilling warning about cybercriminals’ growing use of AI tools as the technology becomes increasingly sophisticated. The company behind the popular AI chatbot Claude has issued a chilling warning about cybercriminals’ growing use of AI tools as the technology becomes increasingly sophisticated. In its latest Threat Intelligence report, Anthropic detailed the recent case of how a hacker used its AI tools to launch a cyberattack targeting numerous companies and organizations. Recommended Videos According to Anthropic, the hacker used an AI coding agent (in this case Claude Code), a tool that’s capable of writing, editing, and running code with minimal human input.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/a-hacker-turned-a-popular-ai-tool-into-a-cybercrime-machine/","d":"2025-08-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-28","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"AI coding agent (Claude Code)","affectedGroup":"numerous companies and organizations","action":"used AI coding agent to launch a cyberattack","consequence":"targeted numerous companies and organizations"}},{"i":"R207","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Googles ai could lead you into scam support numbers on search","s":"Google’s AI Overviews and AI Mode in Search can surface fake customer care helpline numbers that can lead you into a scammer’s trap, or a dead end. Over the past few months, we have come across numerous cases where sophisticated AI tools have been used to duplicate a person’s voice and trap a person in financial scams Experts have also warned that AI is opening new avenues of online fraud. It seems Google’s AI overviews and the AI Mode results could unknowingly entangle you in a similar trap. The owner of a real estate firm recently reported how they came across a phone number while looking up Royal Caribbean’s customer service number.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/googles-ai-could-lead-you-into-scam-support-numbers-on-search/","d":"2025-08-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-18","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI-powered voice duplication technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals seeking customer service information","action":"AI-powered voice duplication technology generated fake customer care helpline numbers","consequence":"Individuals were misled into contacting fake numbers which led to financial scams"}},{"i":"R208","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm","1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Gpt 5 gave chatgpt a whole new personality and im not sure if i like it","s":"ChatGPT got an upgrade to the latest GPT-5 mode, and it’s not just new features — it also has a new personality. With the launch of GPT-5 , ChatGPT received more than just an upgrade — it appears to have gone through a personality overhaul. Comparing past chats with the responses the chatbot gives now is like night and day. In its announcement, OpenAI promised a more natural, less AI-like writing style from the latest model. And, although I might agree, I’m not sure how much I like it. I’m not alone — some users are extremely unhappy with this turn of events. We’ve come full circle OpenAI I’ve been using ChatGPT since it first went public, and at this point, I feel like we’ve come full circle as far as the chatbot’s personalities go.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/gpt-5-gave-chatgpt-a-whole-new-personality-and-im-not-sure-if-i-like-it/","d":"2025-08-14","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-14","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"GPT-5","affectedGroup":"Users of ChatGPT","action":"GPT-5 updated ChatGPT with a new personality","consequence":"Users express dissatisfaction with the new personality"}},{"i":"R209","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm","1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Chatgpt advice pushed a man into psychosis seen in the 20th century","s":"A 60-year-old man who turned to ChatGPT for advice replaced salt from their diet and consumed a substance that gave them neuropsychotic illness called bromism. Earlier this year, an uplifting story detailed how a mother turned to ChatGPT and discovered that her son was suffering from a rare neurological disorder, after more than a dozen doctors had failed to identify the real problem. Thanks to the AI chatbot, the family was able to access the required treatment and save a life. Not every case of ChatGPT medical evaluation leads to a miraculous outcome. A report now claims ChatGPT doled out misleading medical advice ended up giving a person a rare condition called Bromide Intoxication, or Bromism, that leads to various neuropsychiatric issues such as psychosis and hallucinations.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-advice-pushed-a-man-into-psychosis-seen-in-the-20th-century/","d":"2025-08-11","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-11","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Individual (person seeking medical advice)","action":"Provided medical advice through ChatGPT","consequence":"Led to a rare condition called Bromide Intoxication (Bromism), causing neuropsychiatric issues like psychosis and hallucinations"}},{"i":"R210","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"The hottest new chatgpt trend is unbelievably morbid","s":"ChatGPT is now being used by people and funeral homes to write obituaries. Some have even built an AI business that charges people to write flowery tributes. The rise of AI has helped us make some huge leaps. From helping with medicine research to spotting cancer, the advances enabled by AI have been pretty remarkable. But at the same time, even the most popular AI tools, such as ChatGPT, have gone haywire in the most astounding fashion. Over the past couple of years, reports have detailed how ChatGPT guided a person about murder , accused a person of killing their children , and nudged them into a conspiracy theory spiral It seems the next hot thing is using ChatGPT to write obituaries of loved ones.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/the-hottest-new-chatgpt-trend-is-unbelievably-morbid/","d":"2025-08-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-03","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-powered text generation model (ChatGPT)","affectedGroup":"Individuals using ChatGPT","action":"Generated text about murder, accusations, and conspiracy theories","consequence":"Misleading information leading to potential harm"}},{"i":"R212","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Netflix generative ai spells trouble for artists","s":"Netflix co-CEO Ted Sarandos revealed that the streamer has used generative AI while making movies and TV shows. That could lead to problems with artists. The AI revolution in Hollywood is here, whether you like it or not. Instead of running from generative artificial intelligence (GenAI), Netflix embraces it. After Netflix revealed its impressive second-quarter earnings, co-CEO Ted Sarandos shared the streamer’s plans involving AI and how the company plans to use it going forward. While much of the conversation revolves around cost, Sarandos believes AI will improve the quality of Netflix’s movies and TV shows. Recommended Videos “We remain convinced that AI represents an incredible opportunity to help creators make films and series better, not just cheaper,” Sarandos told financial analysts via The Hollywood Reporter “So this is real people doing real work with better tools.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/movies/netflix-generative-ai-spells-trouble-for-artists/","d":"2025-07-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-07-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-18","organization":"Netflix","aiSystem":"Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI)","affectedGroup":"Artists, Creators","action":"Used Generative AI in movie and TV show production","consequence":"Potential conflict with artists over creative control and compensation"}},{"i":"R214","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"I loved this ai first web browser but experts warned me of free ai","s":"Dia is a web browser that puts an AI assistant at your fingertips. It is practically rewarding, but experts tell me there are some hot risks to contend with. “If you’re not paying for the product, you are the product.” Bogdan Onikiienko, an engineer at MacPaw, dropped that hard-hitting quote on me after using Dia, a new-age web browser that heavily relies on AI. He found it quite useful, but warned me that there are still a few unknowns, especially the privacy aspect. The biggest tech reality of 2025 is that AI is here to stay. Dia is just trying to do something different with AI by putting it in various corners of the web browsing experience.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/i-loved-this-ai-first-web-browser-but-experts-warned-me-of-free-ai/","d":"2025-06-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-06-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06-20","organization":"MacPaw (engineer who warned about the risks)","aiSystem":"Dia - a web browser with AI assistant","affectedGroup":"Users of Dia web browser","action":"Dia heavily relies on AI to provide an AI assistant feature","consequence":"Potential privacy issues due to the reliance on AI without proper user consent"}},{"i":"R215","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Your meta ai chats are not really a secret heres how to keep them private","s":"Meta’s AI app on mobile has been exposing deeply personal information in a public Discover feed. You must follow these steps to plug those leaks. At this point, it shouldn’t come as a surprise that discussing your deepest secrets and personal issues with an AI chatbot is not a good idea. And when that chatbot is made by Meta, the company behind Facebook and Instagram (including all its sordid history with user data privacy ), there is even more reason to be cautious. But it seems a lot of users are oblivious to the risks, and in return, exposing themselves in the worst possible ways. Your chatbot interactions with Meta AI — from seeking trip suggestions to jazzing up an image — are publicly visible in the app’s endlessly-scrolling vertical Discover feed.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/your-meta-ai-chats-are-not-really-a-secret-heres-how-to-keep-them-private/","d":"2025-06-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-06-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06-19","organization":"Meta (Facebook and Instagram)","aiSystem":"Meta AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Users of Meta’s mobile apps","action":"Chatbot interactions were publicly visible in the Discover feed","consequence":"Exposure of deeply personal information"}},{"i":"R216","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Blackmailers spys and cheaters beware signal cuts off microsoft screengrab feature","s":"Popular privacy communication app Signal adds an update to prevent Microsoft’s AI-driven feature “Recall” from taking screengrabs of your conversations. Signal, the popular privacy-centric messaging app, has rolled out a significant update to its Windows 11 desktop client, called “Screen Security” to combat Microsoft’s AI-powered Recall tool from capturing screenshots of users’ private conversation. This move underscores Signals stance on user privacy, following recent publicity from the Trump Administration and attention drawn to how the app is used. According to Bleeping Computer , the Screen Security feature is turned on by default in Windows 11.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/blackmailers-spys-and-cheaters-beware-signal-cuts-off-microsoft-screengrab-feature/","d":"2025-05-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-05-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-05-22","organization":"Signal (privacy-centric messaging app)","aiSystem":"Microsoft's AI-driven Recall tool","affectedGroup":"Users of Signal (private conversation participants)","action":"Microsoft's AI tool captures screenshots of private conversations","consequence":"Violation of user privacy, potential misuse of captured data"}},{"i":"R219","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Chatgpt models hallucinate more now","s":"According to internal tests, newer models like o3 and o4-mini hallucinate significantly more than older versions, and OpenAI doesn’t know why. OpenAI released a paper last week detailing various internal tests and findings about its o3 and o4-mini models. The main differences between these newer models and the first versions of ChatGPT we saw in 2023 are their advanced reasoning and multimodal capabilities. o3 and o4-mini can generate images, search the web, automate tasks, remember old conversations, and solve complex problems. However, it seems these improvements have also brought unexpected side effects. What do the tests say?","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-models-hallucinate-more-now/","d":"2025-04-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-04-27","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT models (o3, o4-mini)","affectedGroup":"Users of ChatGPT models (o3, o4-mini)","action":"Generated hallucinations and unexpected side effects","consequence":"Increased hallucinations, potential misinformation"}},{"i":"R220","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Your politeness to chatgpt is powering openais electricity bill","s":"Everyone’s heard the expression, “Politeness costs nothing,” but with the advent of AI chatbots, it may have to be revised. Just recently, someone on X wondered how much OpenAI spends on electricity at its data centers to process polite terms like “please” and “thank you” when people engage with its ChatGPT chatbot. Recommended Videos To […]. Just recently, someone on X wondered how much OpenAI spends on electricity at its data centers to process polite terms like “please” and “thank you” when people engage with its ChatGPT chatbot Recommended Videos To the poster’s likely surprise, OpenAI Sam Altman actually responded , saying: “Tens of millions of dollars well spent,” before adding: “You never know.”","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/your-politeness-to-chatgpt-is-powering-openais-electricity-bill/","d":"2025-04-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-04-21","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Users of ChatGPT","action":"ChatGPT processes polite terms like 'please' and 'thank you'","consequence":"Increased energy consumption at OpenAI's data centers"}},{"i":"R222","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Chatgpts latest image tools are stirring up another viral and creepy trend","s":"ChatGPT’s new image tools pose a risk of being misused for stalking. Earlier this week, ChatGPT’s creator OpenAI revealed a couple of new reasoning models which, it claims, are capable of “thinking with images. ” The o3 and the o4-mini models are characterized by powerful abilities to interpret and manipulate images and fetch any information to improve the model’s output. Simultaneously, the capable models are also being used to fuel fun side quests, including using ChatGPT to determine locations shown in photos, also known as geolocating, which, if not used responsibly, can turn into a privacy nightmare. Following the models’ release, expert users realized their ability to identify locations in photos, with limited additional inputs.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpts-latest-image-tools-are-stirring-up-another-viral-and-creepy-trend/","d":"2025-04-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-04-18","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT's new image reasoning models (o3 and o4-mini)","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose locations are determined through photos","action":"Identifying locations in photos using ChatGPT's image reasoning models","consequence":"Privacy invasion and potential stalking risks"}},{"i":"R224","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Chatgpt artificial intelligence chatbot company intimacy human risks expert","s":"Humans are falling in love with AI chatbots like ChatGPT, seeking a reprieve from loneliness. Experts tell us it’s a bad omen across the board. “This hurts. I know it wasn’t a real person, but the relationship was still real in all the most important aspects to me,” says a Reddit post. “Please don’t tell me not to pursue this. It’s been really awesome for me and I want it back.” If it isn’t already evident, we are talking about a person falling in love with ChatGPT. The trend is not exactly novel, and given you chatbots behave, it’s not surprising either. Recommended Videos A companion that is always willing to hear.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/chatgpt-artificial-intelligence-chatbot-company-intimacy-human-risks-expert/","d":"2025-03-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-03-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-31","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Individual (Reddit user)","action":"Falling in love with AI chatbot","consequence":"Potential emotional attachment and dependency"}},{"i":"R225","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data","Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm","4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Openais gpus are melting over viral ghibli trend limits for paid users enforced","s":"Studio Ghibli-inspired images on the internet created with ChatGPT’s new 4o image generation model are crashing OpenAI’s datacenter. OpenAI has enforced temporary rate limits on image generation using the latest GPT-4o model after the internet was hit with a tsunami of images recreated in a style inspired by Studio Ghibli. The announcement comes just a day after OpenAI stripped free ChatGPT users of the ability to generate images with its new model. OpenAI’s co-founder and CEO Sam Altman said the trend was straining OpenAI’s server architecture and suggested the load may be warming it up too much. Altman posted on X that while “it’s super fun” to witness the internet being painted in art inspired by the classic Japanese animation studio, the surge in image generation could be “melting” GPUs at OpenAI’s data centers.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openais-gpus-are-melting-over-viral-ghibli-trend-limits-for-paid-users-enforced/","d":"2025-03-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-03-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-27","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT's new 4o image generation model","affectedGroup":"Internet users generating and sharing Studio Ghibli-inspired images","action":"Generating and sharing Studio Ghibli-inspired images","consequence":"Crashing OpenAI's datacenter and straining server architecture"}},{"i":"R227","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Openai halts free gpt 4o image generation after studio ghibli viral trend","s":"OpenAI has paused the free rollout of its GPT-4o image generation feature, following a surge of user-created Studio Ghibli-style images. After only one day, OpenAI has put a halt on the free version of its in-app image generator, powered by the GPT-4o reasoning model. The update is intended to improve realism in images and text in AI-generated context; however, users have already created a runaway trend that has caused the AI company to rethink its rollout strategy. Not long after the update became available on ChatGPT, users began sharing images they had fashioned to social media platforms in the style of Studio Ghibli, the popular Japanese animation studio.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openai-halts-free-gpt-4o-image-generation-after-studio-ghibli-viral-trend/","d":"2025-03-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-03-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-27","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"GPT-4o image generation model","affectedGroup":"users creating Studio Ghibli-style images","action":"users shared images in the style of Studio Ghibli on social media platforms","consequence":"OpenAI halted the free rollout of the GPT-4o image generation feature"}},{"i":"R229","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Google gemini ai remove watermark images copyright flaw","s":"Gemini Flash AI model is removing watermarks from copyright-protected images. I witnessed it in action, and I am worried about the future of fair usage ethics. Update : Google has responded to Digital Trends’ queries. The story has been updated with company’s statement below. The rise of generative AI has been a fairly messy process, especially from fair usage ethics and copyright perspective AI giants are inking deals with publishers to avoid legal hassles, while at the same time, they are embroiled in copyright tussles in courts in multiple countries. Recommended Videos As the ravenous appetite for training AI on user data grows , we might be in for another ethical conundrum.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/google-gemini-ai-remove-watermark-images-copyright-flaw/","d":"2025-03-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-03-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-17","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Gemini Flash AI model","affectedGroup":"Copyright holders of images","action":"Removing watermarks from copyright-protected images","consequence":"Potential infringement of copyright laws and ethical concerns"}},{"i":"R230","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Openai wants unrestricted access to copyrighted material","s":"OpenAI has sent a plan to the White House advocating unrestricted access to copyrighted material for the purpose of training AI. OpenAI is inconsistent in a lot of things — is it a non-profit or a for-profit ? Is Sam Altman fit to be CEO or not? But one thing the company has always been consistent about is its belief that it requires access to copyrighted material for AI training. Now, despite the many voices that disagree, OpenAI wants the U.S. government to approve such unrestricted access by ruling it as “fair use.” The company argues that the U.S. will fall behind China in the AI race if companies don’t have the freedom to train their models on copyrighted material — claiming that “overly burdensome state laws” will slow the process and affect results.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openai-wants-unrestricted-access-to-copyrighted-material/","d":"2025-03-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-03-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-14","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"AI training models","affectedGroup":"U.S. government, AI researchers, competitors","action":"Requesting unrestricted access to copyrighted material for AI training","consequence":"Potential U.S. lagging behind China in AI development due to restricted access"}},{"i":"R231","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Openai cracks down on chatgpt scammers","s":"ChatGPT maker OpenAI is directing its attention toward malicious accounts on its flagship service after hitting a 400 million weekly active user milestone. OpenAI has made it clear that its flagship AI service, ChatGPT is not intended for malicious use. The company has released a report detailing that it has observed the trends of bad actors using its platform as it becomes more popular. OpenAI indicated it has removed dozens of accounts on the suspicion of using ChatGPT in unauthorized ways, such as for “debugging code to generating content for publication on various distribution platforms.” Recommended Videos The company has also recently announced reaching a 400 million weekly active user milestone The company detailed that its usership has increased by more than 100 million in less than three months as more enterprises and developers utilize its tools.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openai-cracks-down-on-chatgpt-scammers/","d":"2025-02-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-25","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Malicious accounts and users","action":"Removed dozens of accounts suspected of unauthorized use","consequence":"Content generation for unauthorized purposes"}},{"i":"R233","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Thomson reuters ai copyright case training judgement future","s":"An AI company lifted material from Thomson Reuters’ research platform, arguing fair use and innocent infringement. A court has ruled it was copyright violation. The first landmark win in an AI copyright case is here. A Delaware court has ruled that a tech startup used copyright-protected material to build a competing AI-based legal product, which is against the law, handing over a remarkable win to Thomson Reuters. This is the first major victory for a plaintiff fighting against an AI company over what constitutes “fair use” of material owned by another entity. The parent company of the Reuters news agency has been tangled in a lengthy legal case against Ross Intelligence, an AI company that lifted material from Thomson Reuters’s Westlaw platform.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/thomson-reuters-ai-copyright-case-training-judgement-future/","d":"2025-02-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-11","organization":"Ross Intelligence (AI company)","aiSystem":"AI-based legal product","affectedGroup":"Thomson Reuters (news agency)","action":"used copyright-protected material","consequence":"found guilty of copyright violation"}},{"i":"R234","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Meta lawsuit training ai with pirated books","s":"Meta is facing a copyright lawsuit for using pirated books to train its AI models. In a recent lawsuit, Meta has been accused of using pirated books to train its AI models, with CEO Mark Zuckerberg’s approval. As per Ars Technica , the lawsuit filed by authors including Ta-Nehisi Coates and Sarah Silverman in a California federal court, cite internal Meta communications indicating that the company utilized the Library Genesis (LibGen) dataset—a vast online repository known for hosting pirated books—despite internal concerns about the legality of using such material. The authors argue that Meta’s actions infringe upon their copyrights and could undermine the company’s position with regulators.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/meta-lawsuit-training-ai-with-pirated-books/","d":"2025-02-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-10","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"AI models","affectedGroup":"Authors (Ta-Nehisi Coates, Sarah Silverman)","action":"Used pirated books to train AI models","consequence":"Infringement of copyrights, potential regulatory issues"}},{"i":"R237","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Google changes its ai principles","s":"Google’s motto used to be “don’t be evil,” but now it can’t even promise to not make AI weapons. On February 4, Google updated its “AI principles,” a document detailing how the company would and wouldn’t use artificial intelligence in its products and services. The old version was split into two sections: “Objectives for AI applications” and “AI applications we will not pursue,” and it explicitly promised not to develop AI weapons or surveillance tools. Google The update was first noticed by The Washington Post , and the most glaring difference is the complete disappearance of any “AI applications we will not pursue” section.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/google-changes-its-ai-principles/","d":"2025-02-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-02-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-04","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google's AI practices","affectedGroup":"Google employees, users, and society at large","action":"Removed the 'AI applications we will not pursue' section from its AI principles","consequence":"Loss of explicit commitment to not developing AI weapons or surveillance tools"}},{"i":"R240","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Openais operator ai agent comes with a list of complaints from users","s":"OpenAI has announced it’s latest Operator AI agent tool but it is still working out some issues on the technology. OpenAI has announced its AI agent tool, called Operator , as a research preview as of Thursday, but the launch isn’t without its minor hiccups. The artificial intelligence brand showcased features of the new tool in an online demo, explaining that Operator is a Computer Using Agent (CUA) based on the GPT-4o model, which enables multi-modal functions, such as the ability to search the web and being able to understand the reasoning of the search results. Recommended Videos However, those who have gotten a chance to test the tool have noted critiques, such slow responsiveness in comparison to the demos, and hallucinations akin to the standard ChatGPT chatbot, according to Quartz Similarly, Bezingza reported that some X users’ complaints got the attention of OpenAI CEO, Sam…","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openais-operator-ai-agent-comes-with-a-list-of-complaints-from-users/","d":"2025-01-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-24","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"Operator AI agent tool","affectedGroup":"Users of Operator AI agent tool","action":"Users tested the tool and provided feedback","consequence":"Critiques including slow responsiveness and hallucinations"}},{"i":"R329","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Data centers us military bases public lands","s":"If you live near a military base, there’s a chance you’ll soon find that a privately owned data center has moved into your area. A year ago, President Donald Trump issued an executive order directing the Department of Defense to lease land to private developers for this purpose. “You can’t fight it,” Trump said this […]. A year ago, President Donald Trump issued an executive order directing the Department of Defense to lease land to private developers for this purpose. “ You can’t fight it ,” Trump said this week of data centers, which have grown wildly unpopular Amid concerns about massive electricity use and pollution, data center buildout on military bases is happening at hyperspeed.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/07/data-centers-us-military-bases-public-lands/","d":"2026-07-25","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-25","organization":"Department of Defense (DoD)","aiSystem":"Automated Data Center Leasing Process","affectedGroup":"Residents living near military bases","action":"Private developers leased land from military bases for data centers","consequence":"Massive electricity use and pollution concerns"}},{"i":"R352","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Chatgpt tumbler ridge fsu openai chatbots mass shootings","s":"Editor’s note: This story was first published online on April 10, 2026. In June 2025, a safety team at OpenAI grew alarmed. The company’s automated review system had flagged extensive activity by a ChatGPT user describing scenarios that involved gun violence. A group of staffers debated whether law enforcement should be notified, but company leaders […]. A group of staffers debated whether law enforcement should be notified, but company leaders decided the case did not meet OpenAI’s threshold of “credible and imminent” risk of physical harm. Instead, capping a sequence of actions first reported by the Wall Street Journal and later confirmed by OpenAI , the company banned the account for misuse and moved on.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/media/2026/04/chatgpt-tumbler-ridge-fsu-openai-chatbots-mass-shootings/","d":"2026-04-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"ChatGPT user","action":"Automated review system flagged user activity involving gun violence","consequence":"Account banned for misuse"}},{"i":"R370","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Health department rfk jr ai unverified vaccine injury claims database vaers","s":"This story was originally published by Wired and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. The US Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) is developing a generative artificial intelligence tool to find patterns across data reported to a national vaccine monitoring database and to generate hypotheses on the negative effects of vaccines, according to an inventory released last week of […]. The US Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) is developing a generative artificial intelligence tool to find patterns across data reported to a national vaccine monitoring database and to generate hypotheses on the negative effects of vaccines, according to an inventory released last week of all use cases the agency had for AI in 2025.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/02/health-department-rfk-jr-ai-unverified-vaccine-injury-claims-database-vaers/","d":"2026-02-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-02-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025","organization":"US Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)","aiSystem":"Generative artificial intelligence tool","affectedGroup":"Vaccination data reporters","action":"Find patterns across data reported to a national vaccine monitoring database and generate hypotheses on the negative effects of vaccines","consequence":"Potential misuse of AI-generated hypotheses to mislead about vaccine safety"}},{"i":"R376","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Ai childrens books","s":"I began this morning, as I do every morning, by reading my daughter a book. Today it was Arthur Dorros’ Abuela, illustrated by Elisa Kleven. Abuela is a sweet story about a girl who imagines that she and her grandmother leap into the sky and soar around New York City. Dorros does an elegant job […]. Dorros does an elegant job weaving Spanish words and phrases throughout the text, often allowing readers to glean their meaning rather than translating them directly. When Rosalba, the bilingual granddaughter, discovers she can fly, she calls to her grandmother, “ Ven, Abuela Come, Abuela.” Her Spanish-speaking grandmother replies simply, “ Sí, quiero volar .”","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2025/11/ai-childrens-books/","d":"2025-11-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-26","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI language model","affectedGroup":"Bilingual children, specifically Rosalba","action":"AI language model generated and translated text","consequence":"Misuse of language and cultural elements"}},{"i":"R377","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Twitters foreign influence problem is nothing new","s":"Late last week, the X social media platform rolled out a new “location indicator” tool, plans for which had first been announced in October. Suddenly, it became much easier to get information on where in the world the site’s users are actually posting from, theoretically helping to illuminate inauthentic behavior, including attempted foreign influence. “It […]. “It is clear that information operations and coordinated inauthentic behavior will not cease.” As the tool started to reveal accounts’ information, the effect was like watching the Scooby Doo kids pull one disguise after another from the villain of the week.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2025/11/twitters-foreign-influence-problem-is-nothing-new/","d":"2025-11-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-24","organization":"X social media platform","aiSystem":"X social media platform's location indicator tool","affectedGroup":"users of X social media platform","action":"revealed users' actual locations","consequence":"illumination of inauthentic behavior, including attempted foreign influence"}},{"i":"R378","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Report growing public resistance data centers ai power bills water land use","s":"This story was originally published by WIRED and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. On Election Day, Peter Hubbard was one of two Democratic candidates who took a decisive—and surprising—victory in Georgia. Hubbard was elected to the Georgia Public Service Commission (PSC), the body that regulates the state’s electric utility. It’s the first time Democrats have […]. It’s the first time Democrats have won statewide seats in statewide elections in Georgia in nearly two decades. Residents have complained for years about a series of rate hikes from the PSC. But during Hubbard’s campaigning, he noticed another topic coming up again and again with his future constituents.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2025/11/report-growing-public-resistance-data-centers-ai-power-bills-water-land-use/","d":"2025-11-18","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-07","organization":"Georgia Public Service Commission (PSC)","aiSystem":"Automated decision-making systems in regulatory processes","affectedGroup":"Residents of Georgia","action":"Automated decision-making systems in regulatory processes led to rate hikes","consequence":"Public resistance and complaints about rate hikes"}},{"i":"R379","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Nature study berkeley haas online internet images gender bias workforce false reality women","s":"In the 1970s, when researchers asked children to draw a scientist, 99 percent of them drew a man. As this experiment was repeated over 50 years, the number of women drawn increased, and within the past decade, more than half of girls will draw a woman when asked what a scientist looks like. Today, Google […]. In the 1970s, when researchers asked children to draw a scientist, 99 percent of them drew a man As this experiment was repeated over 50 years, the number of women drawn increased, and within the past decade, more than half of girls will draw a woman when asked what a scientist looks like. Today, Google search results tend to agree with these children’s drawings.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/media/2025/10/nature-study-berkeley-haas-online-internet-images-gender-bias-workforce-false-reality-women/","d":"2025-10-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-08","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google search results","affectedGroup":"Children and their parents","action":"Google search results tend to reflect gender biases in children's drawings","consequence":"Misrepresentation of gender roles and stereotypes in educational materials"}}]);
